      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>toper's *nose is the pink of imperfection. WARN a man is his own worst enemy, he is minially willing to forgive and forget. THE man who agrees with us is a mighty clear- headed fellow. "Miss WHEELER was arrested." "What for ?" "No bell on her bike." "What did she do?" "Wrung her hands." "Swim men," said Uncle Eben, "kin whistle jes' ernuf ter make dah neighbours mis'ble, an' not ern&amp; ter stop a street-cah. THE Wife: "It must be bedtime." The Hus- band : "Hardly. The baby hasn't waked up yet." AN EARTHLY PARADISE. Mrs. Winks: "So your friend George is married. I hope he is happy." Mr. Winks: "Happy is no name for it. His home is a little paradise on earth. His wife is an accomplished cook." A LITTLE WISE CAUTION.—First Burglar (at back window of big store) ; "I've got the hole big enough now, and we can git in without any more trouble." Second Burglar: "All right, go ahead; but don't make so much noise. Some o' these 'ere watchmen might happen to be awake." SCENES OF TERROR.—He: "My new book is bound to make a hit. It is full of thrilling situa- tions." She: "What is it about ? He: "The adventures of a mouse in a young ladies' -college." A HOME ILLUS'TRATION.—She : "Do you think it is true that marriage delights in contrasts ? " : "Certainly. You know you are continually -contrasting me the man you supposed me to be before we were married." AUNT TABITHA : "You naughty boys! Playing marbles on Sunday! What are you doing it for ? ' Nephew: "A "a'penny a side, aunt." A SAD FATE.—Kindhearted Old Gent: " !..'"our 43tory is, indeed, a sad one. And how did you lose your husband ?" Applicant for Relief: "Please, eor, he wor killed while testin' a live-savin' apparatus." AN ISSUE IN DOUBT.—Stranger: "I was over to 'your court-house to-day at the murder-trial. I don't see why the jury deliberates so long; the prisoner proved a complete alibi." Resident (Boomertown, Oklahoma) " Wal, I wouldn't like 'to gamble on the result. This is the first chance fer a hangin' since the new gaol's bin built; an' local pride's at stake." " Youß—er—father and mother have noticed that I am--er—calling on you quite frequently, haven't they ? " "They couldn't very well help it." "Are they mad?" "Not a bit." " Truly ?" "Certainly. They know it isn't my fault that you come so much." A BLESSING IN DISGUISE.—Mrs. Norris: "Don't complain of the dinner all the time. Just suppose you had lost the use of your palate like poor Cousin Henry ancl'emeaftta:ite a single thing you ate !" Mr. Norris: "1 shoultin't mind that if I always dined at home." UNIQUE IGNORANUE.—" You speak of that boy across the s!zeet as a 'geezer,'" said the elderly customer the country. "What is a geezer?" "A geezer," replied the bootblack, busily plying both his brushes, "is about the same thing as a gazabo." "And what is a gazabo ?" "Say," retorted the boy, straightening up and looking at him with open-eyed astonishment, "you'd better go to a night-school." SISTER: "There, you have the candy all over your new suit. What wil! mamma say?" Little Brother: "Well, mamma won't let me have any fun in these clothes till I get 'em spoiled." AN OPPORTUNITY FOR PRACTICE.—" Jack," said the father, "are you going to enter any of the games at school this year . " "Yes, sir," answered the son: "I'm going to try for the mile race." "All right. Here is a package I wish to deliver to Blinkins. It is just about a mile from here, so let me see how long it will take you." WITH TEARS IN HER EYES. —She was thrown on the world. "Merciful Heaven !" she gasped. Considerable turf was knocked off the world where ~.f3he struck it. Before anybody could reach her she had risen and was swiftly leading her bicycle away. ONE EXPERIENCE SU7FTCIENT.—Mrs. Newed : Darling, _if I should die would you get another wife ? " Newed : "No." Mrs. sewed: "Do you mean it ? " Newed : "I swear it !" Mrs. Newed : "Oh, you darling! Why wouldn't you ? " Newed : "Because I know when I've had -enough." A FRIENDLY TlP.—Smither : "Know any especi- ally goc.xl places to fish, old man ?" Binktums : "Well, no; but I will give you my experience. I have always caught the most fish where there have been signs concpieuously placed which, strange to say, most broadly proclaimed that there should be no fishing there.' HIS DREAM REALISED.—It was the first time that the Chicago bride had ever been separated 'from her husband. Her heart, naturally given to forebodings, beat strong in her breast as the tele- graph boy slowly approached with stolid indiffer- ence. She took the envelope with a trembling hand and was soon secluded within the privacy of her lonely room. "Can it be that he is sick, or eau he be (lead?" she murmured, with a tearful tremor in her choking voice. One mighty effort and the envelope is broken, while through her blinding tears she reads: "Pork gone skywards. We can now move to Gotham. Hubby." WILLING TO Wonx.—He was an unkempt- looking fellow, and he stopped at the suburban residence and asked for employment. The lady of the house was herself superintending the trans- planting of some plants. The door of the green- house was open. "Are you a gardener?" asked the woman. " Aint't had much experience." "Can you plant these bushes?" "I'd hate to risk spoilin' em, ma'am." "Then what can you do ? " "Well, ma'am, if 'you'll give me one o' your husband's cigars," he replied, meditatively, ,ru sit in the greenhouse an' smoke out the insects that's eatin up the leaves o' them rose- bushes." AN AVERTED TRAGEDY. The parlour lamp, which did duty for the same young man seven nights in the week, had struck and was slowly gomg out. The red glow grew fainter and . fainter, and as the shadows merged one by one into the gloom, an eerie feeling crept into the young maid's soul. "George," she asked, in tones treni; ulous through suppressed agitation, "what would you do, if you were strapped hand and foot in a .chair, while a candle, stuck in the top of a keg of gunpowder, a foot from your head, burned slowly down? And George, who had been gazing expectantly at the lamp, answered: "I would )3lew it out." She shivered.</p>
