      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>"CAPITAL" 011 AI.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" 011 AI.</p>
        <p>Nothing since the Jameson raid has hap- pened which has set conversation flowing in Radi- cal quarters so volubly as Sir H. Campbell-Banner- man 8 notice of motion about the ex-Commander- in-Chief's pension. The parliamentary Radicals are certainly jubilant, though they are well enouiti as- sured that their utmost hope in the division lobby is to diminish the Ministerial majority by a dozen or two. In Radical quarters, both outside and in- side Parliament, there is an unbroken unanimity of hostility to the pension. Some of the Moderate men immediately behind the Front Opposition tench,would, I imagine, had preferred that the question had been left untouched, and there may thus be some Liberal abstentions when the vote is is taken. The duke's present income from the coun- try is about £20,000 per annum, an amount with i which both he and the Government might very well have been satisfied. The proposed extra pen- sion.of MAK in respect of his late position as Commander-hief, is accordingly a scandalously superfluous. gift. The office he has vacated carries no pension; but in addition to the proposed grave departure from rul&amp; the duke was allowed to hold office for many years beyond the age at which all other ofl3cers in the service are compelled to retire. Still his disappearance from the head of the army is cheap at £l,BOO a year, though that does not lessen the scandal of the job.</p>
        <p>The attention of philanthropists is being earnestly drawn to the circumstance that Dr. Henri Dunant, who, jointly with his friend and fellow- citizen Moynier, chairman of the society of Public Utility at Geneva, founded , the Red Cross Society, should be at present living almost universally for- gotten in the hospice of Heiden, in the Swiss can- ton of Appenzell. Some not altogether unnatural indignation is estcpressed that, whilst the successful warrior and even the thwarted filibuster are PlC- tolled and glorified. Dunant's fortunes should offer so flagrant an illustration of the world's ignorance of its purest heroes, who give all for its benefit, and after spending all be had in the cause of suffering humanity he is now a pensioner on public charity. It was the sickening horror and disgust with which he contemplated the terrible scenes that at- tended or immediately followed the battle of Solferino, June 24, 1859, that first moved him to make an appeal to the nations to take common action for the protection of the sick and wounded in war. Baffled for a long time by opposition or in- difference, Dr. Dunant's indefatigable efforts were at length rewarded by the conclusion, August 1864. of the Geneva Convention, and the institution of the Red Cross Society. It is not doubted that statues will he erected to Dunant, after his death ; but can nothing be done, ask his friends, to impart a little more comfort into what still remains to him of life?</p>
        <p>General Booth arrived in London on Monday from his tour round the world, and went direet to his home at Hadley Wood. lie came from India to Brindisi by the liner Carthage, and then travelled overland. The General is in good health, and well satisfied with the success of his tour.</p>
        <p>It. appears that Mr. Ballington Booth's new or- ganisation, the Christian Crusaders, is to strike out a new line of work—to wit, the conversion of the American "middle classes." We thought they did not have classes in the free country across the At- lantic, and it is surmised that what is meant is the wage-earning classes. No doubt there is 'plenty of room for work in this direction. But what about the welfare of such upper-class persons as Mr. Depew and the others who expreFead their wish to vet their ascension robes mailed vitt Lexington and Bunker's ? Are they to be neglected by the Christian Crusaders?</p>
        <p>As a result of woman suffrage in New Zealand, it is stated that, since the recent. elections, steps have been taken to improve the condition of prisons,and to promote the welfare of children supported in their early years by the State. The men with whom the ladies have co-operated acknnwledge that their labours have been of great service. Several indus- tries in New Zealand are almost, entirely in the hands of women, among, them being market-garden- ing, bee-farming, and flower-growing. The medical profession number among its members many women doctors with good practices, and at a recent Melbourne University exatnination women stood at the head of several of the class lists. No symptoms of " mannishness " have as yet, it is thankfully said, broken out in New Zealand.</p>
        <p>It Must not be supposed that the renreseutatiyes of the liquor trade are united in acceptance of the prop-vial for an inquiry by Royal Commission into the licensing system. On the contrary, the trade both in Lancashire and iii Ireland is strongly op- pneed to the holding of any such inquiry, on the around that it is unnecessary and likely,to intro- duce an element of disturbance and uncertainty into the conduct of a lawful business. At the recent conference on the subject in London the Lancashire representatives entered an emphatic protest against the proposed Conimission.</p>
        <p>A further meeting of the Liberal members outside the front opposition, bench was held at the house of Commons on Tuesday at the suggestion of the Radical end Labour Committee to consider the (meg- !ion of the organization of the party. Mr. T. Ellis occupied the chair; and after proceedings lasting two hours, in the course of which Sir C. Nike, Mr. I Labouchere, Mr. P. Stanhope, and other radicals spoke in condemnation of the present system, it Wsl,l agreed to pass the following resolution "That the Radical and Labour Committee be in- structed to summon a Meeting of the Radical and Labour members after the meeting of the Nstional Liberal Fiaderstion at Huddersfield to consider the organisation of the party " In the meantime the advocates of reform within the party will, of course be.at liberty to *convert the Federation to their views.</p>
        <p>The demand for Chippendale furniture is a keen as ever it was, a. was demon.trated on Tuesday st, Christie's when the late Sir Rdward J Dean Paul's extraordinary collection of this particular branch of deccrrative objects came under the hemmer. Here are a few of the high prices trahogany 34in. wirle,loo guineas; a pair of mahogany lamp stands, 50 guineas; an upright cab;net, %ft. high by Bift. wide. 166 guineas: a set of six chairs, 82 guineas: a set of four large ditto. 112 guinea.; a set, of six hall ditto. 105 guineas a set of three fsnteuils,so guineas; and another set of eight ditto, 121 guineas; a pair of arm chair., 44 gnincas ; and a large ditto,with open lyre-shaped back,3o guineas. This lextramdinsry sale has realised a total of over £20,000 in Jive days. The choicest portion of Sir Edward J. Dean Paul's collection, the old English silver, jewels, snuffboxes, bronzes, pictures, and so forth,will not be sold until the last week in June.</p>
        <p>The eppenrenee of Dr. .Tetneenn and his fellow- prisoners at Bow-street on Tuesday did not create so much excitement among the sight-seeing public as everybody expected. The famous police mutt is a small apartment. and between the 15 prisoners, the large number of counsel. anti the host of repor- ters. the most of its space was taken up. and very little accommodation was left for the miscelltinetans public. A number of titled people were provided with seats on the bench. but both inside end out- ' side the court the general /while was very decor- ous. While those inside were for the most part quiet • and self-restrsined, those outside did not make herneq of the little bend of prisoners. The doctor evidently felt his position more keenly than any of Hs eollengues, who seemed to view the situation with a light end eAsv spirit. As the pro- edir ;ch are merely preliminary, have been ceo adjourned fnr a week. and as further adjournments may take place, it will he some time hef-ret this initial stage is over. The legal fight, will he keen enough even at this stage, and if it results in favour of a committal, the subsequent proceedings will he protracted and tedious before they ore over. But they will be conrinunns. end free frem the, dreary ordeal of a weekly adjournment,end tbe patliemen- tent session will probably be at an end before the last of the case is heard of.</p>
        <p>The Bangor magistrates had before them on Tuesday a man named David James Morgan. on the chary' of making a false declaration of residence under the Mar- riage Acts, by which he had prncured a Ala Friars bet- ween himself and a girl named Sadler. The prisoner, whn. aecording to the case for the prosecution, has cruelly deceived and ruined many women. nd has hAen in prison for " long firm " frauds was remanded fcr a week.</p>
        <p>The *"toy al La undresses-- say Cri! el sfey's STARCIT (3 Tfy,t3 is nnem.alled for imparting a beantiful finish to starci-,ed in, n &amp;c., either cold or boilinfr water starch. Mr.—. Corn- wall, says -" My wife will rot finish our linen without your •• Starch Gloss.'" From Mrs —. Bradford :—Dear sir,- 3 am pleased to inform you tlit I imve tried many so-called Starch Glazes end Polishes. hut find none equal to yours." It shcrrd be used in every house. Sold at id. , 3d., and 6d., by most starch sellers. If not in stock will procure it. T. CRITCHLEY, Blackburn. Proprietor.—Adrt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY MARCH 14. 1896</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY MARCH 14. 1896</p>
        <p>Britain and Empress of India, she can sever inherit her 1 father's honours. The Duke of Fife's new house on Deeside is to be built of pink granite, and the roof cov- ered with red tiles. It will be a low building with gables and turrets, and the effect will be very picturesque. There will be several large sitting-rooms on the ground floor, with suites of apartments for the Prince and Prin- cess of Wales on the upper floor. The kitchens and offices are to be entirely separated from the main buildii.g, and extensive new stables are to be erected. The cost of the building is estimated at £20,000, and the whole house will be ligbted by electricity. Mr. Reuben Bassoon, with whom the Prince of Wales stuyed during his recent visit to Brighton, comes of a family which has a singularly interesting history. It is said the Sassoons are descended from one of tne Jewish families carried into captivity to Babylon, and as they have lived from time immemorial in Bagdad, before emigrating to India, such a tradition is not altogether so improbable as it might seem to be at first sight. This year being leap year, the birthday of Rossini, the famous composer, can be celebrated, as he was born on the 29th F.bruary, 104 years back. In Pesara' the town in which he was norn, a musical festival took place on the 29th ult., conducted by Mascagni. Rossini's father was an inspector of slaughter-houses in Pesaro,and with this occupation be combined thepost of town trumpeter, this instrument being the first that young Rossini tried to play. From the age of seven he studied music and singing under various masters, and his unusual tal- ents were discovered eaaly by his relatives. Be died at his villa at Passey, near Paris, in November, 1868. Almost every person one meets has, at the present time some astounding tale to tell of the advanced state of the spring, Now it is a pushing, and aspiring fruit blossom, and then it is a cuckoo, while even those of us who sojourn in the great metropolis where vegetation is in a more or leas suppressed condition, are one and all eager with our little experiences, which we give in due hope and expectation of seeing our listener's eyebrows creep up into heir hair in sympathetic astonishment. As I have remarked before, however, this very previous spring, is, received modistically, trying beyond all mere ordinary terms of expression,and what to wear ie a pro- blem difficult indeed to solve. Many of the new spring stiffs are unusually beautiful this year. Tne printed velvets and satins have Oriental patterns, but these designs are small, and somewhat different to those of last season. Grenadines are much en evidence and,for skirts, will not be gored but gathered at the waist, over silk foundations. This is good news for those of us who happen to possess middle aged satin or silk skirts, which alas, uncovered, no longer bear the light of day,or even lamp light upon them. One parti- cular grenadine I espied at a fashionable "At Home" one evening last week was entirely covered with chrysanthemum blossoms, in all tones, set close together, and 1 very lovely was the effect. White satin has long reigned supreme but now its sway is to be shared by moire. Many of the shot moires are truly exquisite. the effect of light OD the glancing colours and the liquid look imparted to the sur- face by the markings, rendering them fascinating be- yond all power of description. Speaking of moire reminds me that the fabric in black is just now adorning many of the newest Parisian gowns, and moire ribbon is once again to be seen decor- ating the newest hats. One I saw walking down Bond- street the other afternoon was composed of straw, int‘r- plated with black moire, the high bows being of similar ribbon in a wider width. Bunches of violets, steel buckles, and black feathers, completed the novel head- gear. By the way,l must not omit to mention the fact that the mushroom shape of het is to receive con- siderable attention. The necesstty for long gloves with short-sleeved gowns will be an expensive item again this season. Sleeves are really much too costly, and those of us who are economists, either from choice or from necessity, cannot but fervently hope that sleeves will, ere long,be permitted to come to the wrist. In Belgium,which may be regarded as the country in which the finest gloves are made, the King distinguishes himself by never wearing any,not even when in full uniforgeor during the coldc,t weather. Strict on the subject of etiquette he,however, ' inaists that his gentlemen-in-waiting and his aides-de- camp shall be irreproachably gloved. In England,on the other hand, the Prince of Wales is invariably gloved,and extremely well gloved, too. The Duchess of York's favorite gloves for outdoor wear are of pearl grey sue le with small dark grey buttons, usually four in number. Her hands are by no means small, but are a beautiful shape, and the skin, of course, is deliciously white and satin-like in texture. All Her Royal Highness's gloves are especially made for her by a well-known glove firm, and she is exceedingly particular about cut and quality, although she rarely wears the same pair more than twice. Weeems according to one of our London journals.that many of our most cherished traditions are gc- ing down, one by one. I glean from one particular paragraph in this paper that among other things we do not know how, or when to wind up our watches ! Ac- cording to the expert who writes the paragraph,a watch should always be wound every morning, so that the spring may he at its strongest tension during the day, when the watch is .jolted more or less. At night the comparatively weak spring has nothing to disturb it. "Women," the expert went on to say, " have not the very faintest idea how to treat their watches properly." Apropos, in London, on an average, one person in every three cerries a watch. Of those two-thirds pay from 255. to £3 for them, while the prices given by the remaining third range from the latter sum to £6o.When speaking with the manager of a large West End jewellery shop the other day, he told me that the smallest wateh they had ever made was the size of a three-penny piece, and yet was a complete half-hunter, keyless, and as ac- curate as a large one. Some really awful disclosures have recently been made respecting the wages of many of the poor seamstresses in the East of London. It appears that trousers are finished off ny hand for 3d. per pair, and it is hard tc finish a pair in three hours. Flannel shirts are paid for at the rate of la. 63. a dozen, and coat buttonholes ten a penny. Twenty-tour buttons must be sewn on before a penny can be earned, and cotton, etc., has actually to be found by the poor workers. It would be an event to be grateful for, if the practice of lark eating would go out of fashion—tor once let it became tad form to eat this excinisite sone,ster, and the wholesale massacre of larks all ever the country, amounting to about 40,000 a year would cease. After one has heard the lively song of the bird as he goes higher and higher towards the sky, or listened to the melodious trills fallinN earthwards from an invisible songster high up in the asure, it is impossible to behold unmoved the strings of dead larks hanging up in the poulterers' window. There is, however, very little doubt that if something is not done, and done quickly,to stop the slaughter which goes on all the year round, our sweetest snegster will become extinct. Stewed R;ce with Tripe forms a nice dish with those who like tripe. First cut the tripe into pieces, two inches long and one each broad, and throw it into a pan of cold water, and just let it boil lip, then strain away the water. Cut up two onions finely and fry in a stew pan with two ounces of dripping until soft. Plane the *ens of tripe in a saucepan and add one pint of milker bri th. Have one and a half ounces of the best rice washed, add it to the tripe and season all with pepper and salt. Simmer for an boor. This must not boil or it will cur- dle and he apoilt.—" Madge."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAREST NELLIE, Already the very earliest spring millinery has made its appearance, and very fresh, floral, and charming it looks. The toque is reasserting itself triumphantly, in the shape of a rather flat, wide, and quaintly crumpled shape, with a high brim of flowers. The smartest toques are made of sheeny satin straw, which, though dyed in many colours, is most stylish and effective in leaf-green, violet— the real tint of the flowers, both in dark and light shades, and black. White aigrettes appear on all the nicest hats, some of them being nine or 10 inches high, and very chic and (lashing they look. 'There must be no hesitation about the aigrette; the wearer who hesitates is lost; she must have a good one of daring height, or dispense with the ornament altogether; there must be no half</p>
        <p>In spite of all temptations wear the hats of other nations, the bicyclists prefer those of the Englishman. Wise bicyclists I Simplicity should mark for its ow ti the modes of the athlete, the plain felt hat, pre•eminently suited to her who wheels, or indulges in the game of golf. It is wilful waste if we pay much for simple millinery. This hat is but the example which expresses the rule. It is of drab with a rosette of ribbon placed at the base of a group of pheasant's quills, or it may be of brown, lined with black felt and trimmed with black ribbon and speckled feathers. A happy medium between the summer and winter hat is struck by a "Monte Carle in light felt, trimmed at one side with a cluster of violet leaves with a few blossoms in their midst; another most successful hat is of white felt with a rather broad brim turned up all round, and this is trimmed with a xosette of ribbon and a bunch consisting, perhaps, of eleven thin black quills. Besides being adapted to the bicyclist, these hats are ideal for travelling. A very fashionable cape is made of golden beaver serge cloth, lined with tartan plaid silk, and tied with tartan plaid ribbon. The cape is cut in one and fitted smoothly to the neck, the lower edge and ouch side of front being turned up and machine stitched twice, then lined with silk, which should be neatly slin4itehed, round the lower edge and up each side of front, where it fastens across with poir.ted tabs and fancy buttons. The hoed is lined ith silk, then arranged in a pleat each end and gathered in the centre of back and secured to the neck of cape, and tied in front with ribbon. A stylish mantlette, can be made of Velourdu nord, trimmed with rich fur and jewelled jet, or it may be made Witikuut, the jet and trimmed .with Thibet I fur.</p>
        <p>I told you something about the newest styles of day dresses last weel;,-and now I the, the descrip- tion of 3.11eW design tint.,, will he quite.safe for wear, right through the season, provided the sleeves are made considerably smaller than was the fashion of last season. A prim and really charming gown for a slender figure, it looks well in any soft woollen fabric, or in alpaca. , The full, graceful front is drawn into a point at the waist, but yet the bodice itself is not pointed, but is cut with a round waist, and finished with very smart tiny basques of velveteen, shaped to lie close to the figure, and having a diminutive button on each side and on the point of the hip. The back is fashioned into fall short basques of the cloth, and the square shoulder-pieces, which end at the seam, and velvet collar and cuffs give a very pretty finish. The skirt is four and a-half yards round the bottom, gored off to fit the figure neatly at front and sides, and set in two large box-pleats, or in large gathers at the back. For a house gown the jacket bodice is hooked at the left side over the full vest, and the velveteen trimmings are extremely effective. Altogether, this would be a very inexpensive dress, made in, say, leaf-green cloth, with darker velveteen and pale pink (Thule vest, or in rosewood-brown, with old-rose vest. I know I ought to suggest green, with petunia velvet, for that combination is rife in the West-End. just now, but on a gown of this description, iti3ant for regular wear, the effect is hopelessly tawdry.</p>
        <p>kvery pretty costume is made of rifle-green cloth, trimmed with a passemcnterie of black braid interspersed with gold thread. Your loving JENN.</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CFIAT. It appeers the Empress Of Russia has forbidden all her ladies-in-waiting to smoke chorettes. This order of the Czarina, has created considerable consternation in the Court circles, as Russian women are inveterate smokers. The Dowager CzArina is said to have favoured the habit herself, but the young Empress who doubting inherits her abhorrence of tobacco from her motber,the lamented Princess Alice, has a perfect detestation of seeing a cigar 7 ette in a woman's mouth, and in fact also objects to the habit of smoking, in men. At the present time there are a great many titles which stand in reasonable fear of total extinction. The Duke of Fife's heads the list,and it is a very curious fact that although his little daughter might possibly be Queen of</p>
