      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>TIMELY RESCUE.</head>
        <p>A TIMELY RESCUE.</p>
        <p>A little girl fourteen years old, living in Belfast, had a narrow escape recently, and the story told by her father is especially interesting because it applies to so</p>
        <p>- many persons. Almost any child is liable to get into the same difficulty, and it is always well to know a way out of it. The father has fortunately written a letter ler publication, which is here introduced "Some time ago, our little girl, Mary, age 14 years, began to lose her appetite and cid not care for food of any kind. In March last she got so weak that she could not go to school, and in a few days was unable to rise from bed. She had a slight dry cough, with some !ever, and at first we thought her stomach was out of order. We consequently treated her for stomach trouble, but I became anxious about her lungs. After a careful examination, the doctor said that there was nothing the matter with the lungs, but thought that it was possible the trouble might ev el op there. Three weeks passed, and she still c)t worse. She became very much emaciated and hint and at tles time another examination by two doctors revealed the tact that tubercles were forming on the left lung, and tending to spread. On hearing such Bad news, we, the parents, consulted as to what we had best do to try and at least prolong her life. Considering that the trouble had only just got hold on the lung, I th ii:llagl:t it was surely possible to arrest the disease. n Scott's Emulsion, which we had heard h.i;ll-4' sPoken of, we got a bottle at once, and began to give it ld mond cou her. e watched her carefully and attended her night "d —ay. For the first week and part of th l e ra not see that there was any perceptible prevetnent , but after that we began to notice the Cough getting less, she was not so fretful, and the third Week of takine Scott's Emulsion there was no doubt Whatever that she was improving. The cough wis Nnearly gone, and she began to rest better at night. We .`"lu no doubt that it was Scott's Emulsion which was Riving her such benefit, and consequently we centinued Vereistently with the treatment. "Our physician: recommended Scott's Emulsion intro4lY, and he was very much pleased to see the tieP:hvernetet in our little girl's condition. An examine- tun-. ,41 bSL, the doctor in July showed that the C0u',...." had been affected was healing up. Of of iee recovery was slow at the outset, but by the first takast uloleth, September, our daughter was able to that e h "ercise out of doors, and I am now happy to state er recovery is permanent,as she is improving very ‘aPidlee e. There can be no doubt under God's blessing, ,V 8 Emulsion has been the means of bringing her 7Cir to health. Everyone who knows the case, says it 1: a wonderful recovery, seeing she was so weak and -481Pless When the medicine was first given her. The result is. that Mrs. Love and I are telling our neigh boUrs an d others about the value of Scott's Emulsion, and different persons whom we know have begun taking it• I can safely recommend it to those who are at all Weak in the chest, or young persons with any tendency to Consumption.—l am, yours respectfully (signed), ItENRY LOVE, 1 London Road, Belfast, October 14th, 1895." Rave you a child that has little appetite, is pale and thin, and apparently in poor health ? If you have, you tan Perhaps save it from some wasting disease by giv- ing Scott's Ernusion. This remedy gives rich blood, ew flesh and strength to the body when all other pre- iparations fail to do any good. Mr. Love's letter reveals a typical case where consumption was averted. He attributes his child's life to Scott's Emulsion. (12)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PROM ORIENT TO OCCIDENT.</head>
        <p>PROM ORIENT TO OCCIDENT.</p>
        <p>THE SATA.NESE INVASION.</p>
        <p>(By Mu. H. Wiuntim.) Ambassador Bayard, in the now famous speech delivered before a Scottish audience, gives the fol- lowing quotation from the speech of a famous Englishman :—" Whosoever commands the seas Commands the trade; whosoever commands the trade of the world commands the riches of the 'World, and consequently the world itself." Another Illiotationfrom the Ambassador's speech is the fol- rWing ; together, they will serve as a text for a el,.._ort series of articles I offer to readers of the axaminer "on what I call the Japanese Indus- :till Invasion of the Western world.' Never since ulue, world was peopled has mankind stood in such aftious expectancy, awaiting the outcome of the l_ttiraediate future as in these closing years of the 19th ceritury." For the following matter lam indebted to re- torts et speeches, newspaper articles, and such 'writings, on Japan as the libraries afford me, I tihtinerkesittlikely that there may known be a few who will be n ed in reading of the doings of a country so aittle wn as Japan, the home of the little brown Zaan.</p>
        <p>War seems to have its advantages, well as its disadvantages, yet there are many among us who as WIII neither see them, nor allow them. It is well, perhaps, that it is SO; that we refuse to see bow of blood the good can come out of evil, or, that by the shedding be advanced. Prsvious to the American civil war material prosperity of the world can very little wire was drawn there. During the war the export of wire to the States from England tame to a standstill. It never resumed its old pro- Portions. When the Americans could not import, they commenced manufacturing. To-day they are 2aoving heaven and earth in their endeavours to build up an export trade, their production is so enormous in quantity, Previous to the Chinese-Japanese war, we knew little of the latter country, and not much more of the former. A few idle tourists bent on a little globe-trotting would bring us marvellous tales bOme. We should hear of a country where every- thing was stunted in growth; where they culti- Vated a flora that had all the appearance of age, Maturity, and health, and yet was stuntei in rgowth, and lilliputian in appearance. The travel- rs would tell us of delightful looking gardens, "With lakes crossed by bridges and enclosing the aged-looking oak, and numerous other trees, and /et the whole business could be put in the corner of an average back yard. From where I now write I can, at the cost of 20., board the car and go out to see all these wonders. Yes, the Jap is here, and according to all appearance he has come to stay, Pour years ago the Japs were not numerous in the atreets of San Francisco, now, scarce a block but This numerous Japanese stores, in which can be bought the useful and the useless, the beautiful and the Most wondrously ugly that the mind can con- Ceive. The collection of Eastern curios has a feast before him when he enters a Japanese store. __This is what we have seen hitherto; now the neue has changed. With the close, the successful laese of their war with China has come the scme- %at grudging acknowledgement by the Western Vii?Wers of Japan as a nation to be reckoned with. e:raust think no longer of the little brown man Ike one who struts about girded with a couple of ItWords as long as himself, but as one who has en- tered into the competitive struggle, and having all, ar nearly all, on his side that goes towards Making success. The Chinese have been 11 thorn in the flesh of the white 'Workman. Their boundless conceit and stulti- fying conservatism have kept them as a aationin the rear. They know everything, there- fore no one can learn them anything. With the aap it is different. He is willing to learn at the bands of the merest child. He does not stop at Mere imitation. He seeks to improve whatever he Iputs his hand to, and he is not remarkable fora lack Of confidence, nor yet for too much pride. At a bouse where I wee lodging a year ago, I was sur- prised one day to see two well dressed and well atarched Japs go np into the yard, &amp;trip, and at once Bet in to chop a heap of wood, put it into sacks in a neat fashion, draw their money and "quit." I saw the whole thing, and here I was looking for work.. Well I did a big think, and the Japs did the work. In a recent isFese of the " Examiner" appeared the following significant parsgraph—" The Manchester peoplA are complaining that Japan is now manu- facturing cotton goods, it is remarkable that other ijnglish firms sell cotton-spinning machinery to the 'Japanese and provide them with experts to instruct thera in the processes of manufacture. Hitherto</p>
        <p>- a MINUTE WITH YOU PLEASE Sufferers from Gr rel. Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Back Mr°l)63'. Wind and Water Complaint, Diseases of Kidneys "Redder Stone, Sciatica, Rheumatism and Gout will find a Positive rein Ifoldropre Gravel Pills. Try a small box and II not satisfied your money will he returned. Prices is. ;01ttf all Chemists• Or post. free for 12 stamps. from lioLuttorD's weadrical Checkheaten. Yorks. Sold at 35, Bridge-st., _ etrington. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them w toithe Proprietor. and a box will be tient next poe.- B.dvt4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1896.</p>
        <p>Japan has had to buy her ships abroad. mostly in England, but the " Hochishimbun" discloses on ar- rangement whereby, au no very distant. date. Japan will not only do her own shipbuildirg,hut probably compete with England. The paper states that ar- rangements have been come to for the establishment of a steel foundry by the Armstrong Company. of 'England, on the following terms :-1. The material shall for the present be imported from England. 2. Of the workmen to be employed 20 per cent shall be brought from England and 80 per cent shall be !Japanese. 3. When a new arm is inverited in Eng-1 ' land it shall be manufactured at the works in Japan. 4. For a stated number of years the Japin- ese Government shall give a fixed subsidy to the Company. 5, On the expiration of the period dur- ing which the Company receives a subsidy the. works shall be sold to the Japanese Government." I give this rathedengthy extract as a justification for writing on this subject. and to guard against anyone thinking I am suffering from a bilious at- tack, and conSequetely, in a pessimistic frame of mind. Several meetings have been held in this city of the Manufacturers' and Producers' Association, and the Chamber of Commerce, at which committees have been appointed to draw up reports, and to in- vestigate the alleged invaslon of the Japanese in the commercial and industrial activities of the United States. The result of such reports tends to show " Ihe situation to be doubly grave in view of the quiet working of the Japanese, who never take the public into their confidence, but when the oppor- tunity is presented, act without, hesitation, being fully prepared to meet every emer2ency. Extracts were read from German and English papen, show- ing that, the United States were not alone in en- •ertaining fears of the encroachments of the</p>
        <p>Japanese." In a book just published, "The Key of • the Pacific," the author, Mr. A. Colquhoun, specking of Japan says, " Japan alone has forty millions of people, alert, progressive, and eager to excel alike in peace and war. It, is only seven years since the Japanese began to manufacture cotton, and now they have one million spindles a;; work. Profound changes are taking place in the far East, and what we are confronted with is nothing short of an indestrial revolution, which, if we are not careful, will he worked out on lines not to our advantage." In the London "Telegraph" see a report of a meeting held in London at which were Lord Rose- bery, the Japanese minister, and several other not- ables. and an editorial reviews the position by cay- ing, " Japan is evidently destined tobe, in the future the great military and commercial State of the Western Pacific. She will therefore be a valuable ally and a wealthy customer, and Lord Rosebery's straightforward policy will soon be as generally praised in England as it is in Japan." Read in the light of the quotation at the head of this article,and keeping in view the one from Mr. Colquhoun's book, the writer of the " Telegraph" editorial is evidently somewhat misleading, especially so if the following is true. Mr. Allen Olney writing to the" Standard" says, " In England we find a steady cleteroiration of the English workman in every industry, arising from the gradual extinction of apprenticeships " When we consider that England only ceased to be a purely pastoral country toward the close of the sixteenth century, and that she has since then dis- tanced other countries that had attained commer- cial greatness before the beginning of the eleventh century, we need not be surprised at the progress Japan has made during seven years, having at her call the acumulated experience gained by the Wes- tern nations during the nineteenth century. "In life's lengthened alphabet what used to be to our sires X Y Z is to us A B C." A writer in the" Fortnightly Review," of Sept., 1892, says "Japan can begin the race at the point , where she finds us." She need not trouble about candles, lamps, or gas, she can go straight to the Ilatest. Edisonian improvements while we are adapt- ing ourselves to them. I find in conversing with a dealer in old books here, that the Japs are among his best customers. While being nimble at driving bargain they never allow the cost of a book to Ilstand in the way of getting hold of something good. I must guard against my readers fancying that Japan has only just commenced her industrial career, and that she was a thousand years behind Western nations. Japanese feudalism fell in 1868, up to that data her artists had achieved a high degree of technical skill, and whenever one of her artist artisans developed any special ability, he could count on the munificent patronage of his feudal lord. It is a mistake to look upon these people as barbarians. They have a civilization that differs from ours only, and it is unwise to under- rate it. In 1874 the circulation of newspapers was 8,470,269 copies, in 1877, 33,449.529 conies. The America Consul General reportsi that in 1882 twelve papers in Tokio circulated 20,000,000 annually. So much for mental development, all of which it was necessary to say in order to completely understand what, perhaps, we are more interested in. her mat- erial developments. The Japanese have been char- acterized ns "The Yankees of the Pacific,' I suppose this was beCallA49 the real Yank thought them so " awfully cute.' A survey of the tables of import reveals ttse curious fact that raw products are taking the place of finished articles and that Japan is rapidly becoming self-sufficing so far as manufac- tured articles are concerned. I cannot get hold of England's tables of export and import with Japan, but during the fiscal year of 1895 Japan shipped to tho United States manufactured articles to the value of 5.414,2231015., during the same period America's total exports to Japan amounted to 5,573,429,1015., of which 4,048,0771015. consisted of raw cotton, kerosene and provisions. Scarcely 800,000doln. of the remainder represented manufactured articles. What, becomes of the" wealthy customer" of which the " Standard" speaks? Previous to the war, Japan was an importer ot finished goods ; she is so no longer, or not to any great, extent. From being a considerable importer of cotton textiles, she is now an exporter on quite a large scale of finished cotton goods, and will certainly take immediate possession of the vast Asiatic market previous to sending her laden ships down your own ship canal to Manchester. The head of the frieght department for the Cora- panY under which I am now employed said recently —"lt is true the shipments of cotton in bales or raw cotton from Texas and New Orleans to Japan are on the increase, and it is true that we have asked the various steam ship Companies to give increased facilities for forwarding the cotton the railroad is turning over to them here. These ship- ments, of course, indicate that Japan is increasing ths number of her spindles and is greatly aug- menting her output of manufactured cotton goods." In other words, the Japanese are evidently bent upon first supplying their home market with cotton fabrics instead of buying them abroad, and will later on become a manufacturing competition in the markets of the world. The last outgoing steamer from the port of San Francisco took 1,500 bales of cotton to Japan. During the past few weeks about 14,000 bales of this season s cotton have gone to Yokohama, about 4,000 additional bales are on the way, and before the shipments are ended the rail- way people expect to handle not less than. 50,000 bales. Several months ago the representative of a manufacturing syndicate made arrangements for the extensive shipments now taking place,and.from reliable statistics, the gentleman furnished, it ap- pears that the: Japanese cotton manufacturers have increased the number of their spindles from 500,000 in 1894 to 750,000 in 1895. and they expect that during the season of 1896 to put out orders for not less than 100,000 bales. What it cost in the United States 50,000do1s.in wages to produce from 4,000 bales of cotton, plain saleable cotton products, the same can be produced in Japan for about 17,500d015. Labor and fuel are so cheap that neither Europe nor the states can stand out against her in the open field of competition. as Japan has no patent laws and no international restraints on her citizens,they are free to make the best use of any improvement or invention, and undoubtedly Japanese agents are selling in American markets to day chemicals, and textile goods which the home manufacturer and producer cannot touch. No alien can. in Japan,own real estate, therefore all their mills are built for.and are under the control of Japanese citizens. They are well managed, economical methods prevail, and a large percentage of profit is paid to stockholders. The Japanese have studied well and thorouuhly the industrial outlook,and have taken steps accordingly. They have selected introduced into the States to a very the best among them, and in the educational institutes of the West they have gra- duated, and become familiar with our needs. . They re making watches now for 4s. each, that are Y r!' in g all over the civilized world, and e that are s ell- Rreat extent. It was said on a public platform in Y this city that "if we look in the public street we</p>
        <p>To Aln --"FtERS.—Mrs. Winslow's Soothing Syrup has been used over fifty fears by millions of mothers for their children while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poon sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it producer natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain, and the little cherub awakes "as bright] as a:button." Of all chemists, is. 11d. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
        <p>may see some of our best dressed men wearing clothing made in Japan." A Japanese agency is now established in this city, at the head of which is one who has any thing but a Japiinesque name—Owen Burns. He says, "It is simply a matter of dollars andcents with us and with the purchasers. If we can supply them with an article as good as can be manufactured in America and at the same time sell that article at a rate much lower than they have been in the habit of paying here, I believe, as we have provel, that we will be able to sell that article.'' Mr. Reynall, a gentleman connected with the same agency,says, " My brother, who is at present in the South, has just purchased a cotton plant,which will be entirely removed to Japan. Six American workmen will go over to teach the Japanese how to run it." Mr. Revnall puts the matter pretty s'quare,when he says " We intend to get all we can fol our goods and shall cut just low enough to catch the trade, with- out gouging the market, and we can give better prices than American manufacturers ever dreamed of." One more example of persistence in pushing business may be given. Mr.. Reynall waited upon a firm doing a large importing business here. He showed some samples of buttons. He had a great assortment of them. There were buttons of bone, ivory, wood, mother of pearl, and metallic buttons I of all kinds, sizes and shapes, which when com- pared with the buttons of American and European make, were tile same in quality and appearance., and were all of Japanese make. These buttons were offered at a price per great gross, of twelve times one hundred and forty-four, which was actually less than the cost of a gross of the buttons on their shelves in the store. An order was given for a lot equivalent to a railroad car load, the total cost of which would be, duty paid, £l,BOO. This sale is said to be the largest single sale of buttons ever leffPcted in the United States. This big button sale has made quite a talk, I should say sensation, for it is felt to be the opening gun fired at the manufacturing interests of the United States in the great commercial war which Japan has begun. Japan has set out to conquer with the most powerful of all international weapons--the capacity for producing articles of neccessity more cheaply than the rest of the world. In following letters I will show other and more important phases of this new invasion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC. The severe gales of last week caused an appreciable falling off in the traffic, which was the smallest; yet re- corded this year. As compared with the returns for the preccding ssven days the arrivals and departures coast- wise were fewer by twenty, although there was slight increase in the tonnage. The foreign traffic showed a diminution of eight ships and 5,593 net registered tons. The imports included sixteen general cargoes, eleven of china clay, six of stone and slate, four of pig iron, three of green fruit, two of wood pulp,and one each of pyrites, 'flints, and pit props. Twenty general cargoes were ex- ported, ten of coal, and one of pitch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LIBERAL CLUB.</p>
        <p>THE BILLIARD HANDICAPS.</p>
        <p>SANITARY AUTHORITY FOR TaE PORT OF MANCHESTER. A. PROTEST FROM RUNCORN. At the Town Hall, Manchester, on Tuesday, Mr. Frederick William Barry, M.D., barrister-at-law' opened an inquiry touching the proposal that the Local Govern- ment Board should issue an order permanently constitut- ing a Port Sanitary Authority by forming a joint board having jurisdiction within the port of Manchester, or parts ot the said port. Representatives of the several authorities within the jurisdiction of the proposed Board were present. They included —for Manchester, the Lord Mayor, Town Clerk, Medical Officer, Mr. Alderman Thompson, Mr. Alder-Lien Walton Smith, Mr. Alderman M'Dougall, Mr. J. W. Southern and Mr. Thackray, Assistant City Treasurer; Salford, Town Clerk and Mr. Alderman B. Robinson ; I.,retford, Mr. Charles Estcourt, Mr. Thomas Robinson and Mr. Frank Whitworth, clerk; Runcorn Rural, Messrs. H. G. Heywood, John Mosley- Williams, John B. Chadwick, J. Brandreth and J. Ashton, clerk. Runcorn Urban, Messrs. E. Imisna, F. Wiswall and J.W. Lightburn •, Warrington,Mr.Alderman Sutton, Councillor Roberts, Medical Officer and Town Clerk; Bucklow Rural, Messrs. T. S. Ockleston, George Leigh, T. W. H. Garstang, medical officer; Widnes. Town Clerk and Medical Officer; Whiston Rural Dis- trict, *. W. Brown ; Wirral, Messrs. T. Davies, H. A. Lathahi and J. E. S. 011ive ; Lymm, Mr. John R. Smith; Ship Canal Company, Mr. A. H. Whit% orth ; Chester, Mr. R. Richardson ; Eccles, Town Clerk ; Barton Rural and District Council, Mr. James Berry and Mr. J. W. Whitworth. The Town Clerk of Manchester (Mr. W. H. Talbot) read the notice which has been published relative to the inquiry. Dr. Barry reminded the authorities represented that he met them last October to confer with respect to the formation of a port sanitary district for the port of Manchester, and that as a result of that conference he made a report to the Local Government Board, upon which it was determined to form a Port Sanitary District, and to issue an order accordingly. The terms of that order were now before them, and he proposed to go throligh it clause by clause and to hear any objec- tion there might b 3 to any particular clause, and take into consideration any amendments that might be pro posed. The various clauses of the order were then considered, some amendments were approved, and with regard to one or two others the Commissioner said he would con- sider the questions raised. The order sets forth that whereas Manchester is now a port and due inquiry has been made the Local Government Board direct as follows :—That a joint board be formed and per- manently constituted the Port Sanitary Authority -for the Port of Manchester, the joint board to be termed " The Manchester Port Sanitery Authority," and to consist of ten members, to be chosen—four by Manches- ter, four by Salford, one by Stretford, and one by a joint committee of the Urban District Council of Run- corn and the Rural District Council of Runcorn. Mr. Lightburn and Mr. Ashton, representing Runcorn Urban and Runcorn Rural respectively, pleaded for 20 mem- bers, so that they might each have one—in other words that each of the districts mentioned should have double the representation proposed, and that instead or a joint committee selecting the Runcorn representative there should be one selected by each of the bodies hav- ing to do with Runcorc. Mr. Alderman Smith and Mr. Alderman Thompson opposed this proposal. They urged that ten members would be sufficient,and that to double toe representation would be to put more work on gentle- men who were already heavily burdened. The Com- missioner said he saw some point in what was urged by the Runcorn representatives. There would be some difficulty in getting °Ste representative appointed by a joint committee, and if Manchester, Salford, and &amp;ret- ford would agree to the proposed increase they would not, he thought, find it any great hardship.—Mr. Alder- man Thompson : I do not press the objection.—Dr. Barry: I have found that difficulty in other ports, and it was only in accordance with the feeling expressed at the last conference that the number was limited in this way.—Thereupon the number to be appointed by Man- chester was made eight instead of four ; Salford's repre- sentatives to be similarly increased; Stretford will have two instead of one, and one will be selected by each of the Runcorn authorities. The expenses incurred by the Authority are to be borne in the following proportions: ight-twentieths by Manchester, eight-twentieths by Salford, two-twentieths by Stretford, and one-twentieth by each of Runcorn local authorities—urban and rural. On the subject of expense Mr. Lightburn complained that the amount that would fall upon Runcorn was gt eater in proportion than that which the other authori- ties would have to bear. He would like Runcorn to re- main as it had hitherto been, and that the arrangement they had had with Liverpool for the isolation of any vessels that needed isolation should be continued.—Mr. Williams (Runcorn Rural) supported the view advanced by Mr. Lightburn. They felt, he said, that they were being exposed to a considerable expense for no purpose, seeing that they had a hospital of their own and an ar- rengemeet with Liverpool. The Commissioner pointed out that the Authority would have to deal with all shipping in the district, the health of the crews, and the condition of the vessels— things which Runcorn, he was bound to say, had not done in the past, but he would not say anything about that matter. Mr. Williams remarked that as regarded Runeern Rural they would have no ships to deal with, for they would have no port after Saltport was closed. Mr. Lightburn said their arrangement with Liverpool was a very good one. They had power to isolate any ship or its crew in case of need, and they had nothing to pay if nothing was done. The Commissioner said that Runcorn had now no arrangement with Liverpool that was recognised by the Local Government Board, and any arrangement that would be made in future would be with the Port Sani- tary Authority of Manchester. Mr. Heywood asked if an arrangement could not be made by Runcorn with Manchester similar to that which they used to have with Liverpool. The Ship Canal, he added, had been thrust upon them; they did net ask for it, and it was hardly of any benefit to them—in fact, if anything, it had brought more paupers into their dis- trict than there were before. They felt the burden of the canal in their poor-rate, and now they were going to feel it in their sanitary rate. He thought,if they could have an agreement with the Port Sanitary Authority of Man- chester like that which they once had with Liverpool, it would be more satisfactory, and they would cease all further protests. but he did not think they could let the matter rest where it now was. If there was going to be a halfpenny in the pound put on to their rates by reason of this Port Sanitary Authority he was sure there would be further protests. The Commissioner : I will take note of your protest. Mr. Alderman Thompson: I think Runcorn has no reason to utter these protests. She can open her mouth wide, and has done so, and made an excellent bargain both with the Canal Company and the Authority. These people, moreover, ought to take into account the benefit that will come to them from that excellent navigation, and the benefit it will be to their neighbourhood. Mr. Imison said the " benefit " so far had been a re- duction in their rateable value and an increase in the number of empty houees.—(Mr. Alderman Thompson That is salt.) They felt they would be crushed between these two great bodies—Manchester and Salford. They had had an object leseon that morning: they had sem that it was only necessary that the official representa- tives of Manchester and Salford should meet together and shake hands and say this or that was acceptable to them, and the other authorities were ob- liged to acquiesce in the arrangement that was made, and pay for something they did not require for the pro- tection of their own district. That was what they felt, and felt somewhat strongly. He did not presume to say what would be done by Runcorn, but he thought they would feel it necessary to make further representations. They had no foreign trade practically. Mr. Lightburn said he thought the good or bad of the canal to Runcorn was not under consideration. On that point they probably held divergent views ; but he wished to Point this out, that when the Canal Bill was before Parliament he was assured that Runcorn would not be affected as regarded the arrangements for a sani- tary authority. The Town Clerk of Manchester submirted that as the friends from Riincorn had secured a representation on the joint board, and as the order had been amended to meet their views, it was not competent for them now to take un an attitude of antagonism. The whole of the proceedings so far as Manchester was concerned had been based on the idea that they would act cordially to- gethereand he hoped nothing would be done at this stiee to disturb the general harmony. The points on which he conferred with the Town Clerk of Salford were ex- tremely minor points. He thought the Runcorn people need have no fear than any coercien would be used to the prejudice ef their district. The representatives of Manchester and Salford wished to treat Runcorn as the wished themselves to be treated. Tee commissioner said he proposed to close the in- quiry and would report in due course to the Local Government Board. The objections which had been co well put before him by certain of the authorities would be duly considered. On the proposal of Mr. Lightburn, seconded by Mr. Alderman Thompson, a vote of thanks was accorded to Dr. Barry for the urbane manner in which he had done his duty. Dr. Barry: Thank you, gentlemen. lam at all times glad to come into Lancashire, because I know that I shall have to deal with business men.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION OF PRIZES. The other evening the large Committee room of the Warrington Liberal Club was filled to overflowing by billiard players and their friends, the occasion being the presentation of prizes won in the two Christmas billiard handicaps. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, president of the club, filled the position of chairman, and there were also in- cluded among those in the room Councillors Pierpeint J.P.,Plumpton, Tinnion, Wilkinson, Furness, and Shaw- Green, Messrs. T. elerris, T. Birchall J.P., C. J. Holmes. J. Clayton, J. Gandy, J. Strange, J. Albinson, F. R. Roberts, .1. Harrison and Mather, MR. CROSFIELD ON POLITICAL MATTERS. The CHMO.DIAN said : As regards politics, I should think politics ought to be limited to five minutes at the outside as we have a programme list covering two sides and a half of the paper,aed as we always profess to keep pod hours at this club, I must not detain You too long. If I do not offer you a finished pro-duct in the shape of t, peech it is not from any want of raw material. There is plenty of that both at home and abroad and I suppose in our more serious moments and leisure hours our thoughts do some- times wander abroad where such strange doings have lately been enacted. Some of us feel uneasy—as our good friend and excellent candidate remarked the other day—at the ghastly iniquities which have been perpet- rated in Armenia and I dare say a good many of you wished we had a man with grit and backbone at the head of affairs. (Hear, hear.) Somebody like the G.O.M. who could take that old Sultan by the scuff of the neck and say " Look here, you have got to look after these people in Armenia just the same as you look after your American contingent: you could do it if you chose and you have got to choose." (Applause.) In South Africa we have had some strange developments since our last meeting. We take a very active interest in them at Bank Quay as a good many thousand of our boxes of soap find their way into Johannesburg, Matabele laud, and other places in the Chartered Company's territory.We have had a very ugly interruption in our business there owing to the mad escapade of Dr. Jameson and his confreres.' hope fnrther,that we shall also hear of an amicable settlement in regard to Venezuela, which is another ugly and threatening problem to be dealt with. (Hear, hear, aed applause.) My five minutes are nearly up, but I turn for a moment to affairs nearer home.and there I think I should be remiss if I sat down without offering you my sincere congratulations, and voice the general feeling of satisfaction' at the course which affairs have taken. (Ap- plause.) I don't suppose there is anyone here who does not feel it a matter of great satisfaction that we have seen one cf our ablest, wisest,and best leaders returned to political activity. (Hear, bear, and applause.) lam sure it is your general opinion that the tone of things in the House of Commons and of the Liberal party generally, will be raised and their influence increased for the better by his active presence among us. (Applause.) There are other elections where results have not been less satisfactory, and if that has been the swing of the pendulum in other quarters, coming to our own borough it seems to me that there is a tendency of opinion moving in the same direction. (Hear, hear and applause.) I don't know that anyone who has the wel- fare of the borough at heart will regret—though there are some who will—certain events which have taken place. We, at any rate believe we have cause for hon- est satisfaction in seeing that the influences which have been too much nredominent in the town are to be checked by the counter-influences which I believe are at work. If we have matters for congratulation we are preparing to celebrate them with musical honours at considerable length and I suggest we start at once with the programme. (Applause.) During an interval in the programme which followed Mr. Crosfield presented the prizes for the handicaps, two of which were won by Councillors Tinnion and Fatness. He said he believed there were about 35 different indus- tries in Warriegton and when be had before presented prizes they generally consisted of examples of one or another of our home industriesit would be unseemly for him to say much about the prizes on the table because he had something to do with their selection last August in Switzerland where it struck him that the Swiss carving was interesting. There were four or five thousand men in Switzerland who had a speciality in this work, which did not extend to other countries,and great as was the variety of industry in Warrington he did not know that we had reached that kind of work yet, so he picked up some Swiss carving and MOZSIC work, the latter another sneciality of the people of the Neapolitan district. Among the prizes received,a list of which we have al- ready published was one which had not previously been known of. That was a fully dressed doll which Mr. F. R. Roberts was called upon to accept as his prize,amidst great merriment and cries of " A speech, a speech." Mr. Roberts accepted the troßhy and turning round said " Gentlemen. This may bet ,prophetic or it may not." (Loud laughter.) Councillor TINNION proposed a vote of thanks to the donors of the prizes and said he did so with great pleasure. So far as he knew the donors were just the same gentlemen who had always came forward to help the club in every kind of enjoyment namely, Mr. Cros- field, Mr. Morris, Mr. Pierpoint, Mr. Fleming, and many others. There gentlemen were always willing and ready to help them in any good work. He never re- membered the roll of membership being so large as at present. They had there that night a lot of young men who be might say were the future lighting talent of Liberalism, and from what he knew of them and the way they went into everything they took up, he was sure that the success of Liberalism and the Liberal club was safe in their hands. He only hoped this would not be the last presentation of prizes. As a suggestion he said that he thought it would be better if in future instead of the upstairs and downstairs handicaps they had separate coeteets and prizes for those who were really the crack-players and those that came next on the list. There would then he less likelihood of one player getting more than one prize. He thought the zommittee might consider the matter. In conclusion he said he thought the least they could do was to accord the donors a hearty vote of thanks for their kindness and thus show their appreciation of what they had done for them. Mr. T. ALLEN endorsed all that Mr. Tinnion had said, and seconded the vote of thanks,which was then carried unanimously. SUGGESTED NEW CLUE PREMISES. Councillor PIERPOINT,iG responseisaid that so far as he was concerned ae a donor of prizes he was rather at a loss to understand whether he was a donor or not,except that whenever there had been anything on foot in the club he had been ready to do what he could to help on the harmonious working of the club. He thought the club Was new getting too small, and as it appeared that most things which the Liberal Party put their hands to Came to a successful issue, it had struck him, seeing the crowded state of that room, that it would not be a had thing for the Liberal Party of Warrington to con- sider the possibility and probability of building more commodious premises of their own. (Loud applause.) That club was certainly fairly in the centre of the industrial part of Warrington but he thought they might perhaps have a club nearer their "friends in Sankey-street," and more commodious than theirs but he did not say more pretentious ; and if the party would only determine it would have a club, there was that within it that would ensure the success of a movement of that kind. Me. Pierpoint expressed his sorrow that Mr. Crosfield wag unable to be present at the conversazione in Parr Ball which reflected such great credit upon all the ladies and gentlemen who had the management of it. As they all knew it had turned out one of the most successful re- unions of Liberalism in Warrington. He then referred to Liberalism in general, and said the more they could spread the ideas of Liberalism in this country of ours the better it would be for every individual member of the community RS a whole. He considered the great success of the club dorine the past few months was mainly due to the hanpv end artistic selection of prizes which Mr. Crosfield had made, and which reflected great credit noon him. He (Mr. Pierpoint) concluded by thanking Messrs. Tinnion and Allen for the kind way in which they had thanked the donors. Mr. THOMAS MORRIS proposed a vote of thanks to the chairman and, referring to the suggestion of Councillor Pierpoint, said that new and more commodious club premises were badly needed, and he was prepared to give a round sum of some £5OO or thereabouts if nine or ten other gentlemen would contribute a like amount. This would he sufficient he thought for a good and com- modious building. If the money was not forthcoming that way he suggested that a company might be formed for the purpose and he thought it would be a paying concern. Mr.Morrie' offer was enthusiastically received and the vote of thanks after being seconded was unanimously accorded. Apart from the presentation and speechmaking the proceedings were chiefly in the nature of a smoking con- cert and the programme submitted proved highly enjoy- able to the large audience. Songs (humorous and other- wise) were given in a meritorious manner by Messrs. F. Fieldhotise, Andertnn, Greennalgh, J. Chappel, P. Jones, end Finch ; Mr. Anderton also gave a trombone solo, which was well received and Mr. M. Butterfield delighted the audience with the recitals of"Reubenstein's Piano" and " Tarnation Strange." At the suggestion, yocifernwlv endorsed by the company, of Mr. Pier; noint, the chairman gratified his hearers by playing Reubenstein melody on the piane. Various encores wet demani4erl and the programme lengthened out so the 10 o'clock was reached before the proceedings con f eluded. ees</p>
