        <p>defended. The case, as stated by the solicitor for the prosecution, was that the prisoner was in the neighbourhood of Arnold between two and three o'clock in the afternoon of the day of the tragedy. In the evening he induced Mrs. Powley and her children to leave the house hur- riedly, on the pretext that her husband had met:with an accident at a spot about a mile distant. Finding the message was a hoax they returned, and having their fears aroused gave information to a constable, who ac- cempanied them to the residence. Mrs. Kaye was found in an unconscious condition. She had terrible injuries upon her bead and face and other parts of her body, and tt e ceiling and walls of the kitchen were bespattered with blood. She died three days after her removal to the Nottingham Hospital. From Mr.Powley's house a silver watch and gold. chain, a silver chain, six sovereigns,two half-sovereigns,and six half-crowns were stolen. The prisoner waa not again seen at Arnold on the night of February 14, but he was traced to Not- tingham on the following morning,where at eight o'clock he redeemed from the shop of Mr Poyser in Carrington a tin box containing clothes which he had pledged there on February 12. He tendered a half-sovereign. After redeeming the box, it was believed he went to a public- house in the vicinity, where he changed his Coat and waistcoat, leaving those which he had been wearing behind him. It would be proved that upon that coat, land he believed also upon the waistcoat, stains of blood were discovered. On the same day the prisoner seemed to have gone from Nottingham to Manchester, and at the Central Station there he left the *tin box he had redeemed from Mr. Poyser's. In Manchester on three separate days he was found tendering sovereigns, although he had been begging at Arnold on the 14th. Afterwards he disposed in public-houses of the silver watch and gold chain, and then of the silver chain. There were twenty-seven witnesses for the prosecu- tion. Samuel Powley, at whose house Mrs. Kaye was stay- ing, said he left home on the evening on which the murder was perpetrated to further his candidature for the Urban Council. He was at the Ram Inn during the evening. There he saw the prisoner, and heard him ask one of the customers who he (witness) was. The ques. tion was answered, and then witness left the house he was followed by Wilkinson. Thinking that he was a neighbour, witness shook hands with him and asked him for his vete.—(Sensation). Several other witnesses 'corroborated as to Wilkinson having been in Arnold on the afternoon and night of the murder.—A woman named Vernon identified him as hav- ing begged at her house, and the priloner created some sensation in court by rising in his place and asking wit- ness it she knew that when she kissed the Testament he had sworn to speak the truth. Wilkinson then denied the witness's statement. Another witness deposed to Wilkinson inquiring for Powley's house half an hour before the family were lured out and Mrs. Kaye murdered. Mrs.Powley admitted that when first she was asked to identify Wilkinson she failed to do so, but exclaimed, "Oh, the villain, where is ht-," and then became hysteri- cal. She now recognised his form and voice. The prisoner persisted that witness first identified another man. Medical witnesses described the terrible injuries which Mrs. Kaye was suffering from, and gave it as their opin- ion that the wounds were inflicted by the poker pro- duced. An ostler at a Nottingham public-house stated that a man resembling the prisoner changed his coat and vest in the vaults on the morning after the murder. He failed to return, and the clothes beine old and stained, were thrown into the stable, and afterwards given to the police. The pawnbroker next proved the pledging and redeem- ing by the prisoner of the tin box, afterwards found at Manchester. Evidence having been given of the prisoner's arrest, Dr. Taylor deposed that he found unmiatakable signs of blood upon the prisoner's coat and waistcoat. Wit- ness had a doubt about the trousets,but they were being microscopically examined by an expert. A number of witnesses from Manchester gave evidence as to the prisoner selling the watch and chain stolen from Powley's for 7s. 81., saying that he had won them in a raffle. They spoke also to his having changed gold, Wilkinson spent most of his time in a a public-house til arrested for burglary. The prisoner, who had nothing to say, was committed :or trial on the capital charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WONDRRFUL ORIGINALITY OF THE EARLY ROMA.NS.</head>
        <p>WONDRRFUL ORIGINALITY OF THE EARLY ROMA.NS.</p>
        <p>THE WORLD'S LA.W MAKERS.</p>
        <p>Greece gave the world learnine. Rome gave it laws, and Britain freedom. Greece, however, drew so largely firm.; Egypt that her contribution to the world's store, thougn of the greatest value, could hardly be said to he original. Rome, on the contrary, created her laws and ideas of freedom without outside assistance, and Britain tn-day and for ages has been living under laws copied verbatim often from Roman laws of many centuries ago, The East gave to Greece but not to Rome, for the East, though learned and artistic, has always been the home of tyranny and could not teach freedom. The Romans carried their laws and ideas of freedom to the West and North. Britain fortunately was near enough to the coast of France for the Romans to cross over. Had we been a few hundred miles away we would never have been visited by Cze:ar and would not to-rlay he the great nation which we are, and, further, we would not have carried freedom to all quarters of the world. To the Romans and to our insular position are due the thanks of the world, for the one gave us our excellent laws and ideas of freedom and the other compelled us to build ships or stay at home. Thus we roved about the world and carried freedom with us. If we are not so original as the Romans in certain lines, we are in others. Such men as Stephenson, Harvey, Watts, Lis- ter, and I may say Jenner, have done enough original work to live in name for ever. Perhaps Jenner's dis- covery, however, was not the undiluted blessing he thought it. for it is well known vaccination causes many dis'ases. and often death. British originality, however, has discovered V. V. V., or Vaccination Virus Van- quisher, to protect those who are vaccinated without afTecting, the pure lymph. This wonderful Antisepti blood remedy prevents the catching of any disease through vaccination, and cures all the old chronic dis- eases of skin, hair, eyes, bones, and internal organs, as well as scrofula, tuberculosis, and syphilis, if the germs of these diseases have been passed into the blood direct by vaccination. This point is important, because V. V. V. acts by its antiseptic action through the blood, and when the germs of these diseases are carried into the blood by the vaccinator's knife, it never fails to destroy them. Though powerful in killing these germ', it is perfectly harmless to human beings, even to infants. It is sold by all chemists at 2s. 9d., and by the distributors, Barclay and Sons. Limited, 95, Farringclon Street, Lon- don; also by the V. V. V. Company, Limited, 31, Snow Hill, London, who send free literature to all who apply.</p>
