      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>NEW BOOKS, MA.GA.ZINES, ETC.</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS, MA.GA.ZINES, ETC.</p>
        <p>CASSELL AND CO.—" The Magazine of Art" for March is specially attractive as in addition to its usual quota of excellent pictorial matter and prose there is an extra 16 pages containing numerous re- productions of representative works by the late Lord Leighton A list ef the pictures given is as follows :—" The Industrials Arts as Applied to Peace ;" "Lord Leighton,P.R.A.," by himself; "Helen of Troy ;" " Elijah in the Wilderness;" "The Studio ;" " Study for 'the Bracelet ;" "Athlete struggling with a Python ;" " Moretta" (engraved by M. Dortnoy) ; "Study for Captive Andromache" "A Bacchante ;" "Study for 'Flaming June;'" "A. corner in the small Studio ;" "The Frigidariurn "Study for 'Solitude "" Lord Leighton," by him- self; "A noble lady of Venice ;" "And the sea gave up the dead which were in it ;" "Acropolis of Lindos ;" " A Condottiere " (engraved by C. Fl. Banda); "Study-model for the Garden or the Hes- perides ;" " Phosnicians bartering with Ancient Britians;" and "Lord Leighton." Mr. M. H. Spiel- man supplies the biographical sketch accompany- ing the pictures. A frontispiece to the number is "Permus and Andromeda' also by Lord Leighton,and this itself is a beautiful reproduction and a fitting commencementto this special number. Every lover of art is recommended to secure such a unique pub- lication as this. Messrs. Cassell and Co, have also issued a cheap weekly edition of their concise cyclopeedia, with numerous illustrations. No. two of this work which is published at a penny was issued on Wednesday. This useful edition should be a great boon to the intelligent working class, WARD, LOCK, AND BOWDRN.—Th4) budget of literature in the current number of "The Windsor" is of first class magazine order. Pr. Nikola's unique and striking personality is attracting unusual at- tention and the story is well launched into the realm of the mysterious and exciting. The Martin Hewitt series, the illustrated articles and other familiar items, are all inclusive of subjects likely to please the majority of readers. MACMILLA.N AND Co.—We are pleased to an- nounce further representatives of popular series of books viz :"In Memoriam,"and'eNlaueof the Tenny- sonian edition and "Lavengro" by George Borrow.nf the Illustrated Standard Novels. To the latter Mr. Augustine Birrell, Q.C., M.P., writes a capital intro- duction in which he mentions that " Lavengro " was commenced in 1843. 4.130rr0w'5 books "—he says—" have been found so full of the elixir of life of the charm of existence, of the glory of motion, so instinct with character, and mood,and wayward fancy, that their very names are sounds of enchant- ment, while the fleeting scenes they depict and the deeds they deQcribe have become the properties and the pastimes for all the years that are still to be of a considerable fraction of the English speaking race."</p>
