        <p>E MURDER OF A WARRINGTON WOMAN.</p>
        <p>THE PRISONER COMMITTED FOR TRIAL. At Nottingham, on Tuesday, Bamuel Wilkinson, 40, who was removed from Manchester on a writ of habeas corpus, charged with the murder of Mary Kaye, of War- rington at Arnold, near Nottingham, on February 14, was again placed in the dock. The court was crowded. Wilkinson bore himself with a callous air. He was not</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>DEATH OF MR. KENDRICK.</head>
        <p>THE DEATH OF MR. KENDRICK.</p>
        <p>INQUEST AND VERDICT. Mr. J. E. Worqley held an inquest on Monday, at the London Bridge Inn,Stockton Heath, on the body of Mr. Berry Kendrick, whose disappearance last week caused so much attention, and whose body was found in the Bridgewater canal near Red-lane bridge, on Saturday</p>
        <p>morning. The first witness called was Henry Devaudry Kendrick who said that deceased was his father,and was 57 years of age. The last time he saw his father alive was at 11 30 on Sunda.y night March Ist. He seemed then in his usual health. He had suffered from a pain in his back about four months previously, but would not have a doctor. Witness thought it was influenza. He had been depressed on the Saturday before his disappearance, but he was more cheerful on Sunday. It was discovered that he was missing at seven o'clock on Monday morn- ing when the servant went downstairs. It was tound that he had dressed himself with the exception of his collar and tie and had gone out. He had bolted the door the previous night,and in the morning it was found un- bolted. Witness could not tell whether his father had been in bed. Deceased was an entomologist and some- times got up early in the morning to go to Acton Grange. He never dressed himself fully on these occasions, but used to leave his collar and tie at home until he went to business. -Teem was nothing to upset his mind. The body was recovered on Saturday.—By a juryman: Witness did not think deceased's mind was deranged. William Arthur Cook, Hill Cliffe Cottage, waterman said that he found the hat produced about 200 yards on the Runcorn side of Red-lane bridge on the canal bank ; the name and address being in it. Be found the hat on Monday morning at five minutes to six. There were no signs of any struggle,or of a person having slipped. The hat was two yards ;from the side. The place where the hat was found would be 300 yards from the house of Mr. Kendrick. There was a strong wind that morning, and the bat was dirty when it was found. Be thought the hat had been put there as a token. Be did not take the hat back straight away, because he did not attach much importance to it at the time as they found so , many hats in the canal. P.C. Brew stated that he received information of Mr. Kendrick being missing on the 2nd inst. He caused all all inquiries to be made, but no result came from the handbills sent out. The cans! was dragged on Saturday about 300 yards beyond Red-lane bridge. The bady was fully dressed with the exception of collar.tl3,and bat.D - ceased's boots were laces% and he had his overcoat—an Inverness cape—on.He had since examined the body and there were no marks of violence upon it. From Mr. Kendrick's door to the towing path was only 30 yards. The body was near to where the hat was found. A slip of the bank was about 20 yards away. He considered that slip very dangerous in the dark. The Coroner, in summing up said that the death of Mr. Kendrick was an extremely sad one, and he thought there were few names in Warrington more respected than the name which Mr. Kendrick bore, and he himself by his amiability and universal kindness also had tne respect which was always given to his name. He had made many friends, and, he thought he was right in saying, no enemies. There was enly one verdict he thought which they could return and that was that Mr. Kendrick was found drowned in the canal, as there was absolutely no evidence to show how he gat into the canal. When the body was found, it was fully dressed, and as the bank of the canal was only 30 yards from his door, it looked as though he had contemplated a longer walk than that. He therefore thought that the verdict to be returned was one of "Found drowned." The jury returned a verdict to this effect, after which the Coroner said that he was sure that the jury would think it the proner thing to express their sympathy with the family at the loss they had sustained. The jury agreed to this, and the inquiry terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CIIURCHES-WARRINGTON,</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CIIURCHES-WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CONGREGATIONAL CHURCH. On Sunday evening the above place of worshir was well filled when the pastor, the Rev. John Yonge preached a special sermon to young men. He took for his text, Psalm xvi, part of v. 11, " Toe Path of Life." From these words he delivered a stirring and earnest dis- course exhorting all young men to be certain that the path they were treading. was indeed "The Path of Life" and not the path of rum. lie criticised strongly the course of behaviour described as " seeing life " and warned his hearers against any desire to act out that phrase.</p>
        <p>The sneez3 is not without its advantages, says M. Sobier. In some foreign countries sneezing is considered the height of ill manners, and yet how t.revalent was the use of snuff in England in the reign ot the Georges. A sneeze is an alarm clock, and gives notice that the body is losing its heat too rapidly and needs protection. A sneeze may announce the approach of a cold. The well- wishing that one receives in some countries from the lower classes shows that its good effects are appreciated. While a sneeze need not be boisterous, it should not be suppressed.</p>
      </div>
