        <p>IHR Great City of Manchester is undoubtedly the</p>
        <p>Cargest oentre of commerce in the world. It is. nest to Lon- 40n th _ e most important city in the British empire; its ?otnnaeizial enterprise is famous throughout civilisation, and rafts even penetrated to the daraest recesses of barbaric M"ItY. Manchester is the centre of a population within _Miles of her Town Hall as great as London, the whole of iichis engaged in the cause of labour. Day by day the r• w.erful and mighty engines of steam power are, under the '.!'aL • =trot ot the mech,nic and operative, busily engaged' ?onetributing to the successful trading of this marvellously Z▪ 1v And indostrious community. is my visit to this won- _ :MI city 1 chanced to discover amongst the many gigantic 11.1.?xt Colossal hives of industry a well-known famous furnish - lug eatablishine.nt, 4. Tau PexrEIBON." Bufliee. it to my that" The pantheon" le indisputably the Le,r,3,', IP vest Furnishing Establishment in the North of fln,g- iw;ft ,reinsively engaged in Furnishing. It is not a Drapery tor4l "lent, but is a solid, compact, massive warehouse from t:h.LK over a million square feet of Showrooms packed ture DT,l'entent to the roof by the Finest Stock of Furni- billie%f --ser inspection outside the city of London. The des- by mt great establishment, I Mid, are presided over Nr_bose.n"' H. Worsley, the sole and absolute proprietor. to „„ KM— --"lrnag energy and unflagging enterprise "The. eon we its high position. To visit " The Panthe.on 11,1:417 the tram from the corner of Mossley-street.Piccadilly, tram Park way. In a very law minutes, after Bt. Peter's Church and the mammoth Central Sta- po4l,. was " informed by the guard of my arrival at Tne t ,39.. My visit being in the evening, I was amazed at b. •10.."‘t11 display of electricity, the whole of the vas ler ving just been splendidly lighted by the Manches- Crewk?'o Company. under the able supervision of Mr. ert ebebialricol consulting engineer, of Cross-street, I was Pitt t° evecything as favourably as in daylight. "The tho, ' °,l has immense show windows fronting Gay- tkie 1 0 i.er 100 feet long. forming a gig mtic display. Noting Invitavitition displayed on the windows, Inspac ion earttizi.." wi'bout diffidence I immediately embraced the op- " TIY by wFlklng inside; announced my desire to see riot Atitheon," though not wishing to purchase. I was te most cordial manner by one of the numerous rrasZen. who very courteously offered to conduct me. "'"°ooe struck with</p>
        <p>(r 11WHELMING SURPRISE. tire Of variety. On the ground floor I found a well ',.ordinary immensity of the stock being sufficient ati.4—aitee ~ 11%4(4 stock of Bedroom Furniture of the better cla•s, ktties innumerable at from £3O to £l5O. Magnificent sped- bur; in rithly-figured pollard oak, American walnut and Oth..l.l!_titiviood, exquisitely inlaid rosewood,Chippendale and Iltra--11,, • These stood out prominently. Struck with the Oref one large ollard oak Bedroom Suite, I ventured Isse ~,,ue price expecting it to be quite £4O) to £5OO, and CloodZr,lhised to fiad the price only £120; another Suite, by ThettiL. Which must have cobt ne4rly £203. pece only £65. asloui,''Per bedroom suites I was intormed were displayed in t.ittstohewroom reserved for the purpose. A summary of tueob„,, on view labriefly 200 bedroom suites complete, over lhil , -tl2-7, tteads 150 drawiusuites.-room sues. 150 criblnets, 175 L"hfds-„,".°1:11 su'ites, and over 150 sideboards, besides the thou- bargains in all the sundries which are essential to l'islt furnishing. My curiosity was next aroused in a kte. a`iu the Carpet Showroom, which has been newly refitted. evelled in exquisite artistic colourings 1)188°LVING THE SOUL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <p>acgolissense, of loveliness. Carpets is inwiamolln,thaenidr Tapestry. . Axminster, Persian. Brusse 2 neon, 500 Setsond-hand truest parably low prices. p.„ Is is 11d. Tapestry from s 9d per yard, 'Vets form a special plum for those requiring Carpets barsgfarionm; issgs Second-hand Axminster and Brilebei pet tk- 6"0 £3 10d, according to size and quality. Adjoining Carpet Showroom is the Linoleum and..sOliacylotoht tDheepart-t_ th fitted with rollers for e Proper Linoleums from Ed to 2s 6d.Oilc?oths from 6d to 'S 11d. Inlaid Linoieums at 3s 11d. I was now conducted 144 sirs to the Dining-roona Furniture Showroom. I was impressed by the enormous display of Massive ,81 Dining-room Furniture, the bulk of which is magnifi- Vl4 carved in bas raief, reminding one of the artistic 'it displayed in TRHATUNI&amp;N TEMPLES. ..,„114Reilicent productions by splendid old makers, such as %allows, Garnett, Lamb Henry Ogden and Sons, Az., at Onsitively less prices tlin the common new London furniture Ilbyr so largely retailed by drapers, /cc. For instance, I noted magnificent lit 61n Sideboard, by Doveston, Bird, Bind 'lull, which could not have cost less than £lOO, price £l6 guineas. What possible chanc's has new Londo.. furniture in :npetition with bargains such as here displayed? Next a •Plendid Belk' oak Sideboard, with brilliant crystal mirJors. for gm los ; original cost £75. You cm positively obtain Sideboards, second hand,condition equal to new, from 30s to £lO, worth three or four times the amount; and, better still, you can depend upon the stability. A. common new side- board is practically worthless ; oracks may develop suffi- ciently wide as to almost dis slcse the contents to your visitors. I should strongly recommend the purchase of a good high-class sideboard, second hand, in preference to new. it doesit matter what price you wish to give,yeu can obtain %Lsplendid bargain at Worsley's in Sideboards from 30s up to xlO3, all equalcy cheap and durable. The same rule applies biningroom Suites, Overmactels, and Dining Tables. Pst bargains in this department are simply wonderful. Ville, handsome Diningroom Suite, in morocco, with couch, IV easy chairs, and six single chairs, second hand, but tratlos4_ order cost £55 for £9 lOs ; this is a sample of what atiLus obtained. Of course there is ample variety in price, Ist j.,from £2 10s to £65. A complete Suite in solid oak may ear it for comparatively a trifling sum. My attention was twoetito a Solid Oak Dining-room Suite, consisting of couch, tatY chairs, and six single chairs. in morocco; a m,gni- nt Sideboard, Overmantet, and Dining Table, the lot for worth £75, and cat £lOO. This seems almost in- r.ible, but a visit will amply demonstrate the verification. „o,ms hear a brilliant display of rapid evolutionary chords on ;Pimnoforte, and am thereby attracted to the New Pianoforte 84 '3on recently erteteft adjoining the Dining-room Show- I find that the proprietor is not only a remarkable 41,tti3ierlitg;tio business man' this but th ,t he is endowed with no mean aaply gmaoPnra i:triistt.i.ntghepe,rifpoi magnificent orim aaccmeaogfniaCenht lopiilrarPd iaolponani: forte. The Pianoforte Saloon contains all the best known tstrurnents by the highest class makers at a price irum 2.1 to '29 per cent, less than music sellers' list prices. For instance, liirkman's span brand new Upright Grand Cottage Pianos. with all the lalest imptovements, are offered at 28i guineas, tPrice hitherto unknown. A very fine Tricho:d new Piano- :rtc. specially made for Worsley's, is retailed at 17i guineas, se something new as to price There are a hundred instru- laents on vie Al. but the gems of the bargains are the Second- hand Pianofortes. Here you can get pianos from 60s. tol£so. *imply incredible value. Go and fee for yourself ; it will lost you nothing.but may profit you much. American Organs %Wally cheap. The Music Saloon contains a number of VHARMING PICTURIIS _NJ</p>
        <p>11—Y well-known artists, at astonishing low prices. There are °la this floor varieties of sundries, articles of virtu,ornamental 411 d aaluable property of all kinds, but space forbids all I IlgUld write of what I saw The next showroom is the Draw- .and it is almost impossible to do justice to the ;MY beautiful and exquisite display of really High-class New ,Zus.f.! Second-hand Drawing-room Furniture. You can pur- -414_,'! a suite for Li 10s, and you can buy one as highs' £l5O. re are 163 suites on view, for variety and beauty r- ;al/ailed. There is only space to illusi rate a sample bargain, A magnificent Secoad-hand Drawing-room Suite, tisvinet, overmantel, two tables, card table, and coal cabinet, ewhole in Chippendale mahogany of Louis XIV. style, 3' carved ; the suite has been newly re-upholstered in brocaded silk of delicate artistic colourings ; everything t.7,11nal to new. Fri, e for the lot 21i guineas ; worth quite IC. Very pretty suites may be purchased at £7 to £lO. slext department is the Bedstead Showroom, where you —°,4 once dazzled by the MOUNTAINS OF GOLD. 11:161,, brilliant collection of solid Brass Bedsteads, having the eill'eara, rice of a mountain of gold. Hundreds of exquisitely 1r0b.41.,ed brass bedste xis at incredible low prices are on show 1447 °Zs 6d up to £25. Two thousand bedsteads from Si 6d sh,_3' be inspected, besides many wonderful bargains in m,`,7ld-hand Bedsteids For instance, a magniftcent MRS- borTfla brass bedstead, which cost £25 when new, is shown 343-;,1106- I must now pass on to describe the enormous Suite Showroom, which is the largest in Manchester, fire.",nta.ins 150 tedroom suites from 35s to £65. The suites 151 Own in corridors, with plenty of width and space al- l% for inspection. The great feature at " Tne Pantheon" there is plenty of room, and a brilliant light enabling see what you are purchasing perfectly. In this depart- have splendid oppoitunities for securing a bargain. p, `eed one a tine handsome ma