      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>TILE EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>TILE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MARCH 140, 1896.</p>
        <p>SIR WILLIAM HARCOURT was in A Trenchant his best vein when speaking to a Speech. great Liberal audience at Bourne- mouth on Wednesday. The speech which he de- livered contained a vigorous assertion of Libera principles and some singularly treLchant criticism of Tory policy. As he declares, the Liberal Party does not, because it was defeated last year,e,bandon any of its principles. Self-government for Ire- land, temperance reform, and the promotion of religious equality are three of the great causes with which the Liberalism of ;these days is abso- lutely identified. For the time being they have suffered a reverse, but in that respect they are re- ceiving no worse treatment than most of the great reforms of the past. In 1835 the Liberal Party was defeated through its efforts to disestablish the Irish Church but some thirty years later that re- form was accomplished. Again, as Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT points out, the emancipation of the slave was not accomplished till after a long and arduous struggle. Sir WILLIAM'S reference to the subject of temperance legislation was brief but pointed. He asks those people who think that the drink evil is a thing which will right itself if left alone to study the returns of the Registrar General. Statistics do not support the arguments of these waiters upon Pi.ovidence, for there is evidently it steady and by no means a slow in- crease in the [lumber of victims of chronic alcohol- ism. Even the Unsonists do not now argue that the drink question can be left to settle itself. „lie_ y I ,Iv say. that the greatest ista onl marvel of all is that Mr. Little mere than a year ago Mr. CHAMBERLAIN declared that the promotion of temperance is the vhie,, ,Y nas instituted a nefw Gr dual Payinent System, vim.l4f no Inaugurated. Worblev's offa you all the advan- most urgent social reform," hut in spite of this ee„e'"rigt),B of their !ow price&lt; Anv o' the bargains I have 1- _ WI. It aike6 may be yard-la-tat:at t hi; prices ath dvertised, and ev no plan for dealing with this most urgent matter 4-lifferenrw you to pay oft by Gradual Payments, m i snaking no i s produced by the Government. The position what,ver in the invoi e price. I .cannot t dien my letter without, recommending t. tver) oture tto I . up by the Unionists on this subject is t:retlte eY 8 before ptirelias;‘tg ; all his comp e ors ymoe I neither logical nor dignified, for while style of husin,s, ,-,nt, my expertexice teaches they do not ce,:p=sleY reigt, suoretne'LlVorsiev is there, the rest ar., deny the existence of the evil and criticise and 6:11,e p„...n worstey3L, , k,E; no-n ever;" day from 9to 7. and condemn all th .4titin ' theon." i,e:rl,, •1, - ' -; .. ~ door to the Central e proposals of dealing with it,they l'ark, s Manchester i.,,a..nost next fnd The Alexandra utterly fail to prod road' unetio,. a, d. B er ,asy to 1 •produce any scheme of their own. 3 ali ;going City- pass th • r.idsaaw-street trani Turning t t.,i door. 1 o the declared policy of the Government,</p>
        <p>Avo;'*l,Xt a full account of th-3 va,t, stores of "The pantheon" liienZl3Cenpy the whole et this paper. I may. however, just tale f •Lt ,to.t is you cannot ask Worsley's for att,tiele of faiirniture without being quickly shown what ao.nire You are never met with the response, " Very eat it:, ,unt we are just out of what you require, but we can You." ‘,„oraieys keep a tremendous reserve sock. ),,,it'llean obtain Fnything you require in the furnishing line 'heKit.chen and Sitting-room furnishing tAn'lttuents are cram full of bargsins ; for instP.nre, secont rti4tro• mahogany hair-se dad SJfas or Couches from 15,, g"37 carved back Dressers from £3 ICs, mahogany on. 'nil Tablea trom 15s Kitchen Dressers from 3., and e,) Isnrr:lhe show rooms for Had Purnitute, Lit,rary, and Office tietonirture 'are equally chea,) and V"(l• A magniticient 'l,ROd. Hat and Umbrella Stand and pair of Hall Chairs, loria .inssst, £3O, may be purchased for £6 10s, a smaller set</p>
