      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>cUGAIL'S PATENT S ELFR k ISING FLOUR</head>
        <p>cUGAIL'S PATENT S ELFR k ISING FLOUR</p>
        <p>Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT points out that there Is little promise in it of the six years cf " repose " which was promised some time ago by the Duke of DEVONSHIRE. la domestic affairs the Government evidently intends to concentrate its energies upon two measures, both of which are eminently calculated .to disturb the repose of the country—namely, the relief of the landlords and an attack upon the education settlement of 1870. Upon the first-named of these subjects we had something to say last week, and it is evident from Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT'S speech that any attempt to "gerrymander" local rates for the bene- fit of landowners will be very strongly resisted. As Sir WILLIAM points out, this so-called " relief to agriculture" is to be given solely by transferring additional burdens upon taxpayers while nothing is to be said about tithes and rents. The proposal to interfere with the education system of the country is regarded even more seriously. "If you are," says Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT, "to take Lord SALISBURY'S allocution to the bishops, what is intended is an overthrow, an absolute overthrow of the fundamental principles of the settlement of 1870. If that is what is intended I will," he adds, "promise him that we will fight him, and that to the death." As a matter of fact, the voluntary schools have, in numerous instances, ceased to deserve the name. The proportion of their funds voluntarily contri- buted has rapidly decreased while the proportion obtained from the Government has enormously increased. In 1870 the Government subscription I from the public funds was only thirty-eight per cent ; now it is seventy-four per cent. More than a thousand of these "voluntary" schools have, actually, no private subscriptions; 674 have sub- scriptions of less than a shilling :a child ; 1095 have less .than half-a-crown per child and 1,967 have less than five shillings per child. Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT may well ask what is the use of calling these voluntary schools. The scheme with which the Government is credited for givin more public money to privately managed schools will certainly deserve all the criticism which Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT promises for it, and especially if it is aecompanied by conditions de- signed to lessen the efficiency of School Boards. The action of the Liberal party will, we may feel sure, not be guided by any desire to injure the voluntary schools which are carrying on a useful work, but at Up same time there will be an insis- tence upon the great democratic principle that when large grants of public money are made public control of the spending of that money must follow in one term or another. It is needless to say that in regard to foreign affairs Sir WILLIAM HARCOURT could find no signs of the " repose" or of the" political hybernation " which the DUKE of DEVONSHIRE predicted. He regards the situation abroad as of a very critical character, more critical, in fact, than it has been within the memory of living man. In the East of Europe the influence and reputation of Great ' Britain was never lower than it is at the present time, and this is a direct result of the pcliey adopted by the Tory party when Lord BEACONS- FIELD was its leader. That policy was designed to make British influence paramount in Tur- key in place of that of Russia, but exactly the opposite results have happened. The SULTAN is now, for all practical purposes, a satellite of the Czar, and the authority of this country has reached so low a point that we have not been able to honour the pledge by which we undertook to protect the Asiatic Christians from Mahommetan fanaticism. In other parts of the world,there is also an absence of"repose."The South Afrkan crisis is by no means at an end yet, and if Tory politicians of the ASHMBAD BARTLETT type are not restrained an extremely embarrassing situation may be produced in the South African colonies. The VeneT.uelan question is still an open sore between this country and the United States, and, so far at all events, Lord SALISBURY'S diplomacy has been conspicuously unsuccessful.</p>
        <p>Now that Africa has been almost Developing entirely appropriated by European Trade. nations the question of "develop- ing trade" is being very seriously considered. It is estimated that the in tropical parts of the Dark Continent there is a population of about eighty millions nearly all of whom are, from the European point or view, sadly in need of clothing. Accord- ing to Sir GEOUGH BADEN-POWELL, M.P., who has read a paper on "The Development of Tropical Africa," before the Royal Colonial Society, two- thirds of these black men are unclothed alto- gether and the remaining one-third are only half- clothed. What is now wanted is the persuading of these natives to wear more clothes and to buy these clothes from English manufacturers. It is declared by some people that England's great mission in Africa is to clothe the half-clothed and half-clothe the unclothed. It is estimated that if this mission were successfully carried out the demand for cotton cloth would far out- run the capacities of all the mills in Lan- cashire and India combined. The dusky Africans are not without the means of buying these im- ported commodities if they care to exert them- selv es. For rubber alone, which is freely produced in Africa, there is a rapidly increasing demand owing to the use it is put to in connection with cycle tyres and electrical appliances. The value of this one product has grown in thirty years from £500,000 to £3,500,000. Foreign commerce is, however, not an unmixed blessing to the African. It has killed some interesting kcal industries. At one portion of the Continent visited by LIVING- STONE the natives carried on a wire-making busi- ness according to an ancient and witnitive pro- cess; now, they find it cheaper to buy Englisk wire than to make their own. In Khaina's country the native joiners used to " sew " their pieces of wood together With leathern thongs ; they new use Settlefold's screws which are sent from Birmingham. The African trade has, it .13- pears, led to the revival of one time-honoured little industry in this country, namely, the pre- paring of flints for muskets. The sale of modern arms of precision to the natives of Africa is pro- hibited by an international agreement, but a brisk trade in the old flint muskets has sprung up and the village of Brandon in Suffolk has benefited in consequence.</p>
