        <p>Sr EDWARD'S DAY SCHOOLS. With the object of reducing a debt on these ssheols, a grand concert is to be provided in the Runcorn Public Hall on Tuesday evening next. The schools are attended by poor children, and it is urged that the indebtedness is due to the fact that free education is given, and the books provided for the scholars. The concert is to commence at 7.30, and a good programme is being arranged. BAND OF HOPE ENTERTAINMENT AT THE PRIMITIVE METHODIST SCHOOL. The members of the Band of Hope at the Primitive Methodist Sunday school provided an entertainmt.nt on Thursday evening. Mr. Hill presided and there was a good attendance. The programme was as follows : Recitation, A. Shingler ; violin solo, H. Cottera.ll ; recita- tion, L. Hitchin ; song, Mr. Richardsoe ; violin solos, Mr. J. Stubbs; recitation' J. Sayle ; songs, Miss Wilson, Miss Davies, and Miss Hitchin ; and recitation, A. Sayle. RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPERANCE ASSOCIATION. On Saturday evening, another concert was elven in connection with the above association, in the Brunner Guild Hall. There was a crowded attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. Leathwood. Songs were given by Mies Pope and Simkin, Messrs. Parry, Leach inchester), and A. Povah ; duets by Misses Herren and Pritchard, Simkin and Taylor, and Miss Carter and Mr. Morris. Recitations were given by Miss Nellie Edwards, Messrs. Hulse, Page, and Flarrop. CHOIR TEA MEETING AT Ruxe The annual tea meeting in connection with Camden Wesleyan Chapel ckoir was held on Monday evening, and was well attended. Mr. P. Speakman, J.P. C.C., piesided over the sulssequent proceed. ASdresses were delivered by the Chairman, also the Ray. T. lirackenbury and Mr. A. Harrison. So--7st were nicely rendered by Misses Emily Bazley, Davies, Wood /yard and Nellie Temperley • Messrs. John Bazley, R. Davies and W. Walker. Mr. Samuel Littler contributed a read- ing. Games were afterwards indulged in, and a most pleasant evening was spent. MAIgNER'S CHURCH MISSION, RUNCORN. At the we ekly entertainment in this mission on Mon- day evening' the chair was occupied by Mr. George Robinson. The programme included an organ recital by Mr. Gardner; songs by Miss Turner, Miss Douglas, Miss A. Bill, Miss M. Hill, Mr. B. Hill, Mr. J. Williamson.and Mr. F. Timmins. Mr. T. Sweetrnan contributed a read- ing, and a nautical duet was sung by Mr. and Miss A. Hill. There was also a comic sketch entitled "Easy Shaving," taken part in by Messrs. B. Hill, T. Sweet- man, W. Horton, J. Williamson, and J. Hill. Aa enjoy- able evening was spent. SONS OP TEMPERANCE, RUNCORN. At the ordinary meeting on Saturday night, in the Greenway-read Sunday School, under the presidency of Mr. Thomas Heesom, an interesting meeting was held, when a conversation took place on the good of the order. It was stated that Mr. Thomas J. Holt, G.W.P., and Mr. Thomas Heesom, G.W.T., had recently paid a fraternal visit to the Weston division, and had each aderessed the members. Mr. Heesnin offered two good books to any two members of the juvenile order for obtaining the largest number of members during the next six months. At the present time they have a good membership,and seem to be doing well. The importance of this branch of the work cannot he ever-estimated, as the numbers of the adult orders of Weston are consider- ably augurnented from these sources. DEATH OF A FORMER LOCAL POLICE SERGEANT. About 7.30 on Sunday night, there passed away. Mr. Richard Billiegton, at the residence of his son-in-law, Mr. Ball. ot 41, High-street, Runcorn. The deceased who was well-known in 8110,301% and district was 61 years of age. He was highly respected and had been associated with the Cheshire constabulary for about 17 years. Ile commenced duty at Chester, whence he came , to Weston Paint, was subsequently removed to Halton, and afterwards was stationed in Runcorn for about 12 years. He was promoted to the position ef Sergeant. Through failing health he was compelled to resign his pest, and was the recipient of a grant. Since then he bad assisted Mr. Ball in his business as fruiterer. The deceased was associated with Greenway-road Primitive Methodist Chapel, where he was a regular attendant when health permitted. The funeral took place on Wed- nesday afternoen. SEAMEN'S INSTITUTE, RUNCORN. There was a large attendance at a concert given in the above-mentioned Institute on Tuesday evening with the object of rendering financial aid to the Distressed Sea- men's Fund. The chair was occupied by Mr. Shaw (missionary), who made a few remarks. Pianofo •te duets were performed by Messrs. Price and Foster, and vocal selections were given by Miss M. Hill. Miss Nellie Dnuglas,Miss Foster, Mr. Ashton Wood, Mr. Ben Hill,Mr. Fl.Darlingion,Maater Cooper, and the Brothers McQuirk. Miss Nellie Douglas was encored for her sweet rendition of the songs "I'll give the world to know," and "Selected." The humorous songs were decidedly popu- lar with the audience,and encores were demanded from the Bros. McQuirk, Mr. Ashton Wood, and Mr. H. Dar- Ington. A recitation was given by Mr. G. Page. Tor NAND BROOCHBS, soli silverd name brooches 100. (any name) Government stamped. Presents or Articles for yourself, you may visit DRBIDIT'S, 10, CHURCH-MIMI'. LIVERPOOL, where yea can get hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery, Blectro-Plate. Cutlery. Purses, Pocket Knives, etc.. at wholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chron- graph watch. 6s. 11d.. etc.), on irge 7 f this Papsr. •Ad.d A STUDY OF " CARLTLII." At a meeting of the Bethesda Literary and Social Class on Tuesday evening, the Rev. J. S. Simpson continued his studies in English literature, and dealt with Thomas Carlyle. Be spoke of him as a man who had made his mark in English literature, one of the strongest men.and one of the most misunderstood men of English letters. He was a deeply religious man, thought not after the I orthodox type. He broke away from beliefs in the super- I natural, believing that life itself was a perpetual mir- acle. He was scathing in his denunciation of all sell- gous shams, and had no sympathy for atheists. He might be termed a Calvinist ' without the theology of Calvin. The lecturer recommended his hearers who were unacquainted with Carlyle's works, to commence first with" Heroes and hero worship" where he showed the history of the wool to be a history of great men. The lecture was a most interesting and instructive one.</p>
        <p>POLICX CANIS AT RUNCORN. At RtInCOTI2 Police Court on Thursday morning,before Mr. F. .J. Norman (chairman), • well-known character to the police named Catherine Bowden, was charged with sleeping in the open air in Mersey-t., at 2.30 that morning, and also with being unable to give a satisfac- tory account of herself.—The prisoner only completed a term of imprisonment on Monday. ?.C. Glegory proved the case, and the Chairman in sentencing her to a month's imprisonment, said be wished she would go somewhere else. There was a very black list against her, and she might give someone else the benefit of her presence.—Superintendent Okell remarked that the pri- soner had similar advice given to her on the last occa- sion she was before the Beach.—The Chairman asked whether her mental condition could be inquired into at Knutsford. He did not think she was responsible for her actions.—Superintendent Okell said he would write to the authorities.—lnspcctor Hicks said he did not think the woman had had a meal since she came out of Knutsford gaol on Monday. The same night she was wandering about the streets pull- ing up the grids. Thomas Gilmour and Thomas Edward Davies were chargei with sleeping in an outbuilding in Waterloo-road early that morning without visible means of subsistence.—P.C. Gregory found the prisoner asleep in " Dukes" stables.—Gil- mour said lie waa unable to get any work, and Davies said he had lodgings at 47, Rutland-street. but did not reach there.—The Chairman said they could not do with people hanging about in that manner. They would be di missed on this occasion, but he advised them to he very careful. Drink was at the bottom of the whale , thing.</p>
        <p>THE THEFT OF BRASS AT RUNCORN. On Saturday morning at Runcorn Police Court before Mr. James Handley and Mr. E. Imison, three youths named Samuel Dutton. Mason-St.; John Gardner. Heath-road ; and Peter Furnival. Rock M.itint, Heath. road, were again brought up on a charge of having stolen a gliantity of brass fittings from the Old Qoay Works of the United Alkali Co.,and the adjoining works of the Hull and Liverpool Red Oxide Paint Co.—William Jameson, of St. Helens, engineer for the Red Oxide Co., , stated that an entrance had been obtained into their works by breaking the padlocks of the door. The ma- chinery was very valuable, and a number of pedestal brasses, syphon cups, taps, and valuable lubricators had been smashed off by a crowbar or something of that kind. The articles thken away would not be of much value to anyone else, but the damage to the machinery was between £6O and £7O. Some parts of the machin- ery would have to be renewed before it. could be worked again.—VVPliam Houghton and Thomas Darbyshire spoke to seeing the prisoners near the works with sticks. P.S. Lee stated that the brasses produced fitted broken parts of the machinery. When charged at the Police Station,Dutton said he got the brass from a man named Adams at Eastham locks last Wednesday, and they were going to try and sell it at Mary Scott's. Gardner said to him" Danny tha' might as well speak the truth and tell all about it." Gardner then made a statement which was taken down in writing by P.S. Gill. He said Furnival had done as much as they had, and must ' suffer as much as they had to do.—P.C. Gregory gave , corroborative evidenct. He stated that Gardner told him he knew where P mne stuff was hidden by Furnival and Dutton, and also where Furnival had taken some of the brasses from. Witness afterwards charged the three prisoners together, Gardner said " I had only about fiths , I threw it into the Big Pool " ; Furnival stoted that he had one little tap and four little screws. about 31b. weight. He threw it into the Old Quay " Cut."—Witness stated that he had visited all th:-. marine st-Jres in floncorn. Widnes and Warrington. and could find no trace of the brass.—l'risoners pleaded not guilty. Dutton and Furnival, who had been previousl7 convicted for theft were sentenced to a month's irn- orisoninent with hard labour, and Gardner for 14 day's hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>EX A MINER SATITRII AY, MARCH 14.. 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EX A MINER SATITRII AY, MARCH 14.. 1896.</p>
        <p>THE ASSISTANT OVERSEERSHIP OF RUNCORN. The Local Government Beard have granted an order to the Runcorn Urban Cmincil, empowering them in future to have the appohatment of assistant overseers. This order will not, of course, affect the present assist- ant overseer. AN HONOURED RUNCORNIAN IN QuHmisLAND• The disti lotion of being created a magistrate has fallen to the lot of Mr. Edmund Finley, Toe Glen, Fitton, for the Toowoomba and neighbouring districts, hi Queens- land. The gentleman in question before going abroad rekidea in !dough land-lane,Hig her Runcorn. He was for some time manager for the late Cheshire Amalgamated Salt Works Co., Limited, of Winsford, Liverpocl, and Runcorn. LANTERN SERVICE AT ST. MICHAEL'S CHURCH. Following the precedent of previous years. a lantern service was held in St. Michael's Church en Thursday, evening. The pictures were, for tke most part, illustra- tive of the Passion of Christianity, and had been taken from the paintings of great artistes in various parts of Europe. Toe Rev. H. C. Moyle conducted the service, and the lantern was successfally manipulated by Mr. Thomas Bowyer. A piece of sacred music entitled " Is it nothing to you ?" was interpreted by the choir under the condactorship of Mr. C. Pownall. The organist was Mr.Timmins. The service which was a most impressive one, was largely attended, and a satisfactory collection was realised. THE BRIDGEWATER CANALS UNDERTAKINGS (MAN.. CHESTER SHIP CANAL COMPANY. The annual staff dinner of the Bridgewater Canals Undertakings (Liverpcol branch of the Manchester Ship Canal Comp.day) was held on Saturday evening, at the Bee Hotel, Liverpool. The chair was occupied by Mr. W. H. Collier (manager), who was supported by M,-!ssrs. W. D. MlCay (Liverpool agent), J. Meadowcroft (Run- e-rn agent). F. Wiswall (Bridgewater Canals engineer), T. Evans (Warrington agent), Harry Roddington (the Andertou Company, Stoke,n-Trent), Winstanley (C. F. Faulkner and Co.) Marsden (Pacific Steam Navigation Company), and others. The toasts included "The Manchester Ship Canal," and" The Bridgewater Canals Undertakings."—Mr. Collier briefly reviewed the his- tory of canals in England, and, alluding to the Bridge- water Canal, paid a tribute to the memory of the great duke hose life was loyally devoted to the accomplish- ment of an enterprise which, in its day, had contributed not a little to revolutionize the carrying trade of Eng- land.—The next toast, that of "The Manager," was proposed by Mr. W. D. Wilay (the Liverpool agent). As a souvenir of the occasion, Mr. M,Kay presented the manager with a silver cigar case bearing a suitable in- scription.—Mr. Collier expressed himself deeply touched by this quite unexpected mark of kindness, and assured the members of his staff that he would al' ays look back with great pleas ire and delight to the event which had called it forth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>RUNCORN ANT)</head>
        <p>RUNCORN ANT)</p>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT</p>
        <p>ItIINCOItN.</p>
        <p>BY OUR RUNCORN CORRIMPONDIANT. It is perhaps rather premature tsi speak of th forthcoming election of urban couacillors for Run- corn,aud to ask whether there is going to be a con- tested election. Rumours are prevalent that two of our councillors are thinking of retiring, and if they do so,:it is very probable that a contest will happen in one or two wards. The rateptAters ratty yet get an opportunity of expressing their opinion on the first year's work of the newly-formed Cmin- 1 cil. The trade depression which exists may be considered to be against an all round fight,but there are certainly matters which should form debatable topics if a contest should take place. For instance, there is the Council's position in relation to the Ship Canal Company,and also the bungling manner in which the local authority have gone about their work with regard to the proposed electric lighting of Rancorn.</p>
        <p>I A SERIOUS CHARGE AGAINST A RUN-</p>
        <p>Mbcrtiament not</p>
        <p>DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTION.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD. BAIILBSTOWS.</p>
        <p>LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— As my term of office expires with the present month, I beg most respect!ully to offer myself for re-election, having • been promised support by a number of influential ratepayers irrespc3ttv.. of parties or creeds. I trust that my services in the past as your representatlws will entitle me to your generous support. If again elected (for the fourth time), I will d 3 my utmost,- to deserve your confidence.</p>
        <p>I remain. Your obedient Servant. E. T. EVANS.</p>
