      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>Of the Parish Council elections, the majority of the townships in the vicinity of Runcorn have de- cided upon their representatives at the parish meet- ings, without the expense of having a poll. There were some rather unexpected occurrences too. For instance, at Halton there were thirteen candidates nominated for the twelve seats on the Council. The Vicar, who by the bye was chairman of the previous council, magnanimously came forward and with- drew his name from the list of candidates. Thi., action of the Rev. G. D. Wray has made a contest unneces- ary. During the career of the previous Council Mr. Wray did geod work as chairman, and considering the composition of the new body, 1 should not be surprised if advantage were taken of the oppor- tunity to elect an outsider for chairman, and if the position were offered to the rev. gentleman.</p>
        <p>At the Weston meeting the most surprising fea- ture of the proceedings was in connection with the voting by the show of hands, when the Vicar of Runcorn who was the chairman of the first council, was decisively defeated. Only one supporter for the Vicar was to be found—at least only one had the courage to hold up his hand. The vote has been challenged, and the electors of Weston will pro- bably have a further opportunity of deciding upon their representatives, and on this occasion, through the ballot box. There will be no contest in the Weaver ward, for the five members of the Council were decided upon at the meeting on Monday. I:. happened that there was a tie in the number ot votes recorded for the fifth and sixth candidates, and singular to say the Chairman's casting vote was agreed to without a poll being demanded.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Tonic Solfa choir gave their last cpncert of the season on Wednesday, and it is satisfactory to record,, that despite the depressed condition of trade, which, alas, still coutinues,they will finish the season, financially, in a tar more prosperous condition than, as yet, it has been the privilege of the choir to enjoy. But apart trom the pecuniary point of view, the members and conduc• tor alike are to be congratulated upon the solid progress which has been made. Their performance on Wednesday was most creditable, and are long they may expect to be in a position to add to the prizes which they have already gained. A former member of the choir, Miss Minnie Curtis, renewed her acquaintance with it on W ednesday in a new role, that of a leading artiste. Though her resi- dence in the town was but for a few years, she lett many friends and well-wishers for her future success.</p>
        <p>The magistrates at Runcorn Petty Sessions were en. gaged for some considerable time in bearing a case in which Peter Deakin, Ashbridge-St., foreman carpenter, was summoned by Annie Woodrow,wife of James Wood- row, licenge of the Millstone beerhouse, on a charge of indecently assaulting her. A great deal of interest was taken in the case and the court was crowded. Mr. H. Reynolds was the presiding magistrate, with Mr. F. J. Norman. Mr. Burton, solicitor, prosecuted on behalf of the plaintiff, and Mr. A. Browne, solicitor, Warring- ton, defended. In opening the cue, Mr. Burton stated that about 8 p.m., on Tuesday, the 25;h Feb., the complainant was alone in the Millstone feu. The defendant came there and she supplied him with drink. Be took it in the kit- chen and remained some little time in the house. When he got up, he caught hold of Mrs. Woodrow, pulled her on his knee, and behaved indecently to her. She struggled and got away from him, and went to the front door for assistance. She could see no one, and returned to the kitchen. Defendant was still there, and she re- quested him to leave. He then made some remark that she must. forgive him, and that he regretted what be had done. He continued to remain in the house, and when she was going down the cellar to bring some coals he followed her. A struggle ensued, and this continued until they reached the taproom. The complainant screamed, and she at length got away and ran into the street. In the struggle her hand was cut and it bled. Her husband was engaged at the theatre, and returned home generally about eleven o'clock. He would la. able to tell the condition of his wife on the night in question when he returned. On I the following morning the complainant's husband went I to see the defendant, with the result that at night he came to the complainant's house to apoloeise for what had occurred the night previous. They then told him that it was too late and that proceedings had been commenced. Annie Woodrow, the complainant corroborated Mr. Burton's statement. Deakin said to her " Will yau tot- give me. What I mean is, not to make it public by tell- ing the neighbours. I have a quid or two." She told him he must leave as she expected her husband. He re- plied "lie won't be here until after eleven ; I know his time." She then went down the cellar to fetch some coal and she heard the defendant coming after her. She rushed back with the shovel in her hand and pushed him back. She ordered him out but he would not go, and she afterwards got hold of his wrists. She dragged him between the taproom and the lobby. He tried to throw her down but she was too strong for him. Sue screamed, and said she would have to break the glass door if he did not desist. He said " you are letting the neighbours know." He released her and she went to the front door,and told him that she should tell her husband when he came. The defendant then swore at her, and went away.—The complainant was subjected to a lung cross-examination. She had been married 2.3 years. She had been separated from her husband in conse- quence of " a bit of temper." She was away from her husband about four years, and during that time she had lived in :Crewe and Nantwich. Her husband allowed her 21. a week. She was convicted at Nantwich for having "had a drop of beer." She denied that she had been lucked up three times,and that it was for immoral conduct. The defendant was in the beerhouse from 8 to 10.25. He had two bottles of beer. She did not know the name of the gentleman who visited the house whilst Mr. Deakin was there. She only know that he lived in Runcorn. No one came to her assistance when she screamed although there were houses in the district. She had never made any charged against other men. About twelve or thirteen young men came to her house on Christmas Eve. They were drunk. She did not invite theta in to have a cup of tea ; they rushed in the house. Her husband did nat make a charge against them. She did not think they had to go to Mr. Burton, selicitor. Oa Saturday eight week her husbaed did give her a thrashing, but it was not for doing any- thing wrong. She refused to answer why he beat her. She denied being guilty of improper conduct recently at a neighbaur'a house, or admitting that her husband was expecting a man azainst whom she had made a charge, paying him £5 or £lO. She refused to say why, er hus- band thrashed her. She did not know that Dr. McKenzie sent for the police in consequence of her screams. Her husband did not use a stick, he gave a "tip" on the face with his open hand. Her object in taking those proceedings was not to obtain a few "quids." She did nA say she would have his " quids" out of him. She knew that the defendant was a respectable man. James Woodrow, money taker at the Theatre, stated that he generally got home between ten and eleven at night. On Tuesday night, the 25th February,just before he got to his house he saw the defendant. Be didn't speak to him. The doors of his house were locked, and hia wife opened the door. She was ceying,and her hand was bleeding. She told him what had occurre He went to the defendant's house at noon the following day, and .asked him what was the cause of him (defendant) assaulting witness's wife. He said he was very sorry and was coming to apologise. He said he must have been ma' to go to the house, and wished to know if it could not be settled somehow. Witness said he did not think she would do so, and de- fendant then offered to go down and see her, as he did not want to make it public. Defendant went to wit- ness's house and apnloeised, but witness told him he would have to see Mr. Burton.—ln answer to questions by Mr. Browne, witness said he was in the police force for 14 years. He left it on account of disagreements between himself and wife. They were with regard to family matters, but he declined to give any particulars concerning them. Defendant did not tell him that the statements were untrue, and that he would come down to the house and face it out. He was in the hospital when the payment of 2s. a week was made against him for his wite's maintenance The theatre jlb was only a matter of Ba. a week. He paid the money to his wife oolong as he could. He did not thrasii his wife but pushed her. She was very excitable, and very quick y screamed. (Leughter.) He had not taken proceeding with the object of getting money Int of people. He admitted that he had made a charge against Dr. Mc- Kenzie, but had spologised tor doine so. Mr. Browne characterised the case As one that was very easily trumped up, but very difficult to disprove. He hoped the Bench would take jot, consideration the characters of the two parties. Mr. Browne reviewed some of the evidence, and said his client had declined to try to effect a settlement or apologise, for he asserted that: he had (bile nothing wrong whatever. They had no reason to apologise. Mrs. Owen was called and was proceeding to speak of the complaintant's conduct on Friday evening week and as to certain admissions, when Mr. Burtoa inter- posed, and objected to the evidence. The Clerk con- sidered such evidence illegal. The Defendant said he was a joiner employed by the Ship Canal Company, and he went into the complain- ant's house on the date named shortly before nine o'clock and left it twenty minutes to ten. He saw a man come int:. the house h t he did'ut know who he was. He denied the allegatio. s of the complainant, and stated that he did not in any way touch her. Nothing of an improper nature took place between them. Oe the fol- lowing day Woedrow came to see him, and told him to pet his coat on. Woodrow asked him whether he knew what he wanted him (witooes) for. Witness said "Ne." Woodrow then said that witness had insulted his wife. Witness replied that he was very sorry Woodrow had brought a charge like that against him. He stated that he would go and sec Mrs. Woodrow about it. He did so, and Woodrow then said his wife had nothing to do with it. The best thing he could do wa ti go and square it with Mr. Burton. Witness then said if that was the game he was leaving. He did not get the summons until the folio log Saturday. The magistrates dismissed the case, a decision ta hich was received with loud applause in court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUNCORN PRIZE CHOIR CONCERT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PRIZE CHOIR CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Tonic Sulfa Prize Choir brought its season's programme of concerts to a fitting close on Wednesday evening when a grand performance of Handel's *• Joshua" was given in the Public Hall. The attendance was most encouraging, the hall being well filled. Allusion has already been made in these columns to the oratorio itself, and it now remains to be said that its interpretation on Wed- nesday was both adequate, and in the highest de- gree creditable, to both the performers and the con- ductor. The choir consisted of about: one hundred voices under the leadership of Mr. Holford, and the instrumental selections were performed by a band of experienced instrumentalists. The prin- cipal vocalists engaged for the occasion were Mr. Tom Child (tenor) representing Joshua, leader of the Israelites; Mr. A. S. Kinnel (bass) Caleb, captain of the tribe of Judah Madame Goodall (soprano) Achsah, daughter of Caleb ; and Miss M. Cullis (contralto) as ()Wilk'', a youthful warrior betrothed to Achsah—a very capable quartet. Prior to the performance proper, as a mark of respect to the late Sir Joseph Barnhy, who was one of the vice presidents of the society, the band played Hande?a "Dead March" from "Saul," and the choir followed with the singing of the hymn, commencing "Sleep thy last sleep "to music composed by the de- ceased musician. Although Mr. Child was indisposed he sang well, and Mr. A. S. Kinnell maintained the rtputa- tiou he has gained as a good bass sluger. Of the two female voices, Madame Goodall was the best, and she well deserved the encore awarded her tor the sparkling vivacity with which she rendered the air" Oh, had I Jubal's lyre." Another beautiful air was that in which there was the double accompaniment of violin and violoncello by Mr. J. W. Collinear' and Mr 3. G. Ter Poorten respectively. Special interest was centered in the performances of Miss Minnie Cullis in-as-much as she was a few years ago, during the construction ef the Ship Canal when her father was en- gaged as one of the engineers,a resident in Runcorn,and became a member of the Runcorn Prat, Choir. She is the possessor of a rich contralto voice,and gives promise of becoming a concert singer far abose the average. Of ' the concerted music the charming duet " Our limpid streams with freedom 1714 w " calls for special mention. It was, nowever,in the choruses that the success of the concert largely lay, and it must be admitted that the choir gained fresh laurels on Wednesday night. Each item was given in a manner which left nothing robe de- sired,and called fourth the unstinted praise of the audi- ence. There was no lack of strength or preci- sion, and the members are to be congra- tulated upon the progress which they have made. The orchestra also ably coped with the demands which the oratorio makes upon them. For their pertormance of the overture the instrumentalists won warm applause; and an exceedingly N% ell ekeouted piece was the "March " which precedes the fall of the walls of Jericho. he performers were also successful in the muted pas- sages accompanying' the vocalists, and also ill the more onerous cikoruses. Mr. Hollord, to whose perseverance ro,cli of the present high positim oh the choir is due. wieloed the baton throuahout the evening,at what proved a most interesting concert, and one Gla:, has teen remarked upn as being the most successful of the I season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>CHILD FOUND IN AN ASHPIr AT</head>
        <p>A CHILD FOUND IN AN ASHPIr AT</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Frodsham Police Court on Monday afternoon, by Mr. libbits, deputy coroner, on thv body of a newly-born male child found in an ashpit at Newton-)y-Frodsham, on Friday night. Superinten- dent Johnson was present, and Mr. F. A. Lake,solicitor, Runcorn, watched the case on behalf of the Bibby family.—Sarah Bibby, single woman, cook for Miss Dutton, of Newton Cottage, said her sister Mary acted as housemaid at the same residence. She continued to perform her work urtil Friday at noon, when she com- plained of being unwell. Later in tho afternoon she went outside,and from what subsequently transpired her mis- tress sent for her mother.—Emma Bibby, wife of Wil- liam Bibby, Church-it., Frodsham, spoke to going to Newton Cottage, and seeing her dativ:hter Mary. aged 21 years. In consequence of what she sail she went to the ashpit and saw a newly-born child. Her daughter came to see her every other week. She was at home on the Ist March. Evidence was given by F.C.Frice and Dr. Selby. The latter stated that in his opinion the child was not born alive, there being no signs nf respiration. He was certain the child was stillborn. It did not seem that the body had been placed there, but was in the spsft where it had fallen. —A verdict in accordance with the medical testimony was recorded.</p>
      </div>
