      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>LIVERPOOL</head>
        <p>LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>UN CORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>During this month ow -third of the ratepayers' repre- sentatives on the Runcorn Urban District Council will retire in consequence of their term of office expiring. The retiring councillors are eligible for re-election, and are as follows :—lleath ward, Mr. .1. Pritchard ; Newtown , ward, Mr. J. Jones ; central ward. Roy. Dr. Gastaldi ; Bridgewater ward, Dr. Mclienzi, ; Mer6ey ward. Mr. R. B. Pocmett • Halton ward, Mr. F. Wiswall. the return- ing officer (Mr. F. A. Lake) has, duriol the week, issued the notices of election. The election day is fixed for Saturday. the 28th day of Marco, and one councillor is to be elected tor each of the six wards into which the town a div ded. Nomination pxpers are to be banded in at the Town tiall,Runcorn, not later than four o'clock on Monday next, the 16th inst. A statement will be issued on Wednesday. giving the names of those nomin- ated, and candidates may withdraw until four o'clock on Friday, the 20th inst. It is stated that twosof the retiring councillors are determined not to seek re-election.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>HELSBY.</head>
        <p>HELSBY.</p>
        <p>SPITIAL SERVICFS.—The annual services in aid of the National Setiools, were held in Sc. Paul's Church,flelsby. on Sunday. The morning service l were conducted by the Rev. 11. E. Blogg, vicar of Overton. Tile evening ser- vices were conducted by the Vicar, the Rev. k.Spafford, who discoursed on the uphringing of children. Both services were well attended, and as a result of the col- lections, £7 163. was handed over to the School Commit- tee. The Oebt on the schools has of l4te been considerably reduced, but there still remains about £BO to be cleared off. FOOTBLLL.—The return match with Aston Ball was played at Helsby cu Saturday, HeLsby winning by 3 goals to 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>Pie.TOSTI COUNCIL MIMING. —The last meeting of the late Council took place in the Courtroom,Froditham.on Thurs• day evening week, when here were present—Messrs.a. E. Heywood (chairman), G. Rodger, J. Midge, Shore, Harlan.. Barrow, W. Booth, Ringrose, J. M. Williams, P. Booth, Youd, and ;ones (clerk).—Th, Poethpaths Com- mittee reported what they had done with respect to the footpath to Saltport and to the one near to Mr. Har- grave's house, Overton, when it was resolved that the committee have f nil power to do the work without Inither delay. A desultory conversation took place with reference to the forthcoming Parish Council election.</p>
        <p>BANKRUPTCY OF A FRODSTIA3I EX•BBlllassLLPß.—SaTritlel Clarke, residing and carrying on bu a potatosiness. Jsal,r. at Church-street, Frodsham. Cheshire, and lately ey carrying on bu ss sine as a beerhouse Ir,-pper and hper seller (Ychoie3ale and retail), at the Cholmondle Arms, Church-str,et, Frodsham, anti in Main-street, Frudsham, underwent his public examination this (Friday) morn- Court. After a : ing at tha Bankr.upt.ty lengthy hearing the case WAS ailynirued until April 17.</p>
