      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BEFORE TETE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE TETE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>MINOR CASES. WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlDAY.—Before Councillor H. Robert 4 (ehairmln), and Dr</p>
        <p>Gormill.</p>
        <p>OIFFENCFS AGAINST TUE FACTORY ACT.—The Warrington and District Mineral Water and Bottling Company Limited, Crown-street. were charged with having on the 7th Febru- ary as occm_lers of a factory employed Charles Henry Horra- bin, under 16 years of age, for more th•m seven days without obtaining a certificate —Mr. Sinnett, the manager, admitted the offence.—Ur. J . Roger', the faetory inspector, stated that his assistant visited the factory on the 7th February and found a boy of 13, working there. He had been five weeks without cer,ificate. The particulars were not entered in the prescribed register.—Mr. Sinnett said that the boy was taken on as his father was dead and for the same reason he had granted the lad's mottle,. some indulgence in getting Ho cer- tificate. She had delayed it because the boy was going to leave.—A fine of is. and 5s mats was imposed. Tia 3 Firth Mistress Company. Friars Green. were charge .t with havinx on the 12th February employed William Craik, a young person, later than six o'clock p.m. they were also charged with like- wise emploiing Thomas Garnett, and Robert Oswald—Mr. Rogers said he himself found the boys workin.• and !earned that they had commenced at 6 a.m. and were going on to 8,30 p.m.. two hours and a half overtime. It was illegal to employ them more than 12 hours.—Mr. Taylor, the manager of the Company, said the boys were doing work which could notbe done before 8 cielnek.—A fine of 10s and costs in the first ease, and of the costs in the other two, was inflicted. Wit Fut DAMAGE. Catherine Richardson. 6 Ashton-street. was charged with wilfully breaking a window the property of Bridget Ganlev. 18 Ashton-street, on the 12th e Ornery.— Mrs. Hanley stated her case and defendant who had far from the best of characters was fined ss. and costs or 14 days hard labour in defaelt. A MELANCEIOLY CANC.—Thomas Wright. an Intelligent and respectable-looking man living at 41 Orford-street.was charged witla assaulting and beating Mary Jane Wright his wife, on the 3rd March.—Mrs. Wright gala that on Tuesday because she would not give defendant some money be struck her about the head with his fist. She screamed an her daughter came to her.—Annie Wright.aged 13 supported her mother's statement, and said she did not know what caused the as- eault.—Defendant alleged that complainant took a chair to him and called him foul names.—He vms fined las. and amts.—Mrs. Wright. as her husband WAS now convicted of an aggravated assault applied for a judicial separation with 15s. a week. Defendant she said could earn as a joiner £2 a week. —The Chic Constable said that, defend Ant had hew) bound over in 1893 for assaulting his wife. For the last 20 years he had misbehaved himself, and be believed that complainant had lived a miserable life for a num ter of years. Defendant Nye, respectably connected and everything had be. n &amp;me to Induce him to reform —The Bench granted the separatUn and made an order upon defendant to pay his wife 12s. 6d. a week. TEMPORARY AtITHORTTY.—George Andrews was granted temporary authority to sell at the " Boar's Head." Town Hill In lieu of William Morton.</p>
        <p>SATIIRDAT.—Before Messrs. lbsrgess, Chandley.and Geddes. DBuNK AND DISORDERLY.--John Kelly was fined ss. end costs, or, In default, seven days imprisonment, for having been guilty of d-unkenness and disor&amp;rly behaviour in Stoney-yard, on the previous nignt. P.O.Bradley proved the</p>
        <p>-case. TliErr FROM A PAWNBIZOKER'A SHOP.—Bridget Claifey, of pump-yard. off School-it., was charged with having stolen a pair of boy's trowel's and seven yards of cloth. value 10s., from the shop of Rdgar V. Morton. on.vonbroke,, St.James's- st.. on the 2nd inst.—The Chits! Constable stated that on the afternoon of the day in question the articlei were seen safe Oa a shelf in the shop by one of Mr. Morton's assistants named William Ashton. Prisoner then entered the allso and when she left the articles were mi.sed. Information -was given to the police, and it wls ascertained that the trousers lad been pawned at the Tower pawnshop, Bthige- et. When Detective-Servant Monaghan anprehended pri- soner she said she had nothing to aay.—Detectiye-R meant Monaghan stated that prisoner had a good hnsbAnd, and she was not in want .4 snything.—She wAs fined 10s. and c.)sts or, in default 14 days' imprisonment. TdOttPs.Y.— Before the Mayor (Counc;lioe Fairelongh). in the chair. Alderman Harrison, and Messrs. Monks and</p>
        <p>Hayward. VAGRANCY. —William Holden. of Friars'-green, a profes- rtonalbeggar. was committed ts prlson fora month with hard labour for begging on Saturday night in Henry-street. P.C. Bradley deposed to arresting prisoner, who had W., and a 'parcel of meat and bread in his nossassino when s.ar—hed ASSAULT Olf POLICE.—Peter Maloney. of 14. Dial- court, was cha-ged with having assaulted P.C. Corbett ashilst In the execution of his duty on Seturd.av night, in Whittle- street.—P.C. Corbett stated that at ten &amp;ohs&amp; ontiatordav night he was marching down Buttermarket -street in company .with other constables, when prisoner and another man tried to break through the ranks. Witness pushed them away and the two men then went to the Police Station and made a complaint. Inspector Cox. who WAS in the office, ordered them out, but they refused to leave. Witness th.n ejected them, hut they waited outside for him. When he (witness) left the office they followed him, and when in Whittle-st., they commenr.•ed to "Jostle " him. Witness pushed prisoner's Companion by the shoulder where- upon Maloney kicked him in the aide. He th us received a violent blow on the side of the heacthits hemlet being kneoked across the street. When prisoner wee taken into eustodv he became very violent, and assistance had to be obtained.— Michael John Hntght. a County Court bailiff, eortalborated. Prisoner denied the assault, and pleaded that the officer tried to choke hi m.—A fine of £1 and costs Was imposed, or In default one month's imprisonment with hard inborn% • DauttxtrvNves —Annie Skelly, or Grav: A•snie Smith, both of no fixed Rhoda. and James Grady. of 68.1illesmere-st..were proved to have been vuiltv of ilisordErly behaviour. Annie Smith received a month's hard labour,and the other two were tined ss. and cats, AN Ur:tics:Nam Otunv.—John Greenonzli. 31 (Tolborno at., was fined ss. and costs for acting as a c.bdriyer on the 3rd March withort a license.—lnspeetor Taylor, hackney carriage inspector, proved the CASS. UNREGUSTERIM LonnING-HOUSEa.—The following persons were fined for keeping lodgers in their houses on the 29th February, without the houses being registered as lodging- houses Paines, 19 Ilrwood-st.. 5g.; James Diclren- son. 4 Chapel-yard, John-st , 5.. ' • William Greenway, 22 Firwoo(l-st., 2s. 6d.; Peter Tatiock.l Siewart's-yard, Turner- at., ss. TUF..SDAY.—Pefore Mr. R. Garnett (chairman), and Mr. S. . . .</p>
