        <p>Anwr DrirPTtoN.—Dunnan-,Gorrion W* chryø4 with being a deserter from Her Majesty's G0,11.1n HighlandArs stat;ontd at. Glasgow. since Fehrna-v 1898.—P.C. Bradley 'dated that on Monday morning at 10 45 he saw def•ridant IT Church•st.. and from his appearance thought he was a deser- ter. He accosted him and defondant admitted the offence He was remanded to Strangcway's gaol. and Bradley was recommended for the usual reward.</p>
        <p>_ THIUDSDAY.—Before Mr. S. Chnndley (chairman). and Alder- m...n Sutton. SCHOOL ATTENDANCE.—The following persons were dealt with for not sendints; their children regularly to &amp;Thom Thomas Chrioaps, 32 Warburton-street, fined 5g.: James Fog. Birchall-atreet, 54. : Cornelius Lennon. 22. Portland. street, 5,.; MOSCS Williams. Back Stamford-street, boy ordered to be birched ; Elizabeth Jane Powell, 12. Naylor- etre; t, adjourned for a month ; Joseph Lightfoot. Wakefield- street . ss. ; Hannah Sixsmith, Glasshouse now. 2i. 6.1. A TICICETLOA TRAVELLER —lrmo Brad.haw. of Crewe, was charg with ridingon the L. and N.-W. Railway from ed. Crewe to Warrington without having paid his fare, on Friday the 16th inst.—Defendant said he knew nothing about it. —The Chief Constable mentioaed that defend- ant was brouoht np on Saturday morning suffering from temporary mania and VMS unable to plead. Since then he had been In the workhouse hospital and we' now all right' lie believed that it wea true that defendant did not remem- ber the offence.—Detective Wallace,in the employ of the rail- way company. stated that. prisoner alighted from the 11 5 a.m. trim n from Crewe on the previous day. and was allowed ti leave the stAtion on giving what they thouzlit was a satis- factory name and address. About two o'clock the tame after- noon prisoner returned to the station,and asked to be allowed</p>
