        <p>to bevel to Blackpool. He was then under the Influence of drink. and gave another name and address. He was then taken into cu,tody.—The Chief Con,table added thV.. (Wend• ant h,d been in the Pre,s.wich Asylum for attemoting to cut his throat.—A tine rif ss. was itn9osed and defelathint was els) oroered to pay I.lie 24. ail Way tare. TEMPORIXY A.urnonrry.—Charles Waltt iker was granted tempo.ary sothority to sell at the Mersey Vaults in lieu of 'Hannah jones.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SERMONS.</p>
        <p>MoxDAY.--Before Mr. Reynolds (chairman) and Mr. F. J. Noeman.</p>
        <p>THE SALE OF lIIAEHALfs —Matthew Thomas, grocer. Balfour-street, was summoned at the Jest •nce of Albert., Timinis, insp,,ctei of weight and measures for the Ches,e- Counts' Council for having on the Bth of Feurnary sold bite.' nol; or the sub Stance and qualil,y demitoild ; and furthee with selling margarine wideli was in .u. w:•appe • wi..hout the w0.,1 " Morgarine" marked thereon:—Mr. Allen solicitor, defethie... John Brace,ird:e. ,!s.;is'.int to .Albert Timmins, inspector. of weights ,and meaAnres.satc.d. that lie visit..d ;he deenibant's shop on the date .name awl in answer to ills inqulev as to the price of his butter, the defend ,nt. said Is. 2.1. and Is. per ponnd. Witness toted them, and borght a onund at a Is. Defendant got it f.kim a parcel and .said oe did not know what it W.l3.—Croc-•examined tr, Mr. Allen, witness vid he hat bought butler at is. per pound•—Allie!'.. Tim mis 5:•.,1.r ! that he met the last vr:tness at the door Of Thomas's bhop, and he Obi the del that is %.:13 his intention to submit the eainple to the analyst., and oXered to divide the samole.. Defendant then said .1 Oh, i ;s a mixte,e." Witrie,a asked him how it was he didn't pet it in a rr,r%arnie paper, and he • replied that he .had none.' He forva,ticd a sample to Dr. Carter Bell, who 'had certilkd ! that it was not a sample of butler, and con;.a.i.,ea 100 nee cent. of margavine.—Fw the defence, Mr. Allen sinted that he . had advis'd the defenilant to. plead uilty to the charge that ho sold:the article without a label, arid he tbouehi: theie IVO ssts would be satisfied that h. was: a sli?ht offenea. He wooh. sugge•t to the inspectoe tbai, he would withdraw that ctiv...•.e. --Mr. 'C,mmins• sold he would be willing to do so on p.y.aeo, of costs. This was agr-ed tO. With reg-rd to the othe- cluzge, Mr. Allen polnied out that the margarine was not, exposed for sale. The parcel was seat to the 6hop without being marked at all, and when he undid the parcel he was no, aware wither It Wit 3 marg,e•ne, or r He told Beace- girdle who bought the butte, that he did not know what' 4 was. He (Mr. Allen) thounnt i was a ease where a small fine would be sufficient.—Jane Bro ...Ihurst, 1, Brouk-sitr,!el.,,g•or.e.,•. was charged with similar ,offerces ce the same date.—Mr. Lake, solicitor. defenOed.—John Br•celirdle s' i.e I thi. on the 6th February he went into the defendant's sluip and aske I for a pound of bu,,it r. The son the terend....nt's sad to his Oster who was in the s,tou " (live bin" a pound of I,lie best." Witness went to anolier • un.,er where iheve -.sass a dish, the Iv /cle Mpg Libelled Is oud anahe- ia: underlie ti there was prini,ed Excellent."_ The</p>
        <p>woman who served h.rn • w.th a . ponnd, eaid_ it was very nice, and he to. id he, Is. roe -11". Timmins sal,: he told (hem that the siile arras for the nu-nose of analysis and the defendent seid lit, worot ndi a very:good mkt nee. He told her that I:. had been bon.;:ht Ft, The pi.reel was pointed out to him, and i had the arpc.tr.ince of bui ie.. There was no mark of ma g.irine o•ri the dish o. on the wrai4 per. The analysis tha.. it vi:.s not a semoleo7;Jutter bat ot margarine. —Me. Lake con) plained. lisp CF'ii did not d .te the ing-eeien• s. it staL.Al t'•• t it; w"3 100 i.e.!' cent. margarine. That looled Fs it i•;ne ;vete and irredeemably bad. There was nothin.4 in the analyel9 to. say how much blater bee was. His client Felt her posii she had always tried to e.-ry on her business in an iinne,t manner, and she contentled ..hot, she had iiot the *slightest intention to sell anythtng hot whau corn pi.inant for. The rnagistr.d.es &amp;al. with the case as wit.i the p,TV:0.13 one, and imposed a flue of 59. and costs od the semind cha.,ge, and the costs on the 11' t. DRUNK AND D;SOEIDEP.I.7.—Geo-ge Maley. West-c °Ail. Weston, was fined ha. and costs fot being drunk and dis- orderly on the 2nd MA Th arid. Will4am Comins.so.ffillesmere- st., lturcorn, was mulc'..cd in a similar penalty for a liLa offence on the 3rd inst.. DRIVING Wirt:tour T.omas, Whiteside Farm, Sutton, wits summoned to ,y;og on the 27th February at Helton, driven aco •nil •ill with•tut h lying mins aqached the horse, on the 27..11 Febrittry.--P.C. Joett, proved the case. audit fine of 2s. 6d. and osiu w,s iiopoted. • DOG AT T.ARGS:. -G130r40 Dodd. fa • ,ser. Moore, WAS fined is. including costs for aliow:eg his do., to be at large on the 26tilFebruarv. NON-PA.YAIE: 2 or POOR RAI Es.—No rewer than 40 sum- mouse- had been issued by Mr. Suitor' for non.-narrient or poor rates. Seven days were allowed For p-y.nent in a num- ber or cases. and where defendstits did not appetr, tint cases were adjourned for a week.</p>
        <p>BRUTAL•TREATIVENI OF A. BOY.</p>
        <p>PARENTAL AU raORITY ABUSED.</p>
        <p>Ott Monday morning at the Warrington Borough Police Court.before the Mayen (chairman), Mr.F.Monks, Alderman 'Harrison, and Mr. J. W. Hayward Henry Wright, o,FCeen-et., was charged with haying on the 2nd March and during the last six months, unlawfully and wilfully assaulted Henry Wright, his son, under 16 years of age. Inspector M'Atear, N.S.P.C.C., said the defendant was the father of three children at home, and two away. The boy he was charged with illtre Sting was the eldest of those at home, and his age was ten years. On the night of the 2nd of this month in consequence of a com- plaint, he went to defendant's house in company with Sergeant Hollingworth and P.C. Clough. and found the defendant in the kitchen with his housekeeper. a Mrs. Moran. Witness asked where the boy was that had been assaulted and defendant gave no satisfactory reply ; the woman said he was in bed. She brought him down and witness examined him and found him in a terrible state. Ho had bruises on 'he arms, temple, back of the headeand abrasions en the hack. The boy was one mass of wounds,and told him in explanation.that he came home from school about 5.30. His father atiked him why he was not in sooner and he made no reply. Defendant then sent him upstairs and followed, taking with him "this instrumcnt." (The Tnsnector here pro- duced a rope, plaited into nine folds and knot..ed- in several places, the whole about a yard ion* and heavy). Continuing, he said, the defendant thrashed the boy :or about 15 minutes with it. The neighbours went to him and cried shame upon him for his conduct to the boy. Defendant came downstairs and then returned and gave the boy another thrashing, and a third time he went up and beat him unmercifully. He then got three marbles and tried to push them down the lad's throat for the purpose of choking him. If the mag,istraPes from the evidence were convinced that the bay had been cruelly Mused, he, the Inepector, asked that they might main an example of the defendant. Sergeant Hollingworth said that he visitedthe defen. dant with the Inspector, and found the boy in the state described ha' him. He could only add that defenlant was drunk and did not appear to know axActly what he was doing. The boy said his fattier was drunk at the time and 'altogether made a very straightforward tale before his father.</p>
        <p>_ Defendant to witness : Is the rope exactly as you re- ceived it.</p>
        <p>Witneas : So far as I cso see. P.C. Clough said he took possession F&gt;f the rope first, as tho bop said his rather had threatened "to give him some more shout 9 o'clock." It was then in the same condition nil it was that moment. _ .</p>
        <p>- Harry Wright, aged 10,-a "thin and dalieate looking , lad, in answer to the questions by the Inactector. said ' when he went upstairs defendant followed him with the rope. At first he doubled it and si laid on" him with it. He than plaited and knotted it, and charged witness with stealing 61. which he denied. He then thrashed him again. AfterwArds he tried to put the marbles in hia mouth and because he would not open it hi o father threshed him again. When the Inspector came he went with him to the Infirmary anti had his back dressed. After the Insp.ctor had gone he was going to damp the- bandages when his father told him to take them off because the doctors at the Infirmary could not ewe him. He also told him he must not go again. Defendant had thrashed him twice befere. On Friday nights he sometimes kept him in the house all night, all Saturday, and most of Sunday without food and drink. He had not played truant from school. With regard to'the bruises on his head he said his father had seizNi him by the neck and threw him over the bed so that his head banged against the wall. His father had always been in beer when he had locked him up. Mrs. Meran,defendant's housekeeper,said that defend- ant was quite drunk on the day in question. When he went upstairs with the rope she said " Now Wright re- meinb?r what you are going to do. You have a rope and mind what you do with it." He then told her not to in- terfere. She afterwards heard the boy screaming and crying for about a quarter of an hour. She had twice before hid the rope from defendant. Defendant had locked the tiny up and given instructions that ho was not to have fond or drink.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Clarke, 6 Keen-it., living onpesite defendant's house said she could see defendant thrashing the boy throueh the room window. She had sp,kan to him afterwards and said she would report him,snd he defied. her. She saw that the boy was stripped. When his father had gone sha examined him and found h s body terribly marked while " his poor head was all of a mummy." Mrs. Harrison, another neighbour, corroborated.</p>
        <p>Dr. Bowden said on Mfmda.y evening he examined the boy at the Infirmary. He found bruises on both armq There were bruises behind the ear.on the temple,and on the head. Oa the bank there were several big abraded weals with the top skin taken off. The wounds had instrument to cause them. The boy's head looked as if mid a little and he thought the rope was a ;Very likely it had been hurt against the wall.</p>
        <p>Defendant said to the magistrates that since the boy's mother died about 12 months ago he had discoverert that he was a bad lad. He played trnant and spent the change of money he was sent errands with. He had also spent Hs stool money and had also gone to Ohmy's circus with it. Mr. Monks asked Mrs. Moran if the boy had been dis- obedient. She said he had in some things but she did not know that he had spent his school money. Defendant said the rope was not knotted and the boy had told an untruth. He put no knot in it, he simply doubled it.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Menlo;: Who do you suspect has knotted it ? Defendant : I suppose Mr. M'Ateer. Thd In.pector :denied the accmatina and the court aided with him.</p>
        <p>Mr. Monks :Is your idea of parental .authority, that a man in a state of drunkenness has a right to thrash his boy naked with a rope like that for a quarter of an hour.</p>
        <p>Defendant: I should not jike to say that. The Mayor: We are letting you off leniently by giving you three months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>The Inspector said he would see to the children being taken core of.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 14, 1896.</p>
        <p>of uninteresting scrimmages and line outs. Very rarAV inleed was an, onni play witnessed. Oa one occasion Wales intercepted a pass, and transferring it to Harmer that player airnoAt got over. In this half the visitors did most of the pressing' and Houghton saved finely on several occasions. Toward 3 the close. Runcorn played vigorously and penned their cpporients near their line tor rome time, but were unable to br,..ak through. Johnson and Lampriere were the saviours of their side, their kicking being particularly line, whilst Warder was most prominent for the homess.ers. An uninteresting game ended in a vie.tory tor the visitors by one point, the scores being Hull live pohits, Runcorn four points.'</p>
        <p>WIDNES v. ROCHDALE HORNETS.</p>
        <p>I- The return game between Widnrs and Hit:Male I Hornets drew about 2,503 people to the Lewerhouse-lane ground. , Tileflornete won the first game by two tries to nothine.. but S atutelay'a game was practically a gift to Widnes, as the. visitors arrived with only ft urteen men. Howarth mised his train, and Satc.iffe, played half back iu his stead. Widnes, on the other hand, had a strong team. Midgley commeneed operations, and some long kicking end:ne. its favour of the home backs, Widnes were on, the aggressive. The game had only been in progress live ;en:lutes when Drummond dropped a fine goal from the 25 line. The Elornets made progress from the centre kick,but Druminond staved their career. Only for a motnent, however, for Wightinan had passed several Of the backs, anti looked like scoring when Rh. pan overtook him. Bather placed his team once more on the aggressive. amid Brennan and N ,vitt gained ground, bet failed to pass Riga. who was slowing great . form.. Alford took a free kick in a good pesitiam. but only made a poor attempt, and a minute later. he mulled the ball when it looltia odds on a try being scored. On. the opposite wine H. •Peters lost also a line elianee, the play of the three-quarters at this period being poor in- deed. Another couple 0 good epporttieities were-thrown away soon afterwards by Rispan and Drnintnond, and ; then ih'ightman • made the beA individual et-lort of the 1 afternoon. Re made a capital run id ost the-length of I the field, and wae not overtaken bf H. Peters until ' within a few betties of -the line. 'This ed to a petty hot attack on the Widnes fine, tut nothing came of it, and . . the home team held a lead of four points at the inter- val. In the second half Widnes did by far the most of the attacking,but there was little if any cohesion among . the team. Opening after opening presented itself, but they let their opoettonities slip by,although at the same time the Rocedale baeks deserve credit for their good defensive play. The game deteriorated sadly, and the referee had more than once to interfere and caution -players for their roughness. Towards the finish the Hornets made a' gallant elort to score. Thanks to some good play by Lheir front rank, they obtained a footing in the Widnes 25, and it was some tune before they were sent to the right-about, play 'being in mid-field when the whistle sounded, leaving , %Woes victorious by one dropped goal (four pofatts) to nothing. , , , - . • -</p>
        <p>THE ROCHDALE, HORNETS' SUSPENSION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the L:sncashite Section of the Northern Rugby Union, was held at the Sprnad Eagle flotel,Max- cliester,on 'luesday evening. The officials of the Rochdale Hornets Club applied for permission to play their match with Runcorn at Heywood next Saturday. The applica- tion was ~ranted subject to the consent of the Runcorn Langshaw, the secretary of the latter organ- isation strongly objected to the proposed arrangement, in view of the tact that the Oldham and Manithigham matches had not been ordered to be played at Heywood. The Rochdale Hornets had arranged to play those fix- tures ou their opponents' grounds, and Runcorn.clahned to be entitled to the same consideration.-4 special meeting of the Runcorn Committee will be held to con- sider the matter. In the event of their electingl not to go to Heywood the match will be played at Rochdale at the expiration of the term of the suipmsiou of the Hornets.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON A Y. TYLDESLRY A.—This return match was played at Tyldesley on Saturday. The weather al- .though dull, in Other respects was grand for football, there being scarcely a breath of wind. The ground had been covered with chaff, and despite the recent heavy rains was in fair condition. Tyldesley turned out with only 14 men, but this disadvantage was about neutra- lised by the aid of several first team players, who were unable to make the jaurney to Warrington. The War- ringtonians also pla-yed two substitues. The teams turned out as follows : Back, Peri-mond': threequa.rters, Bampson, Fearnley,Battersby and Scott ; half-hacks, J. T. Fearnley aud Prescott ; forwards,Wood- ward, Whitehead, Hayes, Middleton, Shepherd, Griffin, and Feartiley. Warrington ; Back. Spruce ; three- quarters, Forster, Hockenhull, Scott ana O'Brien ; half- backs, Price and Clark ; forwards, Wilcock, Thornton, Bailey, Preeth, Berry, Swilow, , Hughes and Boyle. There were not more than 200 present when the game started. The opening stages were confined to Tyldesley's terri- tory, but a miscatch by flockenhull allowed the visitors to get away. Wilcock saved, and throwing to O'Brien, the latter made rood use of the pass And ran to half- way, which was fu..ther supplemented by a good bit of play by Price, which resulted in scrimmages on the Tyldesleyites line. Battersby cleared nicely, but Scott (Tyldesley), failing to pick up, Price took advantage of the mistake and led the visitors back, Clark being held on the line. A break away by the "Bongers" front rank, resulted in play being confined to the visitors quarters where Woodward made his :nark( btit the kick came to nothing. The ensuing play was entirely in favour of Warrington the passing ninonvit the backs being REM A. free kick was taken by Thornton but the ball fell be- low the bar. Passing by Price, Clark, Bockenhull,Scott, and Forster, kopt Tyldesley hard on the Aefc•rice and Sudlow was hurt when over the line a certain score be- ing lost. Half time, Warrington nil, Tyldesley nil. Tyldesley went awN, with a dash On the restart but thia was neutralised by goad kicking by O'Brien and Spruce. Price get nicely away from a scrimmage and giving to Clark be threw to Freeth who was brought down when about to cross the line. It was evident that such a strong siege could not be kept up on the Tyldesley de- fence without a result. Price securing drew his men nicely, and passing at the right moment, Clark was en- abled to score at the corner,the kick at goal failing. The play now became of a rough nature and tackling was of a vigorous order on both sides. O'Brien kicked to the Tyldesley line and Hoyle charging down Ferrimond's re- turn the ball found touch in a dangerous quart-r for the homeaters. From the ensuing play Scott was almost over being brought down Ots the line while a moment later Forster was also he'd when a try seemed at his mercy. At this point Shepherd was ordered off the field for striking Thornton, ryldesley being left to fight out the game with thirteen men. Fearnley took a pass in- tended for flockenhull and running to half way passed to ffampion but the latter was overhauled by Clark after clearing all opposition. Spruce relieved and play being • taken up by O'Brien, Price, and Clark the play waged round Tyldesley goal once More and Forster getting hold he scored a splendid try, Final result Warrington 2 tries, ryldesley nil.</p>
        <p>PARISH COUNCIL MELiZINGS.</p>
        <p>The annual Parish Council meetings were held on Monday, tor the purpose of electing tint councillors for the ensuing year. In a number ,of pirislies round War- rinzton, however, the ele,tions are not complete, as polls have been demanded, aid in these, elections will be held at the end of the prei4ent month.</p>
        <p>PENICETH. A good number or ratepayers were assembled on Mon- day evening- in the Wesleyan School to settle the question as to whom should represent them on the Parish Council for the forthcoming year. By common consent J. B. Air ken,Eq.,wasivoted to the ctiair,and he filled his rather difficult position with great tact. While -the time elapsed for filling up nomination papers, some discussion arose as to whether a man could vote who had lived in a house 12 months but who was not on the register. Mr. J. 0. Routledge, however, read ao extract which effectually settled this in the negative. The nomination papers were then collected and the following list of names was read out B. CrcB4, proposed by J. Wilkinson, seconded by J. S. Sn"iklY ; J. Digninn, proposed by- R. Garnett, seconded by T. G4rne,;,- ;. R. Garnett, proposed by J. Dignum, si-conclial by j. Cooke; W. L9ngshaw, proposed by J. LVlainley. seconded by D. Houghton ; D. McSechnie,pro- posed by J. Dignum, seeoncled by R. Garnett ; J Mil- lington, proposed by E. Cross, seconded by J.S.Sooddy ; .A. Murray, proposed by J. Digrihm, seconded by R. Cooke 0. E. Parker,proposed by T.B. Tetlow,seconded by J. Fiabet ; J. C. Routledge, proposed by W. J. Wig- field, i,econded by C. E. Parker ; T. B. Tetlow, proposed by J. Fishier, seconded by Cheethan ; A.White, Proposed by J. Dignum, seconded by R. COoke. The Chairman then asked whether any of the. candidates wished to withdraw. During a short interval Mr. J. John- son rose and asked the Chairman of 'the late Council as to haw many meetings were held in the last year of office, and what had been, done. Mr. Routledge (the chairman) replied that in his opinion the question was immature, and better be left until the public meeting. The Chairman after, giving final instructions to voters, put the names to the meet- ing, and the following were elected :—.f. C. Routledge, 49 ; C. E. Parker, 46.; T. A. Murray, 45 ; T. B. 'reins,/ , 36 ; J. Dignum,3l ; R. Garnett,2B ; D. M. McKecknie,24-. The unsuccessful candidates were : A. J. White, 23 ;J. Millington, 19 ; W Long,shaw, 16; E. Cross, 8. On the names being read. Mr. W. Longshaw rose cnd demanded a poll. Mr. J. Dignurn asked if he could withdraw in anyone's favour if he wished, but it was too late to do so. Most of those present expressed themselves satisfied with the list, and wished Mr. Longshaw to withdraw his demand, but he refusing, the poll be held. In answer to a question, the Clerk, Mr. J. Forster, Said a general ineeting would be held within • seven days of the 25th of this month, and the ratepayers would have a chance to stare their ihstructions to the councillors. After some discussion the meeting- closed with a vote of thanks to the Chairman</p>
        <p>LITTLE SANKEY. The Parirth Meeting for Little Sankey was held in the IWesleyan Schools, Sankey Bridges, Mr. R. B. Matthews in the chair. Teem was a small attendance, but . every member of the oLI Council was present. The nomina- tions were proceeded with, the following being handed to the Chairman :--1, Walter Brough, baker, Lovely- lane, proposed by Mr. G. 0. Payne, seconded' by Mr. Robert Schofield ; 2, James Corradine, iron worker, 239, Liverponl-road, propose(' by Mr. G. G. Payne, seconded by Mr. Robert Schofield; 3, fhomas Greenwell, 110, Liverpool-road, provision dealer, proposed by Mr. Robert Schofield, seconded by Mr. G. G. Payne; 4, Benjamin Grimshaw, Gate Wharf Farm, farmer, proposed by Mr. Robert Schofield, seconded by Mr. G. 0. Payne; 5, Wil- liam Jackson, cooper, 249, Liverpool-road, proposed by Mr. Schofield,seconded by Mr. G. 0. Payne; 6,Frederick Massey, signalman, 13. Richardson's-rovv, proposed by Mr. R. Schofield, seconded by Mr. Payne ;and 7, Samuel Wilkinson, wiredrawer, 214, Li vernool-road, proposed by Mr. Robert Schofield, seconded by Mr. 0, 0. Payne. The above were declared elected without a contest.—Mr. Schofield asked if anything had been done with reference to a playground for Sankey Bridges. It had been mooted in Sankey Bridges that the matter had been brought under the notice of Lord Lilford.—The Chair- man said he was happy to say that the matter was in a more advanced condition than when they last met. He had that morning received a letter from Mr. Selby (agent to Lard Lilford), which he would read: "Leigh, near Manchester, 7th March, 1896.- Dear Sir,—l am directed by Lord Lilford to inform you that his lordship is willing to give a recreation ground for Sankey Bridges the land shown on the enclosed tracing plan, on condi- tion that the same, except the portions reserved for streets, is fenced before being occupied with an unclimb- able vertical iron bar railing, 4ft. flin. high, that the present yearly tenant of the land be compensated for his interest thereon, that proper covenants be entered into for the paving, &amp;c., of one half of a 20ft. passage on the south and west sides, and of a 40ft. street on the east side of the land, on being called on by Lord Lilford, and that until such streets are made a road to the recreation ground shall he made and fenced eff by the guarantees, who will have to pay all Lord Lilford's costs. The par- ties to the arrangcment would have to be approved by Lord Lilford, and would be the Warrington Corporation, if the district goes into the borough. The arrangement would also be subject to such stipulations as Lord Lil- ford's solicitors may deem necessary.—Youis faithfully, Jo rrg B. SELBY, R. B. Matthews, Esq.. Chairman, Little Sankey Parish Council." (Applause.) He was sure that Vieir heartiest thanks would be accorded to Lord Lilford for his generosity. (Renewed applause.) The donation was a very handsome one end he was sure that an excel- lent playground would be provided. He had shown the letter just read to the Mayor and the Totvu Clerk of Warrington, and they recognized their liability with nleasure, and were delighted to find that such an excel- lent playground would be provided for the children of the neighbourhood. He also wished to express his thanks to Lord Lilford for what he had done in the matter. (Applause.) In answer to Mr. Greenwell. the Chairman said the actual value of the gift was from £1,200 to £1,500. That was the actual value of the land as ascer- tained by competent valuers. (Applause.) The meet- ing was then declared closed.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY. Mr. W. P. Skelton presided over the meeting for the parish of Great Sankey, which took place in the Board Schools. There was a good attendance. The following gentlemen were nominated :—Henry Bate, James Fair- (*.lough, W. H. L. Gorse, George Gould, Thomas Halton Thomas Kil9hvivr, William Dampier Jeans, Joseph Pow- nail, William Taylor, and George Thompson. The Chair- man asked if there were any withdrawals, as there were ten nominations for sir members. Mr. Jeans : I don't care much about effic.e myself, and I will withdraw. Mr. Jeans' name was then struck off the list, but no other candidate expreEsed a desire to withdraw. The Chairman : It does not appear that we are to have any more withdrawals, and I shall take the voting by show of hauls. This was done with the following result, Mr. Bate 30, Mr. Fairclough 27, Mr. Halton 24, Mr. Taylor 24 Mr. Ktlshaw 22. Mr. Thompson 16, Mr. Gorse 11, Mr. Pownall 8, Mr. Gould 4. The Chairman said that subject to no poll being de- manded he should have to declare that Messrs. Bate, Fairctough, Halton, Kilshavr, Taylor, and Thompson had been elected memners of the Parish Council for the en- suing year. Mr. Furness : This is not a fair representa- tive meeting of the ratepayers of Great Sankey, and I demand a poll. We are 600 parishioners in this ward, and this meeting is not a fair representation. Mr. Thomp- son asked if the name of Mr. Jeans would be submitted to the electors ?—The Chairman : It cannat be now. It is not possible to re-instate Mr. Jeans now. He with- drewlbefore the show of hands was taken .Mr. Jeans—l am in the hands of the meeting so far as that is concerned.— The Chairman (after a pause) : I find that Mr. Jeans' candidature is go d, as he has not written his signature of withdrawal at the back of the nomination paper. Therefore he can stand.—Mr. Jeans : I will do so if the meeting is willing.—Mr. Skelton remarked that if any of the candidates wished to withdraw before the poll was taken they could do so by giving ten clear days' notice at the office of the returning officer, Mr. J. C., Sutton.</p>
        <p>APPLETON. sTOCKTON MUTH WA RD.</p>
        <p>The meeting of Stockton Heath clectors was held in the National Schools, Alderman Francomh, JP., being selected to act as chairman. Mr. J. Ellison, clerk, was also present, and the attendance was large. Alderman Francomb asked for he nomination papers to he handed in. i his was accordingly done, when it WAS seen that t e following gentlemen, eleven in number, had been put forward as candidates :—Charles William Daven- port, proposed by W. B. Roberts, seconded by George John Wallis; Thomas Flampson.propneed by John Wig. nail, seconded by Samuel Wesley Dallow ; Ashton Hayes, propoced bi Michael Kaye, seconded by Thomas Ince ; John Hedecock, proposed by Edward Bradshaw, seconded hv Frederick Timperley ; John Pamberton.pro- posed br William Bolton, seconded by Richard Wood ; Frederick Pearson,proposed by Thomas Ince,seconded by WiWim Butterworth ; Willi•im Richardson, proposed by Frederick Pearson, seconded by Charles William Daven- port; William Coppoclr,proposed by Richard Shackleton, seconded by John Whit elegeo, ; James Whitley, proposed by William Bouchier.seconded by A.Daine;Altert Broady proposed by Mark Cross, seconded by Pcter Lester ; and James Warburton, proposed by W. Booth,seconded by Frank Warburton. The poll was then taken with the following results : Davenport. 49: Pearson, 46 ; Flayes, 41 : Richardson, 33 ; Broady, 25; Whitley, 21 ; Conpock, 21 ; Hedecock, 19 ; Pemberton, 19 ; Warburton, 17; Hampson, 15. Mr. James Warburton demanded a poll, and after waiting ten minutes, the Chairman then observed that a poll would have to take place, and he forthwith de- clared the meeting closed. Oa the motion of Mr. Davenport, seconded by Mr. Warburton. a vote of thanks was accorded Alderman Francomb for presiding.</p>
        <p>APPLETON WARD. The electors of this ward met in the Thorn Schools, awl Plected thPir ronrQptjoq withont any difficnitv. The Sweetrn,-at .mitte supply Co.,haying d scontinutit to use CADBURY'S CHOCOLATES in thvir automatic machines those who still wish to procure CADBURY'S CHOCOLATES wit be able to do so from most of the refreshment rooms at the railway stations, and from confectioners,—Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Knowles occupying the chair. The person nominated were Messrs. Jetties and Edward Britch, Hugh Derbyshire, John Derbyshire, Henry Heyes, John Marsh, R. Roughsedge,and John Whitlow. Upon these names being put to the meetingftwelve votes were regis- tered for the first two candidates,two votes for the third candidate,thirteen for tke fourth,eight for the fifth, and eleven for the hat three. No poll being demanded the Chairman declared Messrs. J. and E. Britch, J. Darby- shire, Marsh, Roughsedge, and Whitlow duly elected. The usual vote of thanks to the Chairman brought the meeting to an end.</p>
        <p>WOOLSTON-WITH-MARTINSCROFT. The Parish Meeting for this township was held on Mcinday evening in the Roman Catholic Sch(cols, Ma:r- tinscroft. Mr. William Houghton presided, and Mr. James Daintith, assistant overseer, acted as clerk to the meeting. Five councillors were required, and the.fol-. iowing were nominated for election :--Samuel Elam, Charles Fairhurst, Robert Hunter, Nicholas Massey, William Massey, Thomas Platt, James Houghton, and William Platt. ' No One withdrew, and no questions were asked qf the candidates, and the voting proceeded, with the following result : N. Massey, 38 ;R. Hunter, 29; S. Ellam, 24; C. Fairhurst. 23 ; W. Platt. 22; J. Houghton 17; W. Massey, .M .16; T. Platt, 16. r. Thomas Houghton, farmer, as on the list occasion of the election of parish councillors, demanded a poll, and the matter will now have to be decided by ballot on a day to be fixed by the returning officer (Mr.. Samuel Sutton). A vote of thanks to the Chairman for presid- ing terminated the proceedings.</p>
        <p>_ BURTGNWOOD.. The meeting was held in the Board School. There was a large attendance, Mr. Edwin Dawson occupying the chair. The Chairman asked for the nomination papers to be handed in, and said there were only seven candidates required. There were only seven. somina- tiom papers handed in as follows :—Mr. William Fair - dough. Brook Head Farm ; Mr. Andrew Jackson. Bank House Farm ; Mr. Thomas Norcott, Smithy Farm ; Mr. Richard Naylor, Pear Tree Hotel ; Mr. William Southern, Rose Villa ; Mr. William Sephton Green ; and Mr. John Warburton, Dial-post Farm. The Chairman said as there were no other nominations forthcoming the above seven would be elected. Mr. Jackson moved,and' Mr. Fairclough seconded, a vote of thanks to the Chair- man, and this having been agreed to, the meeting was brought to a close.</p>
        <p>WALTON INFERIOR. The annual meeting of the above parish was held in the large room adjoining the Stag Inn. There was a good muster of parochial electors. Mr. T. Smith Burgess was electqd to preside over the meeting. The papers were scrutinized, and three found to be duplicates. The following were declared valid nominations, viz :—Alfred Broady, publican and farmer; William Beswick, miller; John Cartwright, railway signalman; John Parkes, joiner; Frederick Monks, iron manufacturer; Joseph MoulAeld, builder; William Turner, farmer ; Joseph Ward, engine driver; John Weir, farmer; John Whit- field, wharf manager. Upon the names being read out to the meeting Mr. Broady and Mr. Parkes withdrew their nominations, and the night were declared duly elected. Votes of thanks to the Chairman brought the meeting to a close. The new Council will meet on April 15th, when chairman, clerk, and overseers will be elected.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD WITHOUT. The meeting of the above parish was held in the schoolroom of the Baptist Chapel, Wash-lane. There was but a small attendance, the proceedings being of a formal character. Mr. Percy Davies (chairman of the Parish Council) took the chair. The Chairman asked for any nomination papers to be passed to him, and on find- ing that he was nominated as a candidate he at once vacated the chair,and asked the meeting to elect another chairin.,r, when ;1.1 was unanimously resolved that Cap- tain Waring take the chair. The following nominations were passed as valid, viz :—Messrs. P. Davies, Roberts, Lee, Pierpoint, William Richardson, Isaac Turner, William Hopkins and George Pilling Mr. Isaac Turner and Mr. William Hopkins take the place of the Rev. Edward- Moore (who has left the district) and Mr. George Nicholls. A vote of thanks te Mr. Davies far his services in the chair brought the meeting to a close.</p>
        <p>ITHELWALL. The Parish Meeting for this township was held in the National Schoolroom. on Monday evening, and was fairly well attended. The Rev. T. Pym Williamcon (chairman of the Parish Council), occupied the chair, and conducted the business of the meeting. The nomina- tion papers were: handed to the Chairman, and as only the requisite ntinal,er of councillors were nominated, the Chairman declared them to be duly elected. The new Parish Council will, therefore, consist of Admiral Jones- Parry, J.P., Messrs. William Henry Bleckley, J:P., Wil- liam Houghton, Jacob Site George Lambert, Herbert Roebuck, Alfred Robins, Norman Taylor, and William Tinsley.</p>
        <p>CROFT. The meeting was held at the Board School. There was a fair attendance of ratepayers. Mr. Reginald Owen (chairman of the Parish Council) presided. The following nominations were handed in to the Chairman: —Mr. Joshua Ball, Southworth Hall, farmer; Mr. James Hunt, Back-lane, Croft, gentleman : Mr. Enoch William Sankey, Heath Farm, Croft, farmer ; Mr. William Crane Southern,Springfield Farm, Croft, farmer ; Mr. Samuel Thomason, Heath-lane, Croft, farmer; the Rev. H. I. Wadeson, Croft Rectory. There being only six persons nominated,the Chairman declared them duly elected,and the meeting concluded with a vote of thanks to the Chaitman.</p>
        <p>WINWICK. A largely attended meeting ot the' parish was held in the schoolroom. The Chairman of the Parish Council (the Rev, c mon Penrhyn) occupied the chair, and the assistant overseer (Mr. E. C. Cooper) was present as parish clerk. The following gentlemen were norninased as parish councillors :—Mr. John Caldwell, farmer, Or- ford : Mr. Thomas Corbett, farmer, Orford ; Mr. W. G. Wallace,steward, Winwick ; Mr. Peter Longton, farmer, Winwick; Mr. Thomas Warburton, farmer, Huhne ; Mr. T. L. Shaw, farmer, Winwick. The requisite num- ber being six, and no others being nominated, the Chair- man declared these gentlemen to be the new Parish Council. The Chairman expressed his regret at losing the services of Mr. T.Mather.whe had removed to Bold, And Mr. Sam Kerfoot, who retired. A vote of thanks to the Chairman concluded the business.</p>
        <p>BOLD. -- A ratepayers' meeting was held in the Bold Heath School on Monday night to elect six parish councillors, the following were elected :—Mr. T. B. Dixon, Mr. T. Smith, Mr. W. Darlington, Mr. T. Webster, Mr. W. J. Garton, Mr. H. Hodgkinson Mr. Thomas Baker, D.C., was appointed Chairman on the mntion of Mr.J.Warbur- ton, jun., D.C., seconded by Mr. W. J. Garton.</p>
        <p>- MOORE. A fairly attended meeting was held in the Infant School on Monday evening for the election of parish councillors for the ensuing year. Mr. A. de Prez being voted to the chair, the following eleven persons were nominated :—Rev. J. W. Gilbertson, and Messrs. John Bradbury, farmer; E. C. de Prez, plumber; William Dunbobin, farmer; T. Dwerryhouse, manufacturer ; T. Fleet, platelayer; Peter Gleave, grocer ; John Highfield gentle:nan ; Isaac Lawton, joiner; Charles Rnylance, farmer ; and Miss Dutton. spinster. The result of the vneing was as follows :—Highfield, 12; Gilbertson, 11 ; Roylaner' 10 ; Bradbury, 9 ; Dunbnbin, 8 ; Mita Dutton, Gleave, 8; Lawton, 8: Fleet, 7 ; Dwerrybouse, 6; and de PrPz, 5. No poll biing demand?d, th 3 Chairman d'. dared the first eight duly elected. Mr. Roylance pro- posed a vote of thanks to Mr. Dwerryhouse for his ser- vices as chairman throughout the past year, and also to Mr. de Prez for his services that evening, and the meet- ing terminated.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENFIALL. A good attendance of parochial electors and others FIE.- sembled in the National School, Grappenhall, on Mon- day evening, for the purpose of electing the new Parish C iuncil for the ensuing year. Seventeen noolination papers were handed in to the Chairman, Mr. William Long, but he being one of the candidates vacated the chair. and on the ny tion ot Mr.T.W.Jones, seconded by Mr. E. Dallow. the Clerk of the Coon ill (Mr. Wood- cock) was elected to fill the position of chairman for the rest of the meeting. Twelve of the nominati:-ns were de- clared to he valid and five duplicates. Mr. SH J uikine demanded a poll, hut by Me srs. Alan Lister-Kaye and William Lomas withdrawing their candidature, he um- ber was reduced to ten, the number of councillors re- quired. The Chairman then declared the following elected : Messrs. Richard Boardman, John Bradford, Thomas Burrow, Francie Ellison, J. J. Fairhurst. Rev. Richard Greenall, S.M.Flougoton, William Loor..Tiiomas Smith, and Richard L. Warburton. A poll was thus avoided. Eight of the retiring councillors are re- turned, and the Rev. R. Greenall and Mr. Thomas Sr.r.litti take tie p aces of Messrs. A Lister-Kaye ' and William Lomas.</p>
        <p>STRETTON. The Parish Meeting for the election of parish council- bra for the ahove-namml parish was held in the school- Toom on Monday evening, a fair attendance of parish- ioners beitiv. present. The following were duly elected_ Messrs. Whitler, Faulkner, Leather, Winstanley, Rutter. Howard and Owen. Messrs. Whitley, Winstanley and Rutter are the three new members, being elected in place of Canon Dodd and Mr. E. Brocklehurst, retired,and Mr. Joseph Smith. deceased. A tie resulted on the votino. for J. Darbysldre and E. Owen, but on the casting vote". bling given in favour of Mr. Owen by the Chairman, he was elected.</p>
        <p>PRESTON BROOK. Very little excitement prevailed in this parish during the election or councillors, many of the nomination Papers being filled up in the room where the meeting Was held. The following eight persons were declared elected :—Joseph Spruie (district councillor), grocer and farmer ; James Webb, foreman ; S. Jones, grocer; T. H. Jones, clerk; W. H. Jackson, clerk; Joseph Whitlow, farmer; Henry Whitlow. farmer ; and D. Basnett, fernier. There was a very fair attendance of ratepayers. HAT row. The Pariah Meeting was held on Monday evening at 7.30, but there was not a large attendance. The follow- ing candidates were declared elected :—Messrs. W. Done, W. Fowles, T. H. Fairhurst, J. Plato, H. Mawdsl.ey, R. D. Meredith and A. Moreton. The meeting terminated with a vote of thanks to the Chairman.</p>
        <p>DUTTON. The annual parish meeting for the parish )f Dattoo was held in the Board School on Monday evening. following were elected plrish councillors :.—Joseph ter,farmer,Dutton Hall ,• Robert Fowles,farmer Geor, Kinsey, farmer; John Kinsey, farmer; Herbert Potti; agent ; Arthur Hervey Talbot, J.P., gentleman, Duao_ Lodge ;John Williams,gameke-per; Joseph Withensho, farmer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>FOOT ALT,.</head>
        <p>FOOT ALT,.</p>
        <p>CIIICKSTER3 should seem e the " PockPt 'Diary and Companion." issued bo the well-known Sports Maui- fa !torero, GEORGE G. BUSSES' &amp; Co,, Peckham, London. All the information Cricketers require is found therein, as well as the usual Diary, yet it will go in the waistcoat pocket. Leather bound, gilt edges, 6il. ; postage, ft If you are not satisfied, G. G. B. &amp; Co. will return the price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION. WARRINGTON v. TYLDESLEY.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. WARRINGTON v. TYLDESLEY.</p>
        <p>The return engagement between Tyldesley and War- rington was played on Saturday. The weather in the morning was very unsettled, but during the time the match was played, the rain kept off and there was a Very large crowd of between six and seven thousand people present to witness it. Bill was unable to play and Fairhurst was drafted in, while Bancroft for Tyldes- dey had to take Trow's place at full back, Price filling the vacancy at three-quarters. The following was the Warrington team : Heesom, full back ; Lawleas, Barber, Cross and Carey, three-quarters; Bate and Higginbot- tom, halves; Taylor, Nevins, Dakin, Saunders, Fair- hurst, Barrett, Swift, and Burrows, forwards. Punc- tual to time, Barrett kicked •off ; and Warring- ton commenced in good style by pressing strongly. and Barber and Cross tricking Lawton and Price,a score appeared imminent when B incroft cleared. Fairhurst and Nevins were especially prominent, and though J. Berry cleared, a qoick dribble by Dakin alnaost led up to a trv. Auotleir round of passing saw Carey make a mull; and Tyldesley took advantage of the slip, and 1-lessons was on just in time to save. Bate and Lawless regained the lost ground, fOr a judicious kick by the former and a shard sprint by Lawless gave the localside the attack: Higginbottnm got clear away and had hard luck in being pulled down within a yard of the line. This was only bra moment for Bate taking a pass from Higginbottom, dodeed the Berrrs and got over. The kick at goal by Swift failed. From a Penalty kick Swift just failed to send the ball between the posts. Burrows passed wildly a minute later, and Tyldesley got to the Warrington lines, Cross saving a certain scoreby kicking dead. The packs which were fought at the Warrington quarters were noted for their vigorous nature, and eventually the Warringtonians rushed the game so half-way, when the whistle sounded for the interval with Warrington leading by a try. On re- sliming, Tyldesley by the medium of the Brothers Berry, Price and Roberts, placed the Warrington lines in jeo- pardy for some considerable time. Carey failed on one occasion to hold the ball, and it was almost a miracle how Heesom saved. The locals were penalised in a fav- ourable position,hut W. Berry's kick was charged, and Warrinetnn had a narrow escape. Another opportunity came for Tyldesley soon after,for an infringement of the now off-side rule, and being penalised J. Berry placed a goal from the easy place, this point placing the sides on equal footine.Apparently nettled by this success of their rivals, Warrington swarmed round the Tyldesley goal soon after the start from the centre, but there was no combination. Berber and Lawless checked the visitors at half way, and desultory play following, the latter 'stages of, the match were contested in Warrington quer- w,ers, but no further scoring took place,and the result of ' the game was a d:aw, the score being Warrington a try (three points), Tyldesley a penalty goal (three points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUNCORN v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. WAKEFIELD TRINITY.</p>
        <p>• The crack Runcorn team were entertained on the Belle Vue ground at Wakefield on Saturday, and it was some- what strange that the largest gate of the season should assemble to see toe match with the Trinitarians, con- sidering that s)me seventy miles separate the two clubs. But reputations travel faster than L. and Y. trains, and it was the Runcorn fifteen's recent perform- ances which made the local enthusiasts roll up in such large numbers. There must have been an attendance of 7,000 people. As for the teams' Runcorn reported the . absence of three regular forwards, but their places were taken by men of no mean calihre,while Wakefield played their usual team. Playing down the slope in the first half,the home team opened the game in sensational style. Their forwards went away with a tremendous rush, and by this means secured a capital scoring position. Of this Lockwood, the old International, quickly took ad- , vantage, running in a beauty—as a result of a pass by I Howell—in the first couple of minutes. Moorhouse did nothing with the place kick. Wakefie!d quickly con- trolled the ball again,and by smart rushes and fine kick-I ing put the visitors on the defensive, but only minors accrued, these being gained by drops at goal by Foster ' and Howell. Before the interval arrived Runcorn had ' two good openings, one made by Myers's running and there hsr by passing between Faulkner a ad Butterw( rth; but both came to nought, thanks to some unaccountable mulling. At half-time the Trinitarians were leading by a try to nothing. The peccnd portion opened with a brilliant dribble by W. Walton over half the length of the field. Indeed, he looked like scoring himself, but, as a matter of fact, tie paved the way for Morgan, who scrambled over after some quick, short passing between Lockwood and Price. Foster's kick went agley. Then Runcorn woke up, and, reaching the Wakefield line, J. Faulkner passed to Myers, and he scored a somewhat "soft "try. which Hnueliton made a poor attempt to convert. Just before the finish Mc era was given a chance of equalising with a penalty kick, but nothing came of his attempt. Se the game ended :—Wakefield Trinity, two tries (six points) ; Runcorn, one try (three points). A remarkably even game says" Domino" ill the "Athletic News," taken as a whole, but one in which the Trinitarians showed far the better football. The secret of their success 14 in the forwards, where they simply swamped their opponent ,getting possession nine times out of ten. Thus the Wakefield b cks had by far the most chances, and these they turned to the best ac- count, Lockwood, as usual, proving a thorn in the side of his opponents. Pries and Howell, too, in no way damaged their reputation, while Todd was as safe as houses behind. Beaten in frent, Runcorn could stand little chance behind, hut, as a matter of fact, made poor use of the openings they had. They lost one certain try at least. Myers, Faulkner, and Hougntnn were the pick ef the team.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RUNCORN v. hULL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN v. hULL.</p>
        <p>Hull fulfilled their long deferred engagement with Runcorn on Wednesday. It will be remembered that the match should have been contested at Runcorn on the Bank Holiday succeeding Cnristmas Day, but owing to the time of the rear and the distance between the two places. the Hull afieials were unable to make satisfac- tory arrangements for their team's visit, and on their paying an indemnity, an arrangement was come to that the match should be played on the 11th March. Tile railway journey from Hull to Runeorn is the longest to be undertaken by any Northern League club, and it is very fatiguing to the players. In order, therefore, that they should be fresh for their work on Wednesday, the team travelled from Hull on Tuesday night, and made a break in the journey for a much needed rest at Liver- pnol,from whence they travelled on Wednesday at noon. Rain fell during the morning and throughout the morning and continued until within a short time of the commencement of the match. The ground was in a deplorable condition for playing football, and the east end of the ground was really a quagmire of clay. Under such conditions 1 true exposition of Rugby football was impossible. A few minutes beyond the advertised start the teams stepped into the enclosure, the Runcorn players for the nonce wearing white guernseys in order the bstter to diatiriguish them from their opponents who wore black. The teams were composed as follows : Runcorn :—Fioughton, full-back; Warder, Butter- ' worth, W. Faulkner and Myers, three-quarters; R ,binson and J. Faulkner, half backs; Farmer, Moores, Walker, Darlington, Taylor, Johnson, Gayter and Hughes, for- wards. Ilull W. Johnson, fu'l back ;C. C. Lampriere, Reed, Ff. Thompion, 'l'. Wilkinson, three-quarters ; C. Tnwnend and J. Triwnend, half backs; Wales,Pluvee.W. Manse]., Holmes. Feetham, McLachlan, Jacketts and W. Harmer, forwards. It will be seen that several of the usual Runcorn for- wards were absent in consequence of injuries, hut the teams may be taken as representative. The Runcorn captain won the spin of the coin but there was little choice to make, the wind having subsided. Jacketts set the ball in motion a-id W. Fattikner returned into touch. From the scrimmages which ensued, it was soon appar- ent from the manner in which the visitors were able to " screw " them that they hhd an advantage in this respect. Houghton was called upon to save, which he did in a manner which delighted the spectators. Run- corn now gained a footing in the visitors' territery, but found a stumbling b nck to further progress in Johnson, the fell hack, who placed a ,terling game. Hull were awarded a free kick an I this enabled them to carry the game into the home twenty-flye. Some exchange kink- ing between Floneliton and Lampriere was now wit- nesser.l. S ortly afterwards Lampriere intercepted a pass between W. Faulkner and Myers, and cleverly eluding his opponents crosaed Me line. The place kick which was taken from a favourable positicn, was successfully made by Thompson, who incr ased the score to five points. Runcorn now warmed to their work, and rushed the game to the visitors' line. Butterworth made a drop at goal, hut was unsuccessful, and Johnson toucheft down. Warder was next prominent by a run and a smart kick across the field, and Runcorn had hard urea in not getting over. A series of scrimmages and line outs ensued, until the Runcorn forwards by a com- bined rush crossed the Hull line. A pass forward, how- ever, spoiled the effort. Almost immediately afterwards a free kick was awarded the home team, but Farmer failed at the place. A number of scrimmages followed, and at length, Warder receving a long pass from Myers dropped a fine goal, a perfotmance which was loudly cheered. Roused by this sneers i the Runcorn team made strenuous efforts to increase the score, but the whistle sounded with the home team near the Hull lines. The visitors were thus leldjrn..! bp one point when the ' teams changed ends. To describe fully the play in the second half would be to record for the most part a series</p>
        <p>-VCRS. —StiALSKIN JACKETS ar4l FURS of every dea- l' eripiton Cleaned or Re—Dyed and Altered. Fur Skins IJreem3.l and Made-up into Rugs, Muffs, Collars, &amp;e.—B. COORE, Manuralturing Furrier 11 Upper Newinßton, and 21, lienshaw-street,Liverpnol, Establi shed over 21 years,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>IMPERIAL PA RLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PA RLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY In the House nf Commons on Morsday, Mr. LoNn, in reply to Mr. Eneledew, said legislation would be neces- sary to compel a seller of foreign meat to expose a notice to the effect that he was a dealer in such meat. The vi ,ws of the Government on the matter would be stated wi.e7, the Dill on the subject came on for discussion. Sir M. "RITE RIDLEY told Mr. R. G. Webster that the trial of election petitions formed part of the ordinary duties of the judges,and they had, therefore not been "withdrawn lrem their ordinary duties " to try the petitions. Mr. CHAPLIN informed Mr. C. Morley that the Preston Corinratioa WAS the only public authority in England that had spent no part of the grants from the Customs and Excise duties under the Local Taxation Act, 1890, upon technical or Weller education. Re had communi- cated with the Council, and had been informed that they had applied the money received by them under the Caroms and Excise duties grant for the relief of the borough rate under special circumstances, those circum- stances being that they had spent £lB,OOO on a site for a technical school, now in course of erection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>On the motion for the second r-eading of the London- (Jerre improvement Bill in the Finuse of Commona on Toe,' lay Mr. Kstox called attention to the •fact that CathAies of Londonderry, though an absolute majority of the population, had not a representative in the Cor- poration and had not a sinel.individual in the munici- pal service. He moved the rejection of the Bill. Alter some debate the motion for the second reading was carried by 221 votes to 130. On the motion of Mr. KNOX the Bill was referred to the Select Committee on the Belfast • Corporation Bill. The debate on Mr. KNOX'S rentii,n that the select Committee on the Belfast Corporation Bill should be instructed to in- quire into the municipal franchise and the present mode of election of aldermen and councillors of the city, and whether it was exp.dient to alter the law as to such franchise or mode of election, was resumed. Mr. COURTNEY supported the instruction, remarking that if the Union was to be maintained it was not by making an oppressive use of their majority of 150, but, whilst curbing. the turbulence or the South, they were no less hound to re- strain the exclusive Orangemen of the North. Mr. G. BALFOUR said the Government had hitherto endeavoured to lean to neither one side nor the other, but to hold the balance evenly between the two partieJ, and that was the policy which they would pursue to the end. Mr. J. MORLEY said this was the test case—whether they were going to make any concession whatever to Irish de- mands, whether, though they would not grant the greater demand, they were going to allow localities to try experiments, or whether they were going to surren- der themselve, to the old passion and prejudice which had brought Irish government to its present miserable condition. the " instruction " was rejected by 219 votes to 164.</p>
        <p>The remainder of the sittirTg, after the questions Ind been disposed of, wag devoted to the discussion of a motion for the opening of the national museums and art galleries in London on Sundays.</p>
      </div>
