      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>A. PENKETH CASE AT THE CoIINe</head>
        <p>A. PENKETH CASE AT THE CoIINe</p>
        <p>COURT.</p>
        <p>tf _ A claim was made at the Warrington Colin 0 Court on Thursday last week, before Judge Wynne Ffoulkes, by Edward Percy, farmer,-Widnes, for • alleged to be due from George Tusol. fare:leo° Penketh, for rent for two small crofts. A cotiO„A claim for £5 was made by defendant for alleg7, trespass on the crofts by plaintiff. Mr. T. J. Pete,; appeared on behalf of the plaintiff, and Mr. Artlitt Browne for the defendant. Mr. Peters said 07., his client, let the crofts to the dnfndan in Ain:;f 1894, for -£3, on the understanding that the tenan,7o would expire in the following November, as wished to sell the land. He let the land on t"os same terms again on tke following April, as the land cfeej still not sold. In November. 1895, the plaintiff PI3 some bricks on the land, as the tenancy had expired, 5-1 defendant thereupon refused to pay the 3 due, , compensation was paid by the plaintiff for la- ;pass he alleged had been committed as he canten•ied tbat,l4, agreement was to take the crofts at £3 per annum.--",d plaintiff was then called and said that he had intent: it to sell the land in question, but until he did so be lel4 to Tuson at £3 per annum. Tuson said that he set it with potatoes.—Mr.Peters:What were the arrs°;3o ments made when you let the land ?—Plaintiff: , arranged that I should have £3 for the letting ° pot farm from April to November lat.—Mr. Browne.," you said just now that it was £3 per annum. rr, our ease.—The plaintiff (continuing) said MO ember, he sent in his bill for £3 rent n h rex' the money in December. Truson wish t. nthave crofts again for another reriod, and WI'IIPSS ulthrlat,el agrecd to let it from April to November, 1395, for A') In November of the same year he again tool: and defendant thereupon refused to pay the rent'-, Cross-examined: He had tipped 20,000 hriek4 on NO', ember 25tb, upon one of the erofts which was a quart_e; of an acre in extent. Tuson had no cows on the field that time. He did not let the land at £3 per annum,vni ot was never agreed that thereat should be paid eve,u, December. Tusan had not centinually used the field lac'? , cows from August to the following November. I fhe done so, he would have committed trespass.-"` )1 Browne said that his client was the tenant of a 1,31, • adjoining the two crofts. and as there Iva.: a plentP,l isupply of water in them he wished to rent them for „II cattle. He accordingly went to see Mr. Percy at in April,lB94,and he and his wife would swear that agreement was for £3 per annum, and that the should be paid in December. The plaintiff bad fact made a slip in his evidence to the same effect. also promised to aive a written agreement to ?dr. hut although the latter had frequently aske 1 him for 'w he had failed to do so. Mr. Tipton hail had his cattle aver einee until November 25, 1895, when the plaiot.lo put 20,000 bricks upon the field, and this prevented 11,10 r from being able to take his cows to the field, and this he claimed £5 compensation. Mr. Tuson had to carry water to his cows ever since.—The defends°, , upon being called, said that he wanted the field for t, use of his cows. He still held possession of the field. law, as he had never had notice to leave, and practicsi held possession until next February. The agreeineli,4 was £3 per annum, and not from April to November, I stated by the plaintiff. A little time after the briej had been tipped on the ground, witness met Pere'? Ithe latter then promised to take the bricks out of Ifield and witness promised that he would then pay "4 rent.--Cross-examined :He had manured the grnvc,,l with blood and bone. He used one croft for cows the other for oats. The two together were ala;4ut I, Cheshire acre in extent.—Mr. Peters: Do yo; l not thno that that is worth mere than £3 a year P—Witness : iyou expect me to pay more than he asked. They say " Tilson'a a fool." (Laughter.) Continuing, admitted that he bad written to Percy clairninz only 1,, damages for the tipping of the bricks on the land,but Claimed that small sum because he had never been in court in his life before, and did not want 5n7 Mrs. Tuson gave similar evidence, and Charles Kilstavo farmer.residing near the defendant in Cuerdley said Olio he frequently saw defendant's cows on the field to the time the bricks were tipped. He considered ,•'„,/ reasonable sum for the loss incurred by the bricks on the field.—His Honour said that the evidence Cep, Itainlv showed that the aaTeement was £3 per annum, , fact the plaintiff had said so in his evidenee. There vt't, no doubt that the defendant was due £3 r the rOje , but it was also clear that wilful trespass bad been res r- ' by the plaintiff,and he did not think that the fact ti2,.0 the defendant had only claimed 15s. for compertaatlt'of should bind him. He would therefore give TuJgmentr the plaintiff for £3,and for defendant for £3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
