      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>THE CORPORATION CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>LAST OF THE SERIES.</p>
        <p>A good assembly of people attended at the Parr Hall on Saturday night to hear the last of the ex- cellent series of concerts given on Saturday nights, and which have been inaugurated by the Corpora- tion. The following six artistes appeared on this occasion :—Miss Lizzie Skeet (soprano), Miss Alice 1 Richardson (contralto), Mrs. W. B. Roberts (solo pianist), Mr. Harry Crellin (Tyrolean vocalist), Mr. J. H. Greenwood (humorist and accompan- ist, and Mr. J. G. Hewson (bass). Miss Skeet, Miss Richardson, and Mr. Ilewson opened the entertainment by efficiently, singing "Queen of the Night," after which Mr. Howson I created a good impression by his rendition of "0 Hear the Wild Wind Blow." Other songs by him I were "So, Sir Page," "Two Spoons," the first of which was the best performance. Miss Richardson sang with credit to herself considering the fact that she was suffering from hoarseness, "Twickenham Ferry," and also took part in the duet " &amp;laying," with Mr. Hewson. Owing to Miss Itichardsen's inability to make all her announced appearances, the majority of the singing fell upon Miss Skeet, who was happily equal to the emer- gency. For her first song "Ii Bacio," she was en- cored and responded with "Killarney." After this she sang "The Zuyder Zee," "The Carnival," and the "Holy City," and was encored for each. Not even with nine appearances were the audience satiated, and this tact is a distinct tribute to Miss Skeet's finished singing, graciousness, and pretty voice. Miss Skeet has also established a record of appearances at one concert in the Parr all, and it is further to her credit that the last song of ail "Eileen Alanna " was rendered with pureness and strength of tone equal to the first. Mr. Harry Crel- lin's singing was a uoveity in its way, and therefore, came in for much appreciation. The chorus of each of his songs" The Mountain Guide," and " Shni- der, how you vas," finished up with a Tyrolean carol which Mr. Crellin rendered with a clearness and force which, we should think, would have carried far over the hills and dales it is usually heard upon. He was encorfd on each occasion. Mrs. W.B. Roberts displayed considerable proficiency as a pianist, and her selections "Etude de Concert " (Loeschorn), " Valse " (Chopin),aad " La Fontaine" (Mayee), were received with hearty approval, repe- titions being demanded and accorded. Mr. J. H. Greenwood kept the audience in constant merri- ment with his sketches" rile Picnic," " The Show- man," and a " Trip to the Isle of Man." The for- mer included an imitation of the recitation of the 1" Charge of the Light Brigade," by representatives , of various nationalities, and this itself though not new was far above the average in the capability with which it was rendered. Dialect was abund- ant in Mr. Greenwood's last sketch, and very amus- ing did he make the "trippers." Needless to say he was required to reappear for each sketch and give further evidence of his humoristic talent. Much regret is felt that this is the last concert., though the feeling is tinged with the satisfaction teat II next winter will see their re-commenzement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>DR. NANSEN'S JOURNEY.</head>
        <p>DR. NANSEN'S JOURNEY.</p>
        <p>NO FURTHUR NEWS OBTAINABLE.</p>
        <p>All attempts to explain the news about Dr. Nansen's return and to get any further details have thus far resulted in failure. rho Russian semi-official news agency continues (says the " Times" St. Petersburg correspondent) to receive telegrams from Irkutsk, which contain nothing but variations of the first report, and it has not yet been possible to trace that report Leyond the trader Kuchnareff, although more than one answer has already been received from Yakutsk, where Kuch- nareff's letter was received by Kandakoff. It is true that in the latest telegram from Irkutsk we are informed of dogs and provisions having been sent to the New Siberian Islands for the use of the returning explorer, but not one single new fact has yet been elicited as to the origin of the news, and the writer of the letter, Kuchnareff, strangely enough, does not appear to have said where and how the report reached him, and it is very astonishing that Dr. Nansen himself should not have sent word of his return to Ust Yansk. It' the circum- stances Captain Wiggins, who is still in St. Petersburg, suggests that search hould be made for traces of Nansen at Cape Cheliuskin, where, he thinks, the Fram most probably left despatches before going north. It is sup- posed that Nansen's ship may have been unable t, leave ' his last despatches at Dickson's Haven, as promised, on account of the drift ice, which Captain W iggink himself saw only a little later, on his way into the 'Yenisei. Therefore Nansen most likely was able to touch only the northernmost part of the coast at Cheliuskin, in which case he would not have failed to leave his last despatches there. Captain Wiggins i roposes that it nothing further is heard before the spring Baron Toll should be advised to undertake another expedition to Cheliuskin, starting with natives from Goltchikha,where the Baron is already well known. Captain Wiggins also says that he himself might possibly be able to get round as far as Cheliuskin on his next trip to the Yenisei if he should happen to have only one vessel,but he gene- rally has charge of two or three, and in that case would be unable to go so far. In a later message the same Cor- respondent says that Baron Toll himself mow beelins to have serious doubts about the news of Dr. Nansen's return, and agrees with the suggestion of Captain Wig- gins that the explorer most likely left despatches of some kind at Cape Cheliuskin.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Gladstone returned to Lindon from their Continental tour on Tuesday. They had travelled con- tinuously for about twenty-3even hours on the return journey. Liuerpool Assizes were opened on Tuesday. Mr. Jus- tice Kennedy. who presided in the Crown Court,said the calendar was a " formidable " one, and he pointed out that many of the offences charged were due to drunken- ness.</p>
      </div>
