        <p>kettiro to the ebitor.</p>
        <p>CHRISTIANITY AND THE ROMAN EMPIRE 7</p>
        <p>• Sir,—lt was not my intention to have taken part in the discussion which rim been going on for some time in your columns. lam perfectly aware that Mr. Peers is quite able to render a good account of himself and to give a sufficient reason for the faith, or the want of faith,that ia in him. But after reading Zaminer's letter of a fortnight ago I feel constrained to have a little say in the matter. It is a great pity that the amenities of discussion are so rudely disregarded by the various parties to the controv- ersy. The headings of some of the ,letters are not in good taste. For instance: " Mr. Peers--pictured and polloried," "Credulous logic," "An outing with the Salvation Harriers." Such language is not creditable to the writers and is only calculated to arouse prejudice and ill-feelings. It is a pity, a great pity, that these discussions are not carried on with courtesy and good- feeling:,„ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>• In aminer letter of Saturday fortnightihe makes the following statement, "The present condition of ciyilizttion among the leading nations upon earth is due to the ennobling influence and divine character of the teaching of Christ." In his previ- ous lett•T Zaminer says, " Let it be noticed how conveniently he "—Mr. Peers—" seeks to elude the force of my reference to the state and condition of the Roman world at large when Christ came in, and applied the purifying salt, so rescuing it from utter corruption and ruin." Now I d9n't want to follow Zaminer's example and make use of offensive epithets, but I con- fess that when I read these two passages in his letters my courtesy was somewhat embarrassed. Mr. Peers in his last letter quotes and very aptly quotes— Buckle. ;nether Z %miner's statemonts are due to gross ignorance or wilful fraud I must leave others to decide.</p>
        <p>New, first of all, what was the state of the Roman Em- pire before the advent of Christianity? Shall we appeal to Gitbon ? I suppose he is about the best authority we have touching the history of the Roman empire. He says "Notwithstanding the propensity of mankind to exalt the past, and to depreciate the present, the tran- quil and prosperous state of the empire was waumly telt, and honestly confessed, by the provincials as well as Romans. They acknowledged that the true principles of social life, laws, agriculture, and science, which had been first invented by the wisdom of Athens, were now firmly established by the power of Rome, under whose auspicious influence, the fiercest barbarians were united by an equal government and common lsnguage. They affirm that with the improvement in arts, the human species was visibly multiplied. They celebrate the in- creasing splendour of the cities, the beautiful face of the country, cultivated and adorned like an immense garden and the long festival of peace, which was enjoyed by so many nations, forgetful of their ancient animosities, and delivered from the apprehension of future danger." Gibbon' assures us that this is "perfectly agreeable to historic truth." Again, be tells us that "If a man were called to fix the period in the history of the world,during which the condition of the human race was most happy and prosperous, he would; without hesitation, name I that which elapsed from the death of Domitian to the accession of Commodus„ The vast extent of the Roman empire was governed by a'asolute power,under the ,uid- ance of virtue and wisdom. The armies were restrained by the firm but p,entle hand of four successive Emperors whose charactera and authority commanded involuntary respect." "The labours of these monarchs were over- paid by the immense reward that inseparably waited on their succeas ; ,by the honest pride of virtue, and by the exquisite delight of beholding the general happiness of which they were the authors," Such a picture of Ancient Rome ill-accords with the one drawn for us by Zaminer. He, and others of his kind, would have us believe the world to have been cockpit before the advent of Christianity—where the strong fought together and the weak were trampled under foot. They would have us believe that this noble empire was one mass of immorality and corruption. But if we turn to history we find that its peopie were adopts in all the arts of peace, as well an in those of war—that they had an abounding love of literature, and the arts, and sciences. T. what high moral and intellectual stan- dards many of her great men attained is amply testified to by Archdeacon Farrar. In his work " Slekers after God" he saya—". With all our knowledge and enlighten- ment we fall far short of some of them; we are less stern with our own faults, less watehful,les eelf-denving,less tender with one another. With our superior gifts, with our surer hopes, with (ler more present means of grace, what manner of men ought we to he? We ought to have attained to far loftier moral altitudes than they, but we hava not. Let us admit with shame and sorrow that some among these heathens showed themselves to be nobler, loftier, holier, freer from vanity, freer from meanness, freer from special pleading, freer from false- hood, more sp ritual, more reasonable, on some points even more enlightened,than many among ourselves. The very ideal Of the Chri •tian life seems to have been dwarfed to a poor, and vulgar, and conventional stan- dard. Perhaps the cintemplstion of virtue, and zeal, and integrity, and consistency, even in heathen lives, may produce at least same infinitesimal effect in arous- ing some of us to a desire for "something more high and heroical in relision than the present age affecteth." It is very difficult to reconcile such statements as these of Archdeacon Farrar and Gibbon with the nie- reefs:ma fictions and caricature, presented to us by some of your correspondents. Alas !it is only ton true, that instead of the Roman empire being rescued from utter corruption and ruin by the application of the " nurify- ing salt " of Christianity, its corruption and ruin date from the time that Christianity began to obtain the ascendency in the empire. Then it was that ignorance, I brutality and fierce intelerance, and every vice, that dominates the human mind raged unrestrained through- out the land. In the who of human literature is there 1 any sadder reading than that concerned with the, ripe and progress of Chr stianity is Europe and the decay of the once great and noble Roman empire? The ages that ensued—emphatically the ages of Faith as they were—are known as the "Dark Ages" What "horrors heaped upon horrors" rise to the mind as one remem- bers even a tithe of the unspeakable cruelties and ur.- bounded licentiousness of those ages ? And if we turn to our-own bava and review our present civilisation a. suggested by Zaminer, whilst we see .much that we are thankful for—much to he proud of, and much to give us high hope.; of the filter., we also eee muoli to deplore. We see in this Christian see and Christian country an increasing war going on between capital and labour, between tiIOSA whe have and those who have not. If we look abroad we see great Christien nations —including our own—fnllowere of the meek and lowly jesns—standine, like dogs straining in the leash, ready to fly at each other's throats—nations armed to the teeth and "spoiling" for the light. And Yet "Zaminer," who I surely cannot deny. tint must heartily deplore these facts, tells us that "The present condition of civilisation among the leading nations of the earth iv due to the ennobling influence and divine character of the teaching of Christ." I refuse to believe it. I cannel.. believe it. Neither do I believe that the carder and the good government of the present des, are due to the teachings of Christ. T have no desire tei deny or decry the beneficent iefinence of Christianity in the world.but I protest against the aserlption to this one religion, of sentiments and moral doctrines which are common to all religions and which are the product of humanity and nivililetion rather than of any creed or any religion. And let us not forget the benefits we owe to the late Sir Robert Peel. Withdraw "the gentleman in blue" from our midst and what would then become of the order, sobriety, and morality—of our present day civilisatinn ? If the strong arm of the law were relaxed our civilisation would present a sorry spectacle—the in- fluence of Christianity notwithstanding. I have written this letter, not in antagonism to the teaching.; of Christ, but in the interests nf truth. It can do Christianity no manner of good to blink the facts •af history and of human life. If Christianity has a grander message to mankind then Paganism had. man- kind has much for which to thank Paganism. We owe very miich to those grand old hernes, poets, and philos^- phers of ancient Greece and Rome. Instead of malign- ing them let us speak of them with the generous wed or praise which is their undoubted doe and acknowledge our indebtedness to them with heartiness and humanity. —Yours truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>HARRY ESMOND.</head>
        <p>HARRY ESMOND.</p>
        <p>A " LIN F:" FROM OLYMPUS.</p>
        <p>"If men were to alginate their reason in the simp'est transactions of life in the way they are taught end olcpected to do in matters of religion. they Would be laughed to scorn.' —Theodore Mayne. Sir,—ln these spiritu listic latter daya no one need be surprised if I act as medium and recount further com- munings with " Zaminer's" ineptly called witnesses— Plato and Seneca, When he of the Academy and he of the Porch, left us, without so much as an obo!us to bribe old cross Styx Charon, I thought I saw in thPir cone.. tenanees signs that the report to headquarters would not be very flattering to the pride of earth-born mortals. So off I rushed to my Olympia-phone—the latest electric novelty—and begged Mercury, who, it fteems, as heavenly messenger, has this department under his care, to connect me by switch to Olympus, the ambrosial region of eternal spring. At first hearing I started away from the instrument, so like an angry beehive was the sound. Oradually, however, the din became more cr- herent and then I could distinguish voices in the sound. It was the hilarious numble of many merry voices, chief of which was that of Jupiter who himself was quite jovial and shaking with laughter at the delusions of the</p>
        <p>WHAT CAN CUTICURA DO FOR CHILDREN? Everr- thing that is cleansing, nurifying and heautifying for the skin,scalp,and hair of infants and children,CuTrcun&amp; remedies will do. They afford instant relief, permit rest and sleep, and point to a speedy cure in the torturing of itching and burning eczemas. They clear the skin of the most disfiguring of humours. They cleanse the scalp of dandruff, scales, and crusts, and restore the hair. They purify the blond of simple, scrofulous, and heredi- tary humours. Everything about. them inspires confid- ence. They are absolutely pure and may be used from the moment of birth. They are speedy, economical. and unfailing. Parents, think of the lifetime of suffering often entailed by lack of early treatm,mt. Begin now. Cures made in childhood are almost invariably permac- ent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY. MARCH 14. 1996.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY. MARCH 14. 1996.</p>
        <p>mortals; while the Queen of Heaven herself was not un- moved, if certain silvery tones intermingling with the Thunderer's deeper bass might tell the tale. Evidently something of s higbly amusing nature had been on the carpet. But now Solon intervened and said—" But surely some mistake must have crept in here; do mor- tals now believe in the efficacy of hanging one man in expiation of the sins of others?" "Yea," said Plato, •' and mare to boot. The more enlightened of them now hold the opinion that even this—the sin of disobedience —was never committed-...a mytla, the burden of a fable. To execute one man to atone for the uncom- mitted sin of another is to sound the uttermost depths of futility." " Oh, the Christians," said Seneca, " have a way of their own—proving one thing by an excellent illustration of something else. So why should they net believe their God sent :down his Son to be sacrificed to atone far an uncommitted theft ?" " Extravagant," growled Saturn, smacking his lips. " Ah, your way was more economical," murmured Momua. "and a ready solution of the population question." "My heels," whispered Mercury to the merry god, " be careful !" And then, to the fore front of the august throng, he led impartial Justice, who craved permission of great Jove, to speak. "Say on sister of the even hand." Thus charged, she, turning to the messengers from earth. asked. "And does this principle obtain in British Law ?" " No ; why then do mortals to high heaven impute a principle unworthy of themselves? What the lawyer rejects for man, the creed-maker accepts for his God !" "Cos' he don't know where 'ee are," muttered Momus.. But now the voice of Aristotle claimed attention. Thus to his master the noble Plato spoke," The' documants ' you speak of, were evidently the compilation of men to whom the Law of Inevitable Sequence was unknown and unheeded. They appear to have been quite obliv- ious of the fact, that, of two contradictory statements, one only can be true. Compare Gen. I, 31, with Gen. 6, and Matt. xix.' 26. with Judges 1,19, and Num. xxiii.. 19. with Jonah iii.. 10, and E. xxix., 36, with Is. 1.11-12, &amp;e., &amp;c., and the inconsequential contradi- tions will be evident. And as are the ' documents '-so are the disciples ; exhibiting the same faculty of incon- clusive reasoning—declining to aecept conclusions the premises of which they have admitted Hence, uponssuch, argument and reasoning is thrown away—in all ages the populace have had their religious delusions,and this delusion will exist until another craze arises to take its place in popular estimation." Thus far I had heard when, strange to relate, the Olympiaphone became also an Olympiascope, and I saw our " Zaminer " approach the scales held in the hand of justice,and pile the one side beam high with " documents " labelled " Tradition," " Opinions," " Sentiments and surmises," with one of his own en- titled, " What someone has told me that someone had said that someone had found in some book he had read." Upon this Minerva called the genius of truth and his- toric accuracy, and she dropping a few golden grains of truth into the empty pan, made poor Z %miner's " Senti- ments and opinions" speedily kick the beam. So crest- fallen amidst the jeers of the immortals, unmindful of his scripts, he turned rofiee.but was stayed by. Minerva :who graciously proffered to teach him to ride his holahy without turning his face to the tail ! And then, indeed a touching sight, ISi w fair Venus step from the jeer- ing crowd and, amidst words of whispered consolation, say, "Be not cast down, for though mistaken, know mortal errors are ennobled by good intent! Vale " Which kindly greeting to you and. your readers, Mr. Editor, I here re-echo.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>JOHN PEERS.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST BAZAAR AT</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>BRITISH AND FOREIGN BIBLE</head>
        <p>BRITISH AND FOREIGN BIBLE</p>
        <p>SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON IUXILIAR Y.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the annual meeting of the War- rington branch of the above society was held in the Masonic Hall, Bold-street. There was a poor attendence. The chair was occupied by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Mr. John Fairclough, J.P., not feeling sufficiently well to take the presiding duties as announced, but he occupied a place on the platform in company with the coairman and the R-ws. Canon Burbidge, J. Monk Jones, E. T. Reed,and Messrs. S.P. &amp;cock and W.Owen,J.P. Letters of apology for non-atteudancl were read from the Rev. Jolla Yonge and the Rev. J. Davies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FINANCII.</head>
        <p>FINANCII.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. OwEN,the treasurPr, read the statement ot ac- counts. which showod that the year was commenced with a balanco in hand of £90191. 2d. Tnn receipts bad been £2ll Us. 7d. and the expenditure £llB 15s. id., leaving a balance of £92 16a. 6d. The accounts were certified to be correct by Mr. J. L. Tunstall.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRbIAN proposed the passing of the treasurer's report, and remarked that it was a source of satisfaction that the treasurer was able to tell them that he had over £9O in hand. They were no doubt indebted for that to the ladies, and others who had helped in getting the money together.</p>
        <p>, Dr. T. S. SMITH seconded the passing of the report, which was at once ;.opted.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. E. T. Raw proposed that the following officers be elected for the ensuing year :—President, Mr. J.C. Parr ; vice-prasident, Mr. John Croslield ; treasurer, Mr. William Owen ; secretaries, Rev. E. C. E. Carleton, Rev. John yonge, and Mr. S. P. Silnock ; committer, Messrs. J. R. Buckton, J. Fairclough, R. Garnett, E. J. Guest. John Guest, John Hall, R. A. Naylor, together with the local clergy and ministers and the following ladies :—Mrs. Z. Armitage, Mrs. Balmford, Miss Nellie Bennrtt,Miss Dutton, Mrs. John Fairclough, Miss Garner, Mrs. R. Garnett, Mrs. Gornall,Miss Harding, Mrs. Reed, and Miss Smith. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. 01111 N seconded the proposition, and the above ladies and gentlemen were forthwith elected. Canon BURBIDOR, in the course of a lengthy address. said he came there for the simple object of saying a few words about the Society which circulated the Bible throughout the whole wide world. On one of his visits to the Society's warehouse in Queen Victoria-street, London, he was astonished to learn that from that ;one building there were sent out four Bibles, whole or in part, every minute night and day. 5,000 were issued each day there and another 8,000 from the various branches up and down the world. They must also remember that the Society sent out the Bible alone and without comment. It was a most prolific Society though it had a very small bf - ginning, and now it was so large that the Duke ot Con- naught had said. "You must not class the Bible Society with any other hut call it a National Institution." up and down the world there were 10.000 other little socie- ties which looked upon theirs as the parent Society. The Society had also been very prosperous. In its earls' years it had a hard struggle to get any support and the first years' income was mil., £5,000. Now the receints of the Society were £200,000 a year, and the antnal'in- come of the Society, roughly speaking was £lOO,OOO annually. The Bible Society was also a great cosmopolitan Society ; no country could claim it. It was also a philanthropic society as it helped everyone. no matter who or what he might be, in God's work. The society had 'Tent £40,000 in supplying the Sunday schools of England and Wales alone with bibles. For another thing it wag the grandest missionary society of all,and the missionaries themselves owed an immense debt of gratitude to it, as also did the S.P.G. Society. The latter organisation required the bible in about 35 different languageo, und mcst of them were obtained from the British And Foreign Bible Society. Six-sevenths of all the bibles used by the Church of England were obtained from Ulu soeisty which circulated annually four milliona of et-mita. of God's work. The Rev. J. Mora( .loNgs, district secretary, also gave an address in which he showed copies of the Bible in various native languagea. He also rated the manner in which the different languages were put in written form by the miasionaries, preparatory to their being printed. When the Society started about 91 years ago the Bible existed in about 35 langnages,while now it was circulated in whole or part in 326 living lan- guages, while the addition of the obsolete tongues brought the total te over 400. The speaker also stated that the circulation af the serinferres in Japan rose from 35,000 in 1E94 to 115.000 in 1895. A fact which only cams to his knowledge a few weeks ago, was that a movement was set on foot a short while sine, by the Christian doctors of Emeland to raise a fund from which to send out copies of the Bible to every certificsted doc- tor in Japan. The money was raised and copies sup- plied to the 1..000 licensed doctors in that country. The Cnaigma.N after thanking Canon Btu-bilge and the Rev. J. Monk Jones for their excellent addresses, pronounced the b!nedictinn and the meeting dispersed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>SACRED CONCERTS.</head>
        <p>SACRED CONCERTS.</p>
        <p>On Sunday two sacred concerts were given, in the afternoon and evening, in the Parr Hall by the Besses o'th Barn Brass Band in aid of the new in- strument fund of our local Borough Brass Band. Both concerts were well attended and a fair surplus may be expected to have been realised for the object named. As on previous occasions the programmes consisted of sacred and secular selections from the works of standard composers and, similarly, the concerts gave opportunity to the solo euphonium, trombone, and cornet, to distinguish themselves, which they did to the extent, of wtnning several en- thusiastic encores. The items in which the whole of the band was chiefly engaged were somewhat noisy and lacking in interest as in Berlioz " Faust," and Meyerheet's "La Prophete." On the other hand the two overtures "Crown Diamonds" (Auber) and "The Magic Flute" (Mozart) were characterised by inuch delicate light and shade and both these pieces proved thoroughly enjoyable to the audience. 'the " Austrian Hymn " (Haydn) played at both concerts was, as to the variations, anything but worthy of the subject and the com- poser. Instead of the florid and often .ineffective parts intended to adorn the theme after its first announcement, we would much prefer to have heard some reasonable adaptation of Haydn's grace- ful embellishments as they are scored for the Strings in the original quartette.. Btr, on the whole the concerts were attentively listened to, highly appreciated, and filth, sustained the high reputa- tion attained by tae Besse s tith Barn Band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn team have had a stroke of ill-luck lately. Disappointed at Saturday's result when they were beaten by threes points, the disappointment was made a trifle more galling on Wednesday when Hull beat them by a single point. Thus not only have Runcorn been defeated twice on their own ground this season, but they have sustained a couple of defeats in less than a week. Al- though this has not affected their position in the com- bination table it has interfered very seriously with the ;Average which Runeornians have been so proud to point to. There is reason to doubt whether they have now not bidden good-bye to the combination medals. 'I hese two recent reverses are not so much accountable to any deterioration in the playing of the team as to the weather and state of ground which has prevented the players from indulging in that open display for which they are noted,and which has enabled them to reach their present high position in the competition. In order to meet the al,ered conditions several changes ia the _team are to be made.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Runcorn Rugby Football Commit- tee oil Wednesday evening,it was decided that the Run- corn team should not journey to Heywood on Saturday meet the Rochdale Hornets team. The ground of the Hornets at R4ch4ale is suspended by the Northern Union Committee (Lancashire section in consequence of the spectators having illtreated a referee. The officials of the club have obtained permissios from the Union to play on other grounds, and they have asked Runcorn to play at Heywood. The Runcorn Comtnittee,however,in face of the fact that the Rochdale team have consented to play Oldham at Oldham, and Manningliam at Man- ningham, consider that they have been treated unfairly in being asked to journey to fleywood,and have informed the Rochdale officials of their unwillingness to,accede to their request. If the Rochdale team decis not to j )urney to Runcorn,the match will have to be-postponed until the ground has been removed from the suspension list, when Runcorn will proceed to Rochdale and fulfil their engagement.</p>
        <p>We have been asked to contradict the statement which has gained cnrrency that at the meeting of the Northern Union h -Id at Huddersfield last week, Runcorn were fined for kicking off late at one of their matches. This is qnite erroneous. The club in fault was Broughton Rangers,-and they were fined 35i. for being the cause of a late ,'tart when visitiag Runcorn last month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>NEW ZEALAND GOLD SHARES.</head>
        <p>NEW ZEALAND GOLD SHARES.</p>
        <p>There are signs of an intending mining boom in New Zealand where the facilities for economical gold mining are exceptionally good. Some of the biggest financiers including Messrs. Rothschild are now largely interested in New Zealand gold mines. AnAher 100 per cent. divi- dend was declared last week by one of the Companies worldng gold mines in that colony. Of the low-priced shares, those of the Achilles Goldfields. Limited, are well worth the attention of investors with spare cash at hand. The properties owned by the Achilles Company are entirely situated in New Zealand, and include the cele- brated Pticenix Gold Mine, said to be the best mine in I that co:ony. Cablegrams of a highly satisfactory char- acter have been received from the Managing Director, who is now at the mines in New Zealand. The 30 Stamp Mill already on the property is kept fully employed treating ore, averaging a gold yield of over one ounce to the ton. The first 720 tons of quartz milled produced 725 ounces of grild,the next 200 tons, 214 ounces of gold, and the next 400 tons, 436 ounces of gold, and a cable- gram is expected shortly,eiving the mid-March crushing which it 5 anticipated, will be even more gratifying. Sitisfactory as this state of things is, as sinking pro- gresses, the property is becoming far richer and a cable- gram dated March 7th, 1896,has been received from New Zealand, stating " latest important discovery incontest- ably proves 5 feet gold reef worth 2ozs. per tan." News has also been received from the Managing Director that the 200 feet level is turninc, out ore which gives 10azs. of gold to the ton and that the 300 feet level is producing ore which gives 20 ,zs. of gold t'e the ton. Investors and speculators are therefore buying Achilles shares fully paid up at the present low price of 3s. 3d. per share, at which they are selling on the London Stock Exchange, as now that good dividends are assured there is every probability of their very shortly improving to many times their present price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>EARLESTOWN IND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN IND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNKRNNIESS,</p>
        <p>At .the Earlestown Plice Station on Saturday morning before Mr. J. W. Emmett, Martin McAllis- t.r was charged with being drunk in Railway-st., Earlestovrn, the provioug evening, and was fined 2. 6.i. and costs. In default he was sent to goal [or seven days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>CAUGHT AT LAST.</head>
        <p>CAUGHT AT LAST.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning at the Earlestown Police Station before Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Martin Doyle, a labourer, was brought up on a warrant charged with being drunk end disorderly in Market-street, Earlestown, on the Ist September, 1894. He was apprehended on Saturday by P.C. Burns. P.C.Green proved the charge, and the prisoner was fined 10s. and costs. In default he was sent to gaol for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>VULCAN WRSLPIYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>VULCAN WRSLPIYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening in the Vulcan schoolroom, Mr. J. B. Hartley of Manchester, assisted by the school choir, gave a service of song entitled I. True as steel.' The entertainment was of a pleasing character, the various episodes being capitally illu- strated by litre light views. On Sunday evening Mr. Hartley conducted a lantern service, the sub- ject being" Bible pictures," and other lessons. A collection was made in aid of the school. EAULESTOWN MAY QTYEEN FESTIVAL. The draw for the honourable position ot " May Queen "in connection with the above festival, took place on Wednesday evening in the Mission Hall. Thirty-three girls over the age of 12 years particip- ated in the draw, the position being secured by Edith Plant, daughter of Mr. William Plant, of Ashton-road. Newton-te-Willows. This year each girl had to be provided with a note from her par- ents, stating that they were agreeable to her taking part in the draw. THE LADY FOOTBALLERS AT EARLESTOWN. On Thursday afternoon Mrs. Graham's team of lady footballers engaged a team of gentlemen not together by Mr. T. Siddley, on the ground of the Harlestown football club. 'there was a very large crowd of spectators present, drawn together no doubt by the novelty of the game rather from any exnectation of seeing a grand display of foot- ball. The game was of the kind which usually ac- companies the play of the ladies, aril the gentle- men viewed it rather from its comic side than from any serious intention of playing football. Doubtless the ladies took the matter seriously enough, and they were allowed to score 4 goals whilst their opponents scored 6. GOOD TEMPLARS. The usual weekly session of the Hope of Rifles- town lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co- op.-retive Hall on Monday evening. Bro. G. Clarke presided, and there was a large attendance of mem- hers,and a number of visitors from the Warrington. St. Helens and Ashton lodges, including Bro. A. Frances, D.S.J.T., St. Helens, and Q.NI. Sergeant Roberts, P.C.C.T., Warrington. One candidate was I admitted to membership. A report on the recent visit to Liverpool was given by Bro. G. Clarke. After the business of the lodge was cornoleted the members had a social evening,for which Bro. Barker was responsible. a very pleasant time being spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The time of retirement of a district. councillor for each of the wards in the Newton Urban District draws on apace, but so far we hare not heard of contests in any of the wards with the exception of the Viaduct and Crow-lane. The retiring council- lors are :—Viadact Ward, Mr. MacMahon ; Town Hall, Mr. E. T. Evans ; Crow-lane, Mr. J. Smith ; Wargrace, Mr. W. Thompson ; Newton, Mr. J. Simp- son. It is rumoured that in the Viaduct Ward, Mr. NlacMahon will be opposed by Mr. J. Clarke, iron- monger, who for many years has been a member of the Board of Guardians, and that in the Crow-lane Ward, Mr. James Smith will he opposed by Mr. McDermott. The election has been fixed for March 30th. and the nominations for Monday next, the la.q day for objections being Friday the 20.. h inst., at four o'clock. Mr. R Brierley is the returning officer.</p>
        <p>FI RE AT THE 1-7RIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL</p>
        <p>LATCIIFORD.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon it was discovered that the roof of the above building was on fire, shortly after the commencement of the Sunday school work. The superintendents (Messrs. J. Massey and E. Metres) quietly closed the school in the usual manner, and tne children were dismiseed without being informed of the actual state of affairs. Several of the elder male scholars and teachers remained, however, and one of the young ru-n pluckily ascended to the roof, where water was handed to him by willing hands, and the fire was quickly put out. The stove pipe had become disarranged, and ignited the rafters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>EA ItLEST 0 WN.</head>
        <p>EA ItLEST 0 WN.</p>
        <p>A three days' bazaar and sale of work in connection with the Primitive Methodist Chapel, was opened in the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Thursday. The object of the bazaar, as set forth in the guide book, is to aid the trust debt extinction fund. Up to February, 1895, there remained a debt of £675 upon the premises. As it was felt that this burden was crippling the energies of the church, a sale of work was then held, which realised £145 aacl enabled the officials to reduce the debt to £530. Of this amount two friends generously offered to sub- scribe £l5O, on condition that the congregation raised the remaining £3BO. The members of the church and congregation were working hard to meet this condition, but could only hope to attain success by the kindly help of a sympathetic Cnristian public. Already that sy in- pathy has been shown by a readiness to contribute in service and in kind, and it is anticipated that when the bazaar closed they will have been netted the greater por- tion of the £3BO required. The opening ceremony on Saturday was presided over by the Ray. E. R. Davies, and he was accompanied on the platform by the Rev. J. Bartley, Rev. T. R. Mat- thews. Mr. C. W. Emmett, Mr. T. Ormand, Mr. T. Clarke, Mr. E. J. George and others. After the singing of a hymn, the reading of a passage of scripture by the Rev. E. R. Matthews and prayer by the Rev. J. Bartley, —The Chairman thought they ought to add to the beati- tudes they already knew, " Blessed is the man who maketh a short speech at a bazaar, for he shall have praise." The object of the bazaar was already stated upon the program tne,but he would call their attention to it. It was an object which was well worthy of their notice, and ;Ilso of their very hest possible support. It was only a little over 12 months ago since they had their last sale of work, which was the first since he came amongst them, and they raised £145, leaving them with debt of £530.0n that occasion a gentleman who kindly opened the sale for them brought them the informa- tion that Mr. Hartley and a kind friend would give a fourth of the remaining debt after that sale of work was held, providing the remaining debt did not exceed £6OO. They were better than their word, they were not only giving them a feurth of £530, but a fourth of the £6OO, leaving for them £l5O, and for the congregation £3BO. At their last sale of work they said:they were going in for liquidating the debt. They did not use that word that day, but another term " extinction," the death of the debt. They did not care to have debts upon their church and school premises. He congratulated the friends connected with the church for the excellent manner in which they had worked in connection with the bazaar. He knew they were worthy of every encouragement. He had now to introduce to them Mr. Emmett—not the Emmett whose name was on the bills, but the worthy son of a worthy father, and when they could not get the father the next best thing was the son. Mr. Emmett had been called away to London or he would have been very pleased to be present. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>• Mr. C. W. Emmett: said his first duty that afternoon was to apologise for the unvoidable absence of his father. When his father consented to open the bazaar he had a doubt in his mind as to whether he could be present,and that doubt had been realised Be al- ways thought thatlin getting up a bazaar it was usually possible to attain a two fold object. In the first place the principle and primary object was to raise an amount of money,and that was done more easily by a bazaar than any other way, for it was rarely in a district such as that that they found men of wealth. Consequently it rested with the whole of the members of the church to put their shoulders to the wheel,and in that way they generally accomplished their object. Earlestown and its people were noted for their generosity,and he was quite sure that after the experience of a few months ago,when the VVesleyans held their bazaar.they would find, as Mr. Davies said.they had got the articles, the people and he was quite sure the money somewhere. (Applause.) Be then formally declared the bazaar open,handing in a donation of £5. Mr.E.J.George moved a vote of thanks to Mr.Ernmett and Mr. T. Clarke seconded, the vote being heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>The sale was then briskly proceeded with, there being a very large company of intendinz purchasers present'. The room WRS prettily laid out in early English style,the nine stalls being heavily laden with all manner of articles of a useful and decorative character. The ladies' sewing meeting stalls, RS the name implies, were filled prin- cipally with articles which were the handiwork of the sewing meeting, hut in addition there were numerous fancy goods of endless variety. No I. stall was presided over by Mrs. W. Austin, jun. and Mrs. Ormand ; No. 2 by Miss Dlckens,Mrs. Sargenson and Mrs. Woodsmith a number of assistants. The Young People's Christian Endeavour Stall was in charge of Miss A. Dickens, Miss Newton, Miss Pendry, Mr. J. T. Austin, Mr. E. J. W. Mullins and Mr. C. T. Rawling. The Gentlemen's Stall was presided over by Messrs. J. Austin, E. Dickens, Sargenson, A.Smith, R. Sproston. and P.Tomlinson. The principals of the rlfreshment stall were Mrs. Banton and Mrs. Griffiths, The Children's Stall was in charge of Mra. Arrowsmith, Mrs. W. Austin. sen., Mrs. Garratt and Mrs. Pendry, with a host of children as assistants. The flower stall was very choice.ithe principals at this being Miss Nellie Dickens and Miss Ormand. The Basket and Grocery Stall was of an essentially useful character, and was presided over by Miss Clarke and Miss Shimmin. The Grocery Stall was in charge of Masters P. and F. Ormand. In addition to the stalls there was a bran pie and fish pond. kinetoacope and phonograph,and galvanic battery. During the afternoon selections were given by Mr. Peplow on the violin, and Mr. A. Walker on the piano, and in the evening there was a half-hour concert by the Wesleyan Church choir. There was also an exhibition of living waxworks by Professor Clairioni. The takings for Thursday amounted to £B9 14s. 111</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLT ANENT. (Continued from page 6.)</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLT ANENT. (Continued from page 6.)</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Mr. Kisox , moved an' instruction" to the Select Committee on the Londonderry Improvement Bill to inquire into the present mode of election of aldermen and councillors in the city, and whether it was expedient to modify or alter the la-w as to such mode of election. Mr. FARRRLL seconded the motion. After some debate the motion was withdrawn in favour of an amended instruction suggested by Mr. Courtney. and which was passed, to the effect that the Committee should e into the extension of the municipal franchise to all Parliamentary voters, and that they should have plwer to make provision for the s a me. VjAcAtInt.CRANIIORNR moved the second reading of the Benefices Bill, which seeks to remedy abuses connected reference to the protection of the interests with the s.ale of advowsons and the exchange of livings, The second reading was carried by 259 votes arishinners. Colonel SAWDYS moved the rejection nowffittthhheespb;ell:Rl to 81. It cr:!s Proposed. that the bill should be referred to etnhtet was Standing m 0,1 Law, but as an amend- oCvoedminthlee:ittit should be sent to a Select m i Committee. The amendment was defeated by 283 votes to 62, and the bill was accordingly referred to the Stand- ing Committee on Law. THURSDAY. In the House of Common! on Thursday the Liverpool New Cattle Market and Railway Bill was opposed by LD Mr. FIR and Mr. T. P. 0 CO:SNOR, but after a pro- longed discussion Mr. FIRLD withdrew his proposal to recommit the bill, and it was read c!, third time. On the motion of the second reading of Petersham and Ham Lands and Fnetpathlßill, Mr. PADLTON n oved a e 8 the effect that the House was not prepared rtoeniu entertain in to rotaina hill for the enclosure of metropolitan COM* mon lands. After discussinn the motion for the second reading was defeated by 262 yotest? 118,and the amend- ment was agreed to without a division. sea The House afterwards agreed to suspend the twelve o'clock rule.and then went into committee on the Army Supplementary E timakes. Mr. BRODRICK, in explain- ing how the amounts included in the first vote were to be disposed of,said it was proposed to give the troops who took part in the Aahanti expedition a month's pay as a reward for their services. An item of £120,090 was for the expenses of the expedition, but some portions of that would be repaid. It was also proposed to redress a long-atanding grievance of the volunteers in connection with the payment of the capitation grant, and to try to do something towards filling the 1.800 vacan- cies in the ranks of the volanteer officers. The allowance to the officers who went to camp was to be increased, and some disadvan- tages under which they at present laboured were to be removed. The despatch of the expedition to Coomassie was strongly condemned by Mr. W. Redmond and Me. Dillon, and late .in the evening was defended by Mr. Chamberlain.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords on Thursday the second reading; was agreed to of a 13,11 (the "Whipping Bill") which con- fers upon magistrates the power of ordering offenders of" tender " years to be whipped instead of being sent to prison. The House also passed the second reading of a bill to make burglary an offence triable at quarter sess ons. At present all burglaries,however insignificant they may be,must be tried at the assizes ; housebreaking and shopbreaking have long been within the jurisdiction of the " inferior" court.</p>
        <p>DEATH AND FUNERAL OF A FORMER RUNCORN LADY. The funeral of Mrs. Mason, wife of Mr. George Mason. of Wilderspool Causeway, Warrington, and formerly of Runcorn, took place on Wednesday when the remains were interred in Runcorn Cemetery. Until quite recently Mr. and Mrs. Mason were in charge of the New Inn, High-street, Runcorn, and retired in consequence of ill- health. Mrs. Mason had been in failing health for some months, and died on Sunday. She was very well-known in RLncorn and neighbourhood. SEWING MACHINES Hand £2 183.6 d. Treadle £44s. with handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. Durrox 16 and 13. Sankey-st.. Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>PAItISH COUNCIL MEETINGS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>(Continued from page 6.)</head>
        <p>(Continued from page 6.)</p>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>A meeting for the election of a Parish Council was held in the girls' schoolroom, Halton, on Monday evening. There was a good attendance. At the outset the chair was occupied by the Rev. G. D. Wray. but subsequently by Mr. Lea. The nominations were as follows :—Joseph Astbury, Halton, farmer ; John Ackerley, Aatmoor, far- mer; Samue/ Barrow, Halton, cooper; Charles Birch, Norton Arms, licensed victualler ; William McCullock, Halton-road, works manager ; Robert Hughes. Halton- road, foreman ; John Samuel Jeaceck, Halton Common. schoolmaster ; 'lames Looker, Main-street, Halton, far- mer ; Joseph Manning, Halton,charcoal works manager Alfred Povey, near Elalton Castle, tanners' labourer;; Joseph Pritchard, Brook-place,watchmaker and jeweller; Joseph Clifton Stokes, Runcorn, farmer ; George Daniel Wray, Halton Hill, vicar of Halton.— The Rev. G. D. Wray begged to withdraw his name from the list of those nominated. There were 13 can- didates nominated, and if he withdrew it would avoid a contest. It was an excellent selection of candidates, and he thought he tould not do better than withdraw. In answer to a question as to the number of the atten- dances made by the members of the Council, the Clerk stated that there had been 13 meetings of the Council since its formation and the atteneances were as follows: —Rev. G. D. Wray (chairman). 10 and 5 committee meetings ; McCulloch, 10 and 2 ; Stokes, 12 ; Looker 12 and 5; Astbury, 11 and 2; Barrow. 12 ; Birch, 9; Rey- nolds, 6 and I ; Aekerley, 12 and 4 ; Jeacock, 13 and 5 ; Randle, 2 ; Hughes, 13 and 2.—The Chairman said that as the Vicar had withdrawn, there were only 12 candidates for the 12 vacant seats on the Counc;l. He declared them duly elected. This concluded the buei- ness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>FRODSIIABS.</head>
        <p>FRODSIIABS.</p>
        <p>--- The annual meeting of the ratepayers of the township of Frodsham, was held in the Court Room on Monday evening, Mr. H. E. Heywood presiding. Mr. Heywood took the chair. Nineteen nom- ination papers were leceived, 14 of which were allowed as correct. These included Miss Louisa Glifv, and Messrs. Edmund Ashworth, Joseph Corker, T. Earlam, James Edwards, W. Belsby, W. Eienshaw, James Midge, P. Riley, George Rodger, W. E. Shore,Ralph Tickle,John J. Wilkinson, and William Youd. Mr. Tickle withdrew from the list, and the Chairman called for additional nominations, when the following names were given : Messrs. William Booth, farmer ; John Barrow, John Jones, and A. E. Rimmer. On the nominations being singly put to the meeting the votes recorded for each were as follows :—Miss Grice 6 ; Messrs. Ashworth, 31 ; Barrow, 43 ; Booth. 40 ; Corker, 36 : Earlam, 49 ; Ed- wards, 40 ; Flelshv, 18; Flenshaw, 20 ; Midge. 43; Jones, 17 ; Riley, 26 ; Rodger, 42 ; Rimmer, 10'; Shore, 44; ; Wilkinson, 45 ; Youd, 38. These, with the exception of Miss Grice, Messrs. Jones and Rimmer, were declared duly elected.</p>
        <p>FRODSFIAM LORDSHIP. For the Frodsham Lordship the following zentlemen have been elected members of the Parish Council : Joseph Berrington, Overton ; Thomas Birchall, carter, Five Crosses ; J. Davies, Five Crosses ; J. Dobson, Brad- ley Orchard Farm, Frodsham ;John Hartley Hargreaves, Overton ; Thomas Hulse, Netherton ; Thomas Lewis, Bradley; George Okell, Newton-by-Frodsham ; James Pereival, Frodsham ; Charles Reynolds, Overton; Robert Shepherd, Micklefield, Frodsham ; and Henry Tiley, Overton.</p>
        <p>WESTON.—CANON WOOD OUTVOTED. In the church schoolroom, Weston, on Monday even- ing, a meeting of the parishioners of the Weston ward was held. There was a good attendance. Mr. J. J. Speakman was elected chairman on the motion of Mr. &amp;aron, seconded Mr. Sill. Nominations were handed in on behalf of the following gentlemen :—John Ball, 11, Victoria-road, Runcorn ; Daniel Basnett, grocer,Weston ; Wilbraham Dunbartnn. farmer. Weston ; James Ford, sen., farmer. Weston; Thomas Ford sen.,fArmer,Weston ; Thomas Griffiths,farmer, Weston ; Arthur George Guest, quarry proprietor, the Grange, Weston ; Thomas Tower Sill, manager, Chemical works, Weston Hall, Weston ; David Somerville, solicitors' clerk. Weston ; John Ellis Sweetman, quarryman. Weston ; James Tudor,assista.nt manager chemical works, Heath-road. Weston ; Alfred Maitlamd Wood, clerk in holy orders, the Vicarage, Run- corn, Thus,it will be seen ,that twelve candidates were nominated for eight seats. The candidates having re- tired from the room, a vote was taken by a show of hands. The result was as follows :—Tudor 39. Ball 35, James Ford 34, Basnett 30, Dunbabin 28, Sill 26, Somer- ville 22, Griffiths 20, Guest 15, Thomas Ford 10, Sweet- man 8, Wood I.—Shortly afterwards Mr. John Smith demanded a poll. A vote of thanks concluded the busi- ness.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>WESTON POINT.</head>
        <p>WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>The parish meeting in Weaver ward,Weston, was held on Monday evening. Mr. Robert Cookson occupied the chair,but there was only a moderate attendance. Seven nomination payers were handed in as follows :—Rev. D. S. Bowen, the Vicarage. Weston Point; Peter Burrows, foreman salt works, Weston Point ; Richard Robert Clark, ship broker, Weston Point; Frederick William Clark, cashier, Ashley House, Runcorn ; John Griffith, farmer, Weston-lane; Thomas John Grounds, hotel manager, Dock Hotel, Weston Point: Louic Rosenberg, agent for the Trustees of the River Weaver Navigation, Weaver Villa, Weston Point. No elector exercised his right to put questions, and no explanations were, there- fore made by the candidates. As seven gentlemen had been nominated for the five vacant seatf., a show of hands was taken with the following result :—R. R. Clark, 16 ; linv, D. S. Bowen, 15 ; Louis Rosenberg, 13 ; F. W. Clark, 11 : T. J. Grounds. 10; John Griffiths. 10; P. Burrows, 1. No poll was demanded, and the Chair- man having given his casting vote in favour of Mr. Grounds, he declared the first five mentioned duly elected. 'A ;vote of thanks was accorded the Chair- man.</p>
        <p>HELSRY. A meeting of the parishioners of Helsby was held on Monday evening under the presidency of Dir. Jeffs. The Chairman having stated the object of the meeting,asked for nominations to he handed in.with the result that the following, gentlemen were nominated for the eleven vacant seats :—W. Barlow. James Brandreth, Thomas Brandreth, Dr. Bryant, J. T. Collier, W. Davies, Thomas Ella.mes, William Guest, Samuel Moss, Thomas Noden, Joseph Potts, George Crossland Taylor, Ja:nes Taylor Abraham White, James White, and F. Wright. Mr. White withdrew his name, and Mr. Jeffs declined to be- come a candidate. A show of hands was taken, and was as follows :—James Taylor. 78 ; G. C. Taylor. 62 ; F. Wright, 59 ; Dr. Bryant, 54 ; W. Barlow. 52 i Josenh Potts, 52 ; Thomas Ellameq.49 ; Abraham White, 44 ;W. Davies. 35 ; J. T. Collier. 32 ; William Guest, 29 ; James Brandreth, 10 ; Thomas Brandreth, 7 ; Thomas Noden, ; and Samuel Moss, 3. The Chairman declared the eleven first named gentlemen elected. A poll was de- manded by Mr. Abraham White. The meeting then broke un without the usual vote of thanks to the Chair- man being accorded.</p>
        <p>CLIFTON. A parish meeting for the purpose of electing a parish council of Clifton was held in the Primitive Methodist Schoolroom, Clifton, on Monday evening. The Chair- man was Mr. Rowland. There was a small attendance. Five nomination papers were handed in for the five vacant seats. The pereons nominated were :—William Fletcher, labourer. Samuel Rowland, grocer Joseph Stokes. farmer ; and Jnhn Williams, grocer, of Clifton ; and John Wilson, architect and surveyor, Runcorn. The Chairman duly declared them elected, and the proceed- ings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-14_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>DEA.TH AND FUNERAL OF MRS. DR.</head>
        <p>DEA.TH AND FUNERAL OF MRS. DR.</p>
        <p>WATKINS. NEWTON.</p>
        <p>On Thursday morning of last week, Mrs. Harriet Bromehead Watkins, wife of Dr. Watkins, of Newton, died rather suddenly at the residence of her husband in Hieh-street, Newton, from apoplexy. The deceased la.iy had suffered almost all her life from neuralgia, and two years ago symptoms of other mischief arose, and Dr. Steele. of Manchester. was called in. When she was taken ill on Thursday Dr. Hayward. of Haydock, was sent for and Dr. Steele also was wired for, but the latter did not arrive until after the unfortunate lady had died. None of the family, who were from home, were able to arrive at Newton before the death took place. Oa Sunday night th 3 coffin was taken to St. Peter's Church. where prayers were read by the Rev Allan P. Watkins, 8.A., who with his brother Mr. C. Watkins, remained in the church throngliout the night. On Sunday morning a special service and holy communion, conducted by the Rev. ff. Monk, was attended by the whole of the family. The funeral took place on Monday, the body being in- terre,i at the Cemetery. The chief mourners were Dr. Watkins, the Misses 0. I. and B. M. Watkins, Mrs, Overton, Dr. A. Watkins, Rev. A. P. Watkins, Dr. H. Watkins, Mr. C. Watkins, Dr. B. Watkins. Sister Acmes Mary Watkins, and Mrs. Koszelski. Miss Watkins (Toncester),Dr.Overton.Mrs.A.P.Watkins.Mr.G.and Miss Airey, and the'Misses Pardey. The bearers were Messrs. Liptrott, Crouchley, Frost, Appleton, Ritchie, assisted by Anderton of the Cemetery and Shillets. Amongst those present were —Dr. and Mrs. Hayward, Dr.Handy- side. Dr. Street, Mr. T. J.Gillespie, Mr. C. B. F. Borron, Mr. R. Stone, Mr. J. E. Pardey, Mr, Milligan, Mr. B. B. Glover, Mr. J. W. Glover, Mr. R. Barton. Colonel Wood, Revs. J. Carson, H. Sdidall, F. W. Johnstone, and J. Allat't Messrs. R. Brierley, J. Singleton, R. Barton, Miss Pennington, and others. There were a very large number of beautiful wreaths sent by sympathising friends The special music rendered during the service was, " Rest in the Lord," and " Blessed are the departed," and the hymns sung were, "God moves in a mysterious way," and "Now the labourer's task is o'er." The funeral service throughou was impressively conducted by the Rev. H. Monk.</p>
